window.BROVILLE_INDEX=[{"id":"abandoned-manor","title":"Abandoned Manor","category":"Locations","lead":"An abandoned property in western Broville’s swamp lowlands, also called SOLAR Manor. The identity of its former resident is unknown.","body":"The manor rises above the flooded ground and waterways of western Broville’s [[swamplands|swamp lowlands]]. It is introduced as SOLAR Manor and with the phrase “Qua Patet Orbis Et Argentum”. The property is derelict and had been abandoned years before the account. Its name and clues found inside the house gave rise to the rumor that it belonged to an eccentric scientist or even the founder of [[solar|SOLAR Corporation]]. The description does not identify who lived there or confirm either possibility."},{"id":"abby","title":"Abby","category":"Characters","lead":"A camper who decided to investigate the reports in Braulding and wrote a second account after what she found.","body":"Abby was a camper who decided to investigate the reports in [[braulding-county|Braulding]]. After what she found, she wrote a second account. Her records connect with the investigation of the [[the-braulding-disappearances|Braulding disappearances]]."},{"id":"agraal","title":"Agraal","category":"Characters","lead":"A gold-armored creature who possesses the Eye before Heraldus’s expedition. The playable encounter presents a manifestation named Agraal’s Vengeance.","body":"[[king-heraldus|Heraldus’s]] expedition describes a very tall being with elongated limbs, golden armor and a black sword. Smoke escapes through the armor’s joints when it moves. The diary initially describes its appearance; the name Agraal comes from the voice of the [[eye-of-arburaz|Eye of Arburaz]], which identifies him as its former captor. The quest tradition associates Agraal with the destruction of the Brotherhood of the Vagabonds and the confrontation with Heraldus, in which the knight Wilhelm was severely wounded and Agraal’s body was defeated. The explanation that his spirit survives to protect the Eye belongs to the community interpretation of the subsequent encounter, distinguishing the diary character from Agraal’s Vengeance, the boss fought by the player deep within the swamps. The account and exploration are brought together in [[heralduss-expedition|Heraldus’s quest]]. The encounter uses a scripted mechanism with two creatures stacked together, acting as a single unit, and waves of reinforcements. The black sword belongs to the narrative description; the playable entity uses the enchanted iron sword listed below. | Feature | Agraal’s Vengeance | Agraal’s Rancor | | --- | --- | --- | | Role | Mount | Passenger | | Type | Wither skeleton | Blaze | | Health | 44 points, or 22 hearts | 64 points, or 32 hearts | | Equipment | Full gold armor with Protection I to III, Thorns III and Unbreaking I | Permanent Invisibility | | Weapon or attack | Iron sword with Sharpness IV, Knockback I and Fire Aspect I | 14 attack points | | Special feature | Detection range of 200 blocks | Simulates the fire attacks and spiritual abilities of the figure below | - Two ghasts are summoned when the boss activates. - Waves of skeletons wearing iron and leather armor act as guardians. - A lightning strike, technically identified as LightningBolt, and portal and lava particles mark the appearance. The command block summoning the boss is at −1603, 9, 1393. Combat begins with a message from the “Booming Voice”: “What audacity! Again you come to steal what is rightfully mine. I will teach you a lesson you won’t soon forget!”"},{"id":"alghus-vinto","title":"Alghus Vinto","category":"Characters","lead":"A researcher who records the analysis of samples containing an eye-like object and purple organisms in July 1998.","body":"In July 1998, Alghus Vinto records the analysis of samples that include an eye-like object and purple organisms. His research is presented in the context of the [[canopy-laboratories|Canopy laboratory]]. In Research, dated July 28, 1998, Vinto describes several days of work on an unusual material. The team has removed what resembles an eye from one segment, which appears inactive. He distinguishes it from two samples showing clear signs of life and describes a small purple worm-like organism in the material. His entry ends with a plan to begin animal tests and an ordinary office detail: Bill from Flora has brought flowers for Vinto and Susan. The juxtaposition makes the experiment part of a staffed workplace, not an anonymous report of a completed disaster."},{"id":"alkali-clan-ruins","title":"Alkali Clan Ruins","category":"Locations","lead":"Remains of an ancient jungle clan studied by explorer Digby Braveman. Near the coast, a stairway leads to a cave containing human remains and possible hidden passages.","body":"Explorer [[digby-braveman|Digby Braveman]] describes the remains of the Alkali clan in Explorer’s Log, his exploration diary. After weeks searching the dense jungle, he realizes that the largest and best-preserved ruin he has found stands near the coast. His account describes the other surviving jungle ruins as few and far between. The entries for July 14 and 15, with no year given, describe a stairway connected to a cave containing human remains, mostly skulls. Digby identifies small marks on the bones as the result of traditional Alkali body modifications. According to his examination, the skulls were forcibly separated from their bodies. These observations lead him to ask whether the stairway once provided access to a prison. The diary leaves this interpretation as the explorer’s hypothesis. He fears that the stone-brick interior might collapse and postpones deeper excavation until he can stabilize it."},{"id":"alpine-village","title":"Alpine Village","category":"Regions","lead":"A lakeside settlement in the snowy southern mountains. Broville’s tourist guide presents it as a ski resort, with chocolate and downhill slopes among its attractions.","body":"Alpine Village stands in a recess in the [[southern-mountains|southern mountains]], beside a lake. The railway map shows a dedicated branch to the settlement, which provides a landmark between the high ground and the valleys. Tickets called Alpine Rail Express Ticket identify a one-way journey to Alpine Village Rail Station. Signs in the world also name the village’s station and railway. Warp Recommendations, a guide published by tourist publisher BroTours Publishings, describes Alpine Village as a ski resort far from conflict and, jokingly, from basic comfort as well. It recommends trying the chocolate, sliding down the slopes and attempting to stay warm. For visitors starting on [[fractal-island|Fractal Island]], the guide locates the travel access to the village along the winding road that climbs the mountain south of the starting point. These directions concern the departure point on the island."},{"id":"ancient-peoples-of-broville","title":"Ancient Peoples of Broville","category":"World","lead":"The desert, jungle, Brolapagos and mountains preserve traces of distinct communities. This overview brings together what is known about them.","body":"Broville contains traces of distinct communities, without a shared chronology establishing their origins, periods or relationships. The [[ancient-egyptian-civilization|Egyptian civilization]], in the west of the map, is known for its monumental complex and burial objects. Its architecture recalls ancient Egypt, although the available historical records are limited. The reference point for a visit is −3140, 70, 2300. The diary of [[digby-braveman|the explorer Digby]] describes stairways and skulls belonging to the [[alkali-clan-ruins|Alkali clan]] in the jungle. That investigation has its own sources and does not identify the clan as part of the Egyptian civilization. The [[ruined-castle|ruined castle]], northwest of the city, is another ancient complex, placed by community documentation at −1419, 80, 912. Its appearance is compared to English castles, an architectural interpretation rather than evidence of English historical origins. [[heralduss-expedition|Heraldus's expedition]] begins nearby, and its search continues beneath the swamps. [[bembe-coto|Bembe Coto's]] testament attributes the destruction of a [[brolapagos|Brolapagos]] island community to the religious mission. In the [[southern-mountains|mountains]], the tradition of the [[dwarven-society|Dwarven Society]] is linked to the [[arkenstone|Arkenstone]]. The dwarven settlement is described as lying inside a volcano among snowy hills. Its tunnels and structures extend for hundreds of blocks. The entrance identified as safest is at −360, 88, 3167. The visiting description preserves more information about the site's physical extent than about its history."},{"id":"arkenstone","title":"Arkenstone","category":"Collections","lead":"A jewel named on the map and associated with the Dwarven Society in community tradition.","body":"The Arkenstone is described as a jewel with sharp points that shines with its own inner light. According to community tradition, the [[dwarven-society|Dwarven Society]] discovered it long ago in the heart of the [[southern-mountains|Broville Mountains]]. Considered priceless, the stone is said to have vanished along with the dwarves. The search text advises visitors to be patient, listen to mountain echoes and the howls of demons rising from the depths, and follow the tracks of men who desecrated the ancient lands. These are evocative exploration clues rather than a detailed sequence of events."},{"id":"arthur-bent","title":"Arthur Bent","category":"Characters","lead":"An inventor who uses the Expandinator to enlarge his apartment. His diary describes the experiment encroaching on his neighbor’s space.","body":"Arthur Bent uses a device called the Expandinator to enlarge his apartment. The inventor's diary follows the experiment and describes the expansion encroaching on his neighbor's space."},{"id":"arts-district","title":"Arts District","category":"Regions","lead":"A small waterfront district east of Downtown and north of Chinatown. Its name also appears in Black Line documentation.","body":"Arts District occupies a narrow stretch of coast east of [[downtown|Downtown]] and north of [[chinatown|Chinatown]]. Documentation for [[boardwalk-station|Boardwalk Station]] associates the station with the arts area, the exhibition and museums. The railway survey also mentions an old [[black-line|Black Line]] stop named after the neighborhood, Arts District Station."},{"id":"asylum","title":"Asylum","category":"Locations","lead":"An abandoned property northwest of Concord Commons. Dormitories, a water tower, a crematorium, and the main building surround access points to underground levels.","body":"The Road Map marks The Asylum in grid square E8, between Cattail Town and the [[solar-western-complex|SOLAR complex]]. [[concord-commons|Concord Commons]], to the southeast, provides an urban reference point on the subway network. The team's first diary from 2013 records the approach at −1320, 68, 1470. The dormitories, water tower, and crematorium are separate landmarks from the main building. The 1929 book records patient discharges, while the 1934 book lists admissions. G. Freeman appears in both. The admissions include M. McFly, E. Brown, and B. Tannen, a reference to Back to the Future. The records date the institution's activity, while the description of renovations in the 1930s comes from later testimony. In [[final-notes|Final Notes]], dated August 10, 1998, [[brian-garlick|Brian Garlick]] states that explorers developed severe symptoms after portal tests and that some were sent to the asylum. He explicitly says he does not know what happened to them. The [[broville-paranormal-society|BVPS]] returned to the property with permission to film. Its records distinguish the dormitory area, the floors identified by color, and the facilities beneath the building. The distribution of the volumes is described in the [[bvps-diaries|BVPS Diaries]]. The narrative route is covered in the [[the-asylum-expedition|investigation mission]]."},{"id":"b-virus","title":"B-Virus","category":"Concepts","lead":"An agent applied to Canopy’s experimental samples. Records describe matter that heats up, moves, grows, and survives division.","body":"The notebook Inorganic Sentience describes matter that heats up, begins moving, or changes shape. One sample survives freezing, division, and thawing. Inorganic Sentience records B-Virus being administered to two different materials in July 1998. The magma-cream variant produces rapid heat and movement; a second mixture, made from moss and pig brain tissue, takes a cubic shape and hops. The second material grows overnight and remains active after freezing, division and thawing. The notebook describes these observed results and the researchers’ containment measures; it does not identify the virus’s origin."},{"id":"banhammar-tower","title":"BanHammar Tower","category":"Locations","lead":"A commercial tower in Downtown.","body":"BanHammar Tower is a commercial tower in [[downtown|Downtown Broville]]. In early 2012, construction engineer Mentalgen broke ground and completed the outer shell within a few weeks. The project originally reserved all office space for a single company. Complications surrounding the move-in date led the company to default on its construction loan, and the property was sold to [[foxcreek-properties|FoxCreek Properties]]. Mentalgen Construction had already completed the lobby and access stairway before the investors' default. FoxCreek completed the remaining interiors and converted the tower into a commercial building for multiple organizations, leasing each floor to a different occupant. The large two-story offices at the top were retained for its new commercial leasing division. The inventory records these occupants: - BroTech Web Services Support Division. - BroForce Staffing LTD. - J. A. Sellout Consulting. - FoxCreek Properties Commercial Leasing Division. A year after completion, the formerly stark gray facade received quartz highlights. A few months later, windows on the eastern face were blocked in to accommodate the third remodeling of [[feels-good-man-pharmaceuticals|FGM]] headquarters."},{"id":"beethovens-lost-symphonies","title":"Beethoven's Lost Symphonies","category":"Collections","lead":"Numbered sheets form a collection and a treasure hunt across the world of Broville, spanning the city and other dimensions.","body":"Beethoven’s lost symphonies are collectible sheets scattered throughout Broville. Their item names number a collection of 64 pieces. The search accompanies exploration of buildings, underground spaces and other corners of the world: each sheet adds a number to the set. The v11.4.4 inventory located 62 distinct numbers. Pages 3 and 7 still have no confirmed location in the material examined. This describes the scope of the search without claiming that they are impossible to find in the world. In the book The Beatles, the author says that Broville’s classical music critics rank Beethoven, despite considering him highly controversial, above other musicians popular in European courts. A sign complains in exasperation: “If you play Für Elise one more goddamn time...”. These details place the collection within the city’s humor and cultural life."},{"id":"bembe-coto","title":"Bembe Coto","category":"Characters","lead":"A figure remembered as the monastery’s founder. His testament describes what the mission did to the former inhabitants.","body":"Bembe describes the religious mission’s violence against the inhabitants of [[brolapagos|Brolapagos]]. His text mentions a key and a clue connected with water. The book is in the [[brolapagos-monastery|monastery’s]] burial area and leads into the [[the-monasterys-secret|investigation of the mission]]. Two inscriptions in the [[brolapagos-monastery|monastery]] identify Bembe as its founding father and mark his ashes. My Final presents itself as his last will and testament, rather than an account written by a later visitor. Bembe describes the destruction of the island’s earlier culture as his one regret. His confession and the request he leaves behind are central to [[the-monasterys-secret|The Monastery’s Secret]]."},{"id":"billy","title":"Billy","category":"Characters","lead":"A diary describes someone from outside the familiar world trying to approach campers. The text tells a self-contained story.","body":"The diary associated with Billy describes someone from outside the familiar world trying to approach campers. The text presents this experience as a self-contained story centered on that attempted contact."},{"id":"black-line","title":"Black Line","category":"Transport","lead":"An old underground railway network identifiable through Mid Side signs and entrances in the canals area. Some stations were abandoned, while others received new uses.","body":"The Black Line is presented as the first mass transit system of the city in [[broville-v11|v11]]. Its remains include tunnels, stations and signs, with very few powered rails. Signs for [[mid-side-station|Mid Side Station]] are at −328, 66, 1802 and −340, 58, 1805. Another sign at −276, 47, 2139 marks an entrance in the [[canals-district|Canals]] area. A passage sometimes associated with Canopy station is identified on site as an emergency railway depot. Community history places construction between 1919 and 1923 and attributes most funding to the now-defunct [[canopy|Canopy]]. Its announced public purpose was to serve the population at large, but exorbitant fares restricted use to the wealthy; the private motives for construction are described as poorly known. Abandonment is associated with riots across Broville, preceded by the disappearance of numerous members of the elite who were reportedly never heard from again. The chronological relationship between these events and the [[the-sinking-of-broville|sinking]] remains unresolved. The claim that the line's records and diagrams were lost concerns the historical documentation of the old network. The community inventory lists seven stations, three of which remained in use under other networks: | Station | Described status | | --- | --- | | Arts District Station | Abandoned. | | Canopy Corp Station | Abandoned. | | Library Station | Abandoned. | | Mid Side Station | Abandoned. | | Boardwalk Station | Reused; the v11 Metro Map shows it on the Purple Line. | | Brompton Station | Survived the riots and was reportedly adapted for the Blue Line, established in the 1970s. | | City Port Station | Reused by the regional line, described in this account as the Rural Line between Space Station and Portvale. | The v11 Metro Map confirms [[boardwalk-station|Boardwalk]] on the Purple Line and [[brompton|Brompton]] on the Blue Line. [[city-port|City Port]] appears on the [[regional-railway|regional railway]] diagram. The seven-station inventory belongs to the historical survey of the Black Line and does not replace maps of the later networks. An image of an abandoned station is accompanied by a caption presenting a routine police inspection and using the term “euthanasia” of homeless people. The wording reflects the dark humor of the city's description; it does not establish an additional documented episode in the line's history."},{"id":"black-mother","title":"Black Mother","category":"Characters","lead":"A three-headed figure in the marsh traditions. The origin and details of her cult belong to the community account.","body":"Black Mother is an ancestral mythological entity in the legends of the [[swamplands|marshes]] northwest of Broville. The community account places her in the realm of the dead and associates her appearance with occult rites. Isolated marsh communities, particularly near the [[ruined-castle|castle ruins]], are said to have reported rare appearances. She is described as a powerful three-headed creature, fiercely protective of her eggs and aggressive toward anyone approaching, throwing objects at intruders. The account gives no hatching time for the eggs; these traits belong to the marsh tradition rather than a confirmed reproductive cycle. The community description specifies two components: - Four blocks of soul sand, described as rare but present in marshy areas. - Three “Black Skulls,” whose exact origin remains mysterious. The rite involves shaping the entity's physical form with the soul sand, then arranging the skulls to complete the offering and trigger her appearance. The account does not provide a further outcome for the invocation."},{"id":"bn-tower","title":"BN Tower","category":"Locations","lead":"A tower beside Boardwalk beach, described as Broville’s tallest building and a visible landmark for arrivals by sea or air.","body":"BN Tower stands beside [[boardwalk-station|Boardwalk]] beach near the urban waterfront. Its entrance is at the corner of 6th St. North and Vertigo Blvd. The tower is described as the city's tallest building, rising above cloud level and serving as a skyline reference both within Broville and on approaches by sea and air. Engineer Metalus of Metalus Industries built the tower against Mayor Oldshoes' wish that no building rise higher than [[brocorp|BroCorp's]] pinnacle. The local history jokes about the building surviving the mayor's wrath and includes a macabre remark about people of all races and genders jumping from it to their deaths. On the [[broville-subway|subway]] diagram, BN Tower is an interchange between the Purple and Red Lines near the Boardwalk area."},{"id":"boardwalk-station","title":"Boardwalk Station","category":"Transport","lead":"A waterfront station. Community documentation lists it among the stops reused from the former Black Line.","body":"Boardwalk Station lies east of [[downtown|Downtown]], on the urban waterfront. Community documentation lists it among the stops reused from the former [[black-line|Black Line]]. On the purple [[broville-subway|metro]] diagram, it sits between [[bn-tower|BN Tower]] and [[broville-international-airport|Airport]]. The station once stood inside one of the ravines beneath Broville, until the ravine was declared a nuisance and filled in. Its interior still retains a cave-like atmosphere. The entrance is one of the network's largest, with a spacious, almost cathedral-like pavilion. The combination of this monumental entrance and the subterranean setting distinguishes the station. The station is associated with the [[arts-district|Arts District]], Broville City Expo, Museum of Fine Arts, Broville Museum of Science and the waterfront boardwalk."},{"id":"bralibu","title":"Bralibu","category":"Regions","lead":"A coastal strip north of Oldtown, identified on the district map. Its outline follows the headland facing the sea.","body":"Bralibu occupies the peninsula north of [[oldtown|Oldtown]]. The channels and waterlogged terrain of the [[swamplands|swamps]] begin to the west. [[keister-island|Keister Island]] lies offshore."},{"id":"braulding-campsites","title":"Braulding Campsites","category":"Locations","lead":"Ranger Rich’s station and the campers’ diaries introduce the investigation into disappearances in Braulding County.","body":"[[ranger-rich|Ranger Rich]]'s station is the starting point for a visit to the park in [[braulding-county|Braulding County]], at 450, 66, 2092. The ranger's reports are at 454, 65, 2090. [[abby|Abby]]'s Camping Diary is at 503, 71, 2112. The [[regional-railway|regional railway]] helps locate the county before continuing to the station. The Friday report records the arrival of eight campers on Thursday at around 7 p.m. and the staff's departure at 10:30 p.m. The other report begins from testimony originating at one of the campsites to the west. Abby's Saturday entry shifts the perspective from the ranger to a camper who decides to investigate on her own. These accounts introduce the search; its route and discoveries are covered in the [[the-braulding-disappearances|Braulding Disappearances quest]]."},{"id":"braulding-county","title":"Braulding County","category":"Regions","lead":"A region east of City Port, beside the airport. Its campsites hold accounts by Ranger Rich and Abby.","body":"The district map places Braulding east of [[city-port|City Port]], beside the [[broville-international-airport|airport]]. The regional station lies on the City Port, Braulding, Portvale and Fort Bulwark route. The package reference point, 327, 69, 2038, guides arrivals in the region. The ranger station at 450, 66, 2092 is the landmark for the [[braulding-campsites|campsites]]. [[ranger-rich|Ranger Rich’s]] two reports are kept together at the station. One was retained because the county asked him to preserve his witness account. The other records a visit by a camper from a sector to the west. [[abby|Abby’s]] diary allows comparison between the official investigation and the experience of someone in the park. What the search uncovers is covered in [[the-braulding-disappearances|The Braulding Disappearances]]."},{"id":"breno","title":"Breno","category":"Regions","lead":"A district south of the center, between the canals and Brompton, associated with hotels, casinos and nightlife. It is served by a local monorail and has a station on the regional railway diagram.","body":"The [[breno-monorail|Breno monorail]] connects the district with [[canals-east|Canals East]], where passengers can transfer to the blue and red [[broville-subway|metro]] lines. Breno’s [[regional-railway|regional railway]] station appears on another diagram. The community account associates the monorail with leisure and nightlife, serving hotels, casinos and nightlife establishments. Warp Recommendations, a tourist guide from publisher BroTours Publishings, recommends Breno to visitors seeking city nightlife. Its invitation to spend money on slot machines presents gambling as part of this leisure offering, in a humorous tone. Neighborhood signs identify the hotel Stwanky Hotel and the casino Oriental Casino. These names provide specific landmarks for the combination of accommodation and gambling described in the sources. For visitors starting on [[fractal-island|Fractal Island]], the guide places the travel access to Breno in a bar south of the starting point. These directions identify where to depart on the island."},{"id":"breno-monorail","title":"Breno Monorail","category":"Transport","lead":"A local Yellow Line linking Breno’s hotels, casinos and nightlife venues to the Canal East interchange with the Red and Blue Lines.","body":"The monorail serves [[breno|Breno]], south of Broville. Its station, at −316, 78, 2239, is integrated into an urban building. It provides a connection for casinos, hotel complexes and the district's nightlife; Breno also appears along the [[regional-railway|regional railway]] route. The yellow route connects the entertainment area to the wider network through Canal East, also spelled Canals East in map references. The [[broville-subway|metro]] Red and Blue Lines intersect there, allowing onward travel to other neighborhoods. The route ends above two venues presented as important Broville nightlife destinations: That place those who are something are talking about and Exclisior Night Club. Because it serves this group of attractions, the line is described as recording its highest ridership peaks during the first part of the night, between 9 p.m. and 2 a.m."},{"id":"brian-garlick","title":"Brian Garlick","category":"Characters","lead":"A researcher who recorded the portal disaster and reinterpreted ancient inscriptions as a warning.","body":"Brian Garlick signs [[final-notes|Final Notes]] as a research specialist. He places the [[brian-garlicks-expedition|desert expedition]] at a time when [[solar|SOLAR]], according to his testimony, needed a breakthrough to survive. The team believed that artifacts from a civilization more than a thousand years old recorded technology comparable to their own. Their initial reading treated the inscriptions as instructions for a portal with an obsidian frame. Garlick connects the crossings to severe changes among the explorers. His account mentions night terrors and waking visions of dead relatives. Explorers who, he says, were not immediately executed were sent to the [[asylum|Broville asylum]]. The researcher states that he does not know what happened to them after the transfer. On August 10, 1998, Garlick writes that he escaped after a shock wave, structural collapses, and the deaths of colleagues. He reinterprets the inscriptions as a warning and believes that the hurried team ignored their authors' intention. He blames himself for what happened and leaves a request to destroy the facility. His fate after the final record remains unknown."},{"id":"brian-garlicks-expedition","title":"Brian Garlick’s Expedition","category":"Events","lead":"A SOLAR team interprets desert remains as instructions for a portal. In 1998, Brian Garlick records the consequences and revises his reading of the inscriptions.","body":"[[brian-garlick|Brian Garlick]] says the [[solar|SOLAR]] team found inscriptions and artifacts in [[western-desert|desert]] ruins. They interpreted the material as instructions for building an obsidian portal. The testimony does not identify the excavation with the specific [[ancient-egyptian-civilization|Egyptian]] complex. Explorers were sent through the portal, and creatures were brought back. Some team members suffered nightmares and changes. Garlick mentions some explorers being sent to the [[asylum|asylum]]. In [[final-notes|Final Notes]], dated August 10, 1998, he describes a shock wave, collapsing sectors, and dead colleagues. He concludes that the inscriptions were a warning and asks that the facility be destroyed before the creatures reach the surface."},{"id":"brobrah","title":"BroBrah","category":"Organizations","lead":"Don Bestick's company. His autobiography connects a collider and a purchased discovery to SOLAR's financial crisis.","body":"In A BroBrah Was Born, [[don-bestick|Don Bestick]] says he conceived a men's bra with a massage function while his firm was in debt. In the founder's promotional account, bank loans funded recruitment and the construction of the headquarters in four weeks. The company began manufacturing equipment and improvements for its own facilities, reducing its need to hire [[solar|SOLAR]]. Bestick says he purchased the Giant Hardon Collider and the discovery of the Glans Particle when SOLAR ran into difficulties. He attributes commercial expansion from Melonport to Redlight City to the vibrating core developed with this technology. The Collider Administration Manual identifies BroBrah as the owner and describes its operation. A BroBrah Certified Master Keycard unlocks the controls. The operator launches two Boost Shuttles and follows their progress through the lights on a scale model. The manual instructs the operator to retrieve the particle from the shaft after the collision, which it describes as highly radioactive, and to call the cleanup team. In a message to supervisor Gaffer Honcho, Bestick states that the boxes marked as the final order complete the assemblies in Section A of Facility No. 3. He orders the section's workers dismissed and access blocked. Undercover Log offers another perspective on the company. [[paul-raten|Paul Raten]], working undercover as Paulie Ratzingo, receives an initial assignment to steal a BroBrah prototype. The diary leaves the prototype's function and its connection to the collider unresolved."},{"id":"brocorp","title":"BroCORP","category":"Organizations","lead":"A major corporation present in Broville’s commerce and cityscape. According to the SOLAR reports, its tower was built over the ruins of the old city.","body":"In SOLAR Report [#3], the researcher known as the [[the-doctor|Doctor]] writes that the inauguration of BroCORP Tower, built over the ruins of the old city, was scheduled for the following day. He regarded BroCORP as a competitor while [[solar|SOLAR]] expanded its laboratory to manufacture technologies derived from the [[latency-reactor|Latency reactor]]. Warp Recommendations, a guide from tourism publisher BroTours Publishings, presents the tower as the headquarters of a large company involved in trade between the city’s businesses. Community accounts also associate BroCORP with [[gamebros-arcade|GameBros]] and [[pizzabros|PizzaBros]]. The slogan “Bros Building Buildings, Bro” accompanies its corporate image. Its humorous introduction claims that the company was founded more than 9,000 years ago and guided every version of Broville; this hyperbole supplies no usable date for the history narrated in the reports. The LightSource Information leaflet includes BroCORP among the businesses served by [[lightsource|LightSource]], an advertising and communications company. Its offering includes targeted advertising, telemarketing, bulk messages and employee notifications. The advertisement names BroCORP as a customer but does not specify which services it purchased. A Diary signed by Brocorp Agent, an agent of BroCORP, reports that his superiors believed a group of hermits possessed something valuable. The writer did not share their enthusiasm. His brief account neither identifies the object of interest nor records the company obtaining it. Secret Agenda presents the 2014 plans of a secret society called the Fraternal Order of the Block. Signed by Grandmaster Architect, the agenda includes paying BroCORP management to forget an excavated tomb. This is an intention recorded by the group, with no confirmation that the payment took place. In The Shocking Tru, Phil Dudley, who introduces himself as a doctor of cryptozoology, describes BroCORP as a holding company: a business that holds stakes in other businesses. He credits it with dozens of subsidiaries in the major economic sectors and places its rise in the mid-twentieth century. This description belongs to a conspiratorial pamphlet; the extent of corporate control and the chronology alleged by its author are not confirmed by the other documents cited here. On the Metro Map, [[brocorp-metro-station|Brocorp Station]] connects the green and red lines. Its reference in the package list is −249, 46, 1806. The guide to [[fractal-island|Fractal Island]], the starting island, also identifies a teleport point to BroCORP in the tallest office building north of the island’s starting point."},{"id":"brocorp-metro-station","title":"Brocorp Metro Station","category":"Transport","lead":"A Downtown station near the BroCORP tower, beneath the Bank of Broville. It connects the Green and Red metro lines and has three street entrances.","body":"Brocorp station lies at the center of [[broville|Broville]], in [[downtown|Downtown]], near the [[brocorp|BroCORP]] tower. It is an interchange between the Green and Red Lines of the [[broville-subway|metro]], providing access to routes toward Museum, Chinatown, Canal East and BN Tower. - **Bro Street:** to the right of the Bank of Broville. - **Brah Way:** to the left of Brobucks. - **Lombard Street:** in front of [[broville-city-hall|Broville City Hall]]. Although the entrances appear on different sides of the bank and its surroundings, the station itself lies directly beneath the Bank of Broville. The proximity of the vault leads to very frequent inspections inside and around the station. The stated purpose is to prevent attempts to break into the vault from the underground infrastructure."},{"id":"broific-rim","title":"Broific Rim","category":"Organizations","lead":"The community name for a program of giant machines confronting marine threats. The map records the name Brother Spectrum Mk. 2.","body":"Broific Rim is the community name for the program dedicated to designing, building, and deploying giant robots against colossal monsters emerging from an [[latency-breaches|underwater breach]]. The map records the name Brother Spectrum Mk. 2; budget figures and the broader military history belong to the program’s community narrative. In that account, Broific Rim is presented as [[broville|Broville]]’s most expensive security program, consuming roughly 4% to 11% of GDP during the initial crisis and forming a pillar of local defense alongside [[fort-bulwark|Fort Bulwark]]. The program’s role is to confront exceptionally large threats. The [[bvs-fleet|BVS fleet]] handles conventional threats, while Broific Rim’s machines are intended to fight giant creatures and protect the coastline and infrastructure. The program’s origins are associated with the appearance of a [[latency|Latency]] breach on the ocean floor, beneath a glacier south of Broville. Emerging monsters are said to have prompted the authorities to fund an urgent, large-scale response. After years of effort and considerable losses, the breach reportedly became dormant, sharply reducing the frequency and intensity of appearances. It was reportedly never completely closed: residual activity explains why the program continues, although at a reduced level. Engineers and scientists developed a first generation of giant robots. Brother Spectrum MK1 is described as that generation’s most powerful and imposing machine, with its performance still partially classified. Wreckage scattered across the territory is interpreted as evidence of devastating battles. An MK1 foot in the jungle is a relic of this period. By comparison, Brother Spectrum MK2 is estimated to be two to three times smaller. The destruction of MK1 models is cited as one reason for the transition to MK2. As the breach stabilized, corporate funders gradually reduced their contributions, considering such massive investment less necessary. The program is described as having only one remaining operational MK2, kept at a secret base near Fort Bulwark; operational here does not mean active in the scene shown. Fragments of early units are considered rare and scattered across Broville and its surroundings. Two major remains are identified: - An MK1 foot in the jungle. - An MK2 head at the edge of the breach. The scarcity of other wreckage is attributed to material recovery programs run by Metalus. | Period | Reported situation | Estimated budget | | --- | --- | --- | | Initial crisis, with the breach active | Maximum threat and first-generation MK units | About 4% to 11% of GDP | | Breach stabilization | Transition to MK2, partly because of MK1 destruction | Decreasing | | Apparent dormancy | Only one remaining operational MK2 | Heavily reduced |"},{"id":"brolapagos","title":"Brolapagos","category":"Regions","lead":"An island southwest of the urban core, with a port settlement, a resort and an old monastery connected to its colonization history.","body":"Road Map places Brolapagos southwest of the urban center, in Broville’s western ocean. The package’s reference list marks the island at −2155, 70, 2496. Its port settlement and the Lizuli Azure resort and spa form its tourist-facing side. The resort is presented as one of the city’s premier exotic destinations. The old [[brolapagos-monastery|monastery]] stands on the other side of the island and is described as one of the first buildings erected by settlers after their arrival. This landscape of port, accommodation and religious mission conceals a more violent history, recounted in its founder’s testament."},{"id":"brolapagos-monastery","title":"Brolapagos Monastery","category":"Locations","lead":"An old religious mission on Brolapagos Island associated with its founder, Bembe Coto. Its funerary area preserves a testament and a key that begin an investigation into the site’s past.","body":"The monastery stands on [[brolapagos|Brolapagos]], an island with a port settlement and resort in Broville’s western ocean. The community description places it on the other side of the island from the tourist area and presents it as one of the first buildings erected by settlers after their arrival. One plaque commemorates [[bembe-coto|Bembe Coto]] as the founder, while another identifies his ashes. His testament, titled My Final, lies near copies of the Old Spanish Key. The book is recorded at −2157, 72, 2372; keys are found one block below, at −2157, 71, 2372. The testimony and the use of the key are developed in [[the-monasterys-secret|The Monastery’s Secret]]."},{"id":"brompton","title":"Brompton","category":"Regions","lead":"A neighborhood east of the canals, near the port. Its history connects expansion with port trade and urban decline with the emergence of the Browery.","body":"Brompton lies outside the [[downtown|Downtown]] core, east of [[canals-district|Canals District]], near [[breno|Breno]] and [[city-port|City Port]]. On the Metro Map, it is the blue line terminus after Stadium. It is a medium-sized neighborhood with inexpensive stores, narrow apartments and many abandoned houses. Its architecture loosely resembles early twentieth-century American buildings. The community history attributes Brompton’s rapid growth to its proximity to the port. A major riot is said to have affected the district, followed by rising crime, abandonment and homelessness. The neighborhood was eventually segregated and walled off, becoming an impoverished version of its former self. Changes in building style and unusual breaks in the street pattern support the hypothesis that Brompton once included areas now occupied by Breno and [[cwc-stadium|CWC Stadium]], but insufficient documentation survives to confirm those former boundaries. One of the most prominent structures is Broville Great Society Public Housing. Several older buildings were demolished to make room for this large development of inexpensive homes, accompanied by a swimming pool. The first project is described as successful, but funding ran out during construction of the second, leaving a deep excavation. Over time, tall, cramped residential buildings were erected around a narrow passage at the bottom of the excavation. The area became known as the [[browery|Browery]]. The neighborhood’s decline turned it into a dark, run-down area with buildings in poor condition. The [[black-line|Black Line]] description records the conversion of Brompton’s old station for the blue line. Local commerce also appears in Test Subject Observation Form, found at −1179, 54, 1785: the document states that [[solar|SOLAR]] marketing approved the sale of the tested compound through its own ¡El Medico Rapido! stores in Brompton."},{"id":"brospace-centre","title":"BroSpace Centre","category":"Regions","lead":"A BroCORP-affiliated space center on the southwest coast, officially called Bro S Space Center for launch and research and served by Space Center station.","body":"BroSpace Centre, also called The Bro S, occupies a headland on the southwest coast, in the region of the same name identified on the road and district maps. Its access corridor passes through the valley. The main entrance is at −1560, 68, 2705, and the center is served by Broville's regional commuter train, whose diagram names the terminal stop Space Center. The facility is affiliated with [[brocorp|BroCORP]] and uses the official name Bro S Space Center for launch and research. Its presentation promises “The Pinnacle of Mediocre Space Travel”. A sign welcomes visitors at the entrance, and they must obtain a temporary badge before proceeding. The complex contains areas for launch operations, research, preparation and visitors: - Mission Control. - Vehicle Assembly Wing. - Operations & Research Core. - Training Wing. - Aerospace Engineering department. - Laboratory & Testing. - Tourist Center. - Shipment Receiving Garage. It also employs accounting, security and janitorial services. Chaz Yolo is Mission Director, and Kene Granz is Flight Director. The center's communications declare crew safety the “Lowest Priority”. One comparison claims its public budget exceeds competitor Spess-X's, but classifies the results as INCONCLUSIVE. These messages contribute to the satire of a space institution with questionable performance. Internal rules prohibit siphoning rocket fuel, removing parts to sell as scrap and selling materials as drugs. Helmets are mandatory beyond a designated area, and a warning states that intruders are liable to be shot. - **EARTHS DIAPER:** a mission studying the range of flatulence in space. - **COSMIC PUKE:** a mission comparing vomiting in zero gravity. - **CRSH 69316:** the reference for the launch simulation supercomputer present at the facility. - **Particle Cannon:** a project whose approval is marked PENDING. The names and notices document activities and proposals at the facility, without establishing a launch outcome or approval for the pending projects."},{"id":"brostreet-station","title":"BroStreet Station","category":"Transport","lead":"A subway station in Downtown on Bro Street, beside the Bro Street Diner and the BroCORP Tower. It was one of the first stations in v11.","body":"The station takes its name from Bro Street. It occupies a small setback from the street beside the Bro Street Diner and near the [[brocorp|BroCORP]] Tower. During the initial excavation, witnesses described it as being in the middle of nowhere, although new construction was already appearing nearby. Urban expansion placed it in the heart of [[downtown|Downtown]]. Construction began in early 2012 under MolybDenim, assisted by DarkFox118. After completion, a remodel made the station considerably smaller. The change prompted debate among the engineers because its location beside BroCorp was expected to generate heavy daily traffic. After the discussion, that configuration was retained pending further analysis by the Broville Department of Transportation. Despite the small space relative to demand, its simple circulation allowed many passengers to enter and leave. The account of its public opening in spring 2012 reports only one light being out and no other problems since inauguration."},{"id":"broto-lake","title":"Broto Lake","category":"Regions","lead":"A residential district arranged around a lake, west of the canals and north of the industrial area.","body":"The lake stands out amid the residential blocks. On the subway, Broto Lake lies between Broverly Hills and Campus on the blue line."},{"id":"broverly-hills","title":"Broverly Hills","category":"Regions","lead":"A residential area south of Concord Commons, between Rural Valley and Broto Lake.","body":"The neighborhood borders [[concord-commons|Concord Commons]] and the western countryside beside [[rural-valley|Rural Valley]]. On the Blue [[broville-subway|subway]] Line diagram, it appears at the southwestern end, with [[broto-lake|Broto Lake]] as the next stop."},{"id":"broviet-union","title":"Broviet Union","category":"Organizations","lead":"The communist state that succeeded the imperial regime in CRN065’s account. Its legacy is associated with monumental architecture and the memory of an age of collective greatness.","body":"The Broviet Union is the name given to the regime that succeeded [[czar-broseph-iii|Czar Broseph III]] and his imperial army. Tradition describes the revolution as an attempt to transform Broville’s social and urban organization radically. [[diary-1925|Diary 1925]] records the struggle for the valley in 1924 and 1925, changing authors when the position changes hands. In a separate account, [[crn065|CRN065]] characterizes the war as an internal revolt whose anti-imperialist side sought outside assistance. His letter places the communist state’s prosperity after the Czar’s death and up until the technological era. The succession of regimes can be identified, but the fragmentary records do not establish the Union’s founding and dissolution dates. The Union is remembered as a lasting influence on Broville’s architectural style, characterized by imposing structures, rigorous symmetry and raw materials combined with expensive ornamentation. Emerald blocks on some administrative buildings are cited as examples of this legacy. The Great Broviet Union Monnument is the most specific attribution: the large sculpture of a hand grasping a pickaxe is identified as a monument to the Union’s glory. As well as honoring the work of the city’s builders, it would therefore preserve a political reference to the regime. The community account connects the weakening of central authority with the growth of corporations and private enterprises. By taking control of resources and urban planning, these organizations are said to have encouraged the transition to the corporate era. [[solar|SOLAR]] illustrates the importance of companies in later Broville; CRN065’s letter places the change broadly in the technological era without specifying its duration or each company’s individual contribution. Despite the regime’s disappearance, the community description records a current of support for the “Old Regime” among residents and nostalgia for an age of collective greatness. Together with the monuments, this memory keeps the Union present in the city’s identity."},{"id":"broville","title":"Broville","category":"World","lead":"A Minecraft city, continent and exploration world started by Oldshoes in 2009. Towers, stations, laboratories and ruins preserve stories spanning published maps and fictional eras.","body":"Broville is a series of Minecraft city maps started by Oldshoes in 2009 and developed with a team of builders, including contributors from the United States and Canada. Work across multiple versions combines an extensive city, explorable interiors and secrets scattered above and below ground. [[broville-v10|v10]], released in 2011, focuses exploration on the city and its underground passages. [[broville-v11|v11]], developed in a new world between 2012 and 2016, expands exploration to a continent of snowy mountains, plains, swamps, islands and deserts. Oldshoes associates its landscape and climate with the west coast of North America and cites Victoria as a reference in his 2016 interview. The terrain in v11 received extensive landscaping, with towering mountains, large open areas and deep caves that give the continent features uncommon in normally generated game worlds. The landscape supports finding hidden places, building, adapting the scenery and establishing a base for Survival play. Architecture ranges from skyscrapers and commercial areas to residential suburbs. [[brompton|Brompton]], Canal Town and [[breno|Breno]] are among the names used to introduce the diversity of districts, whose buildings and cultural references change from one area to another. The city also supports free exploration, searches for chests and secret locations, and using the setting for experiments in building or destruction. [[downtown|Downtown]] contains skyscrapers and [[brocorp|BroCORP]]. The district plan places [[canals-district|Canals District]] south of Uptown and west of Brompton. Farther west, the city gives way to the [[solar-western-complex|SOLAR]] forest and the desert. The [[southern-mountains|snowy mountains]] occupy part of the south, while [[brolapagos|Brolapagos]] lies on an island southwest of the center. - **Between neighborhoods.** On the [[broville-subway|metro]], Museum connects the Purple and Green Lines. At Canals East, the Red Line crosses the Blue Line, which serves Brompton. - **Beyond the center.** The [[regional-railway|regional railway]] passes through City Port and Braulding and continues to Portvale and Fort Bulwark. The Atlas road map helps navigate between districts, forest and desert. - **Inside buildings.** The [[museum-of-natural-history|Museum of Natural History]] is a starting point for [[project-chr0nos|Chr0NOS]]. At the [[asylum|asylum]], the first [[broville-paranormal-society|BVPS]] journal is inventoried at X −1320, Y 68, Z 1470. Mission articles identify entrances and place solutions behind a spoiler heading. These routes overlap with searches for buildings, regions, landmarks, items and documents. A visit can remain within one neighborhood or follow clues through tunnels and remote parts of the continent. Underground signs preserve traces of the [[black-line|Black Line]]. The [[ancient-egyptian-civilization|Egyptian complex]] in the far west is identifiable on maps through its buildings and in world records through its items. In the center, underground notices and the [[the-doctor|Doctor]]'s reports offer different perspectives on the [[the-sinking-of-broville|sinking]]. The [[history|History of Broville]] brings these strands together and arranges the dates that can be established from the documents. Maps included with the download guide exploration; signs, items and books support names, coordinates and episodes. Community descriptions also record local conventions, such as Downtown's classification and the history attributed to the Black Line. A book presents a character's account, while a plan shows spatial relationships for the edition it accompanies. For the relationship between the city released in 2011 and the old city narrated in v11, see [[continuity|From v10 to v11]]. During development, team members published updates on Tumblr pages. Names associated with these pages include Oldshoes, Moneybob, Thoth17, Khoraker, Intruder and Darkfox118. Oldshoes.ca was also identified as a place to access the project. The collected map credits include the following contributors: - Addelburgh - Bacu - Baron_Samedi - bickerteeth - Bobson_ - bobwoco - castlemike - ChipUK - combatmedic - Czakima - Descole_ - Ditherliss - Dizigma - DMAN1030 - DocTrev - Dresden260 - Drewpeesax - Envaris123 - FalconRed - Fish_XCV - Foomonchoo - GamingHost - Gargladdy - Goboguy - Golonka - HapsyColour - Herobrine - IndianaWillB - Intruder - jrodsqod - KajTaotsu - kawaii342 - khoraker - Konegido - LukasMaps - mastersteviekun - megatrontehcube - Mentalgen - metalus - Mofman - mokrithorr - Molybdenim - moneybob - Nadaz - nrub - Oldshoes - OwenLudwig - peacebr8ker - pheonix74 - PratTz - QuantumEcho - resir014 - sburc009 - South_Paw - Tarkis - Terre1620 - thebkbroyler - Thoth17 - ultra_dank - Vayeate_OZ - vdetommaso - WorldShaper_ - yumstheman - Z31"},{"id":"broville-bluff-station","title":"Broville Bluff Station","category":"Transport","lead":"A name used in transport references for a modernized station in Broville Heights, within Old Town. The v11.1 history records signs being corrected from Broville Bluffs to Old Town Station.","body":"The community description places Broville Bluff Station in the suburb of [[broville-heights|Broville Heights]], within [[oldtown|Old Town District]], and identifies it as a [[broville-subway|metro]] Purple Line station. In that description, it results from the transformation of the former [[broville-heights-station|Broville Heights station]]. The v11.1 history, however, records signs being corrected from Broville Bluffs to Old Town Station. The names Bluff and Bluffs need to be read with this signage change in mind, without assuming an additional independent station. The structure is described as entirely modernized and decorated in its line's colors. Comparison with earlier images still allows the outlines of the old building to be recognized, despite changes to the station's finish and presentation."},{"id":"broville-bureau-of-investigation","title":"Broville Bureau of Investigation","category":"Organizations","lead":"The Broville Bureau of Investigation, or BBI, investigates local crime from its Downtown headquarters. Undercover investigator Paul Raten records one of its undercover operations.","body":"The BBI headquarters occupies a skyscraper in [[downtown|Downtown]] in v11, near [[brocorp-metro-station|Brocorp Station]]. The agency is directed by Edgar J. Vacuum and handles local crime, including warrants for wanted suspects with bounties on their heads. A board displays the ten most wanted people in Broville v11. The agency's stated motto is “protect and Shoot Doge.” Items stored at headquarters use Minecraft objects to represent police equipment: | Equipment | Representation | | --- | --- | | Baton, with the item name Baton | Wooden sword | | Body armor, with the item name Body Armor | Black leather chestplate | | Stun device, with the item name Taser | Shears enchanted with Fire Aspect | | Helmet, with the item name Tactical Helmet | Iron helmet |"},{"id":"broville-bureau-of-science","title":"Broville Bureau of Science","category":"Organizations","lead":"A nonprofit organization sponsored by SOLAR and Compass to review patents and standards. Its building includes offices and laboratories available to the scientific community.","body":"Broville Bureau of Science, or B.B.S., is described as a nonprofit organization sponsored by [[compass-ltd|Compass]] and [[solar|SOLAR Corp.]] to review and approve new patents and standards. The patent offices and the Bureau’s director are located above the public level. Anyone may visit the offices, but the humorous guidance recommends submitting patents through the “BBS BBS” to avoid having new ideas accidentally incinerated. Below the public level is a series of small laboratories maintained by Compass R+D. They are free to use for anyone interested in scientific activity. The description notes that accidents happen and advises limiting work to less hazardous experiments."},{"id":"broville-city-hall","title":"Broville City Hall","category":"Locations","lead":"A civic building in Downtown, built above The Palisade business center. It houses municipal administration, reception facilities and an assembly.","body":"Broville City Hall stands in [[downtown|Downtown]], above The Palisade business center, and is presented as the center of municipal public policy management. It houses the office of Mayor Oldshoes, also described as chief executive of [[brocorp|BroCORP]], the largest local firm. The satirical characterization of the mayor says that he never visits his office and delegates almost all responsibilities to his deputies. The building includes: - A reception area and several administrative offices. - A staff lounge, meeting room and space dedicated to foreign relations. - A reception hall, guest rooms and a dignitary’s suite. - A rooftop garden. City Hall also houses an assembly responsible for the city’s legal matters, whose motto is “Sic Sember Cannabis”. A tower flying the Broville flag rises from the top of the building, watched over by a tower keeper."},{"id":"broville-disaster-warning-system","title":"Broville Disaster Warning System","category":"Concepts","lead":"A long-term project attributed to Compass LTD to evacuate the metropolitan area, using signal towers and tunnels toward the airport.","body":"The Broville Disaster Warning System, abbreviated BDWS, is presented as a long-term plan developed and maintained by [[compass-ltd|Compass LTD]]. Its purpose is to evacuate the Broville metropolitan area in the event of an apocalyptic-scale disaster. Once completed, the system was intended to include several signal towers and evacuation tunnels directing the population to the airfield associated with the [[broville-international-airport|international airport]]. The description concerns a project in development, rather than a network whose completion is confirmed. The account attributes the suspension of work to a class-action lawsuit against safety-vest manufacturers. Continuation would depend on the payment demanded in that dispute, narrated in hostile language toward those seeking payment. No date for resuming work is supplied."},{"id":"broville-embassy-center","title":"Broville Embassy Center","category":"Locations","lead":"A diplomatic representation center included in Broville’s building inventory.","body":"Broville Embassy Center is a large diplomatic complex in [[canals-district|Canals District]], at −510, 75, 2090. It is intended to receive representatives of other cities, companies, and outside dignitaries, encouraging meetings and relations with [[broville|Broville]]. The building is presented as a symbol of the metropolis’s political influence. The complex stands between the monumental In Booze We Trust statue and the mayor’s house. This position distinguishes it from [[broville-city-hall|City Hall]] in [[downtown|Downtown]]. | Floor | Representation | | --- | --- | | 0 | Hoville embassy | | 1 | Redlight City | | 2 | Vertoak City | | 3 | Melonport | The proximity of the embassies to the mayor’s residence is described in the community narrative as a cause of frequent egg-throwing fights between their occupants. That account attributes the deaths of three people, including one Broville resident, to an incident in 2016. [[melonport|Melonport]] is presented as the city with the most difficult relationship with Broville. A succession of diplomatic incidents is associated with an attack on Melon Harbor, for which no date is supplied."},{"id":"broville-general-hospital","title":"Broville General Hospital","category":"Locations","lead":"The city’s general hospital, distinct from the abandoned property known as The Asylum.","body":"Broville General Hospital, or BGH, occupies an imposing building in [[uptown|Uptown]]. Its interior contains reception areas, wards and patient rooms, operating rooms, and a morgue. Chests contain themed medical items, including healing potions renamed as medicine and empty vials. The [[asylum|asylum]] stands outside the urban core and has a separate body of records; it is a different property from the general hospital."},{"id":"broville-heights","title":"Broville Heights","category":"Regions","lead":"A predominantly residential suburban area just outside the city of Broville.","body":"Broville Heights is a suburban area just outside Broville, recorded in the maps and station directory. Its housing consists largely of single-family houses and townhouses built close together. This density leaves residents with limited private outdoor space. The community description contrasts its range of living conditions with the urban deterioration of [[brompton|Brompton]]. The main landmarks are Broville Heights Outdoor Mall and [[broville-heights-station|Broville Heights Station]]. The open-air shopping center and the station provide reference points within this predominantly residential neighborhood."},{"id":"broville-heights-station","title":"Broville Heights Station","category":"Transport","lead":"A rail transit stop serving the Broville Heights area.","body":"Broville Heights Station is a [[broville-subway|subway]] station in the suburban community of [[broville-heights|Broville Heights]], near [[broville|Broville]]. It stands in the heart of the neighborhood, directly across the street from Broville Heights Outdoor Mall. Its position serves both residents making their daily commute into the city and visitors traveling out of the urban area to shop at nearby stores. The station was built by [[foxcreek-properties|FoxCreek Properties]] in mid-2012. Like [[university-station|University Station]], it combines an underground passenger area with a small kiosk and an aboveground shopping area."},{"id":"broville-international-airport","title":"Broville International Airport","category":"Regions","lead":"The region’s main airport complex, on the peninsula northeast of the urban area. It is an arrival point from Fractal Island and a Purple Line metro terminus.","body":"Broville International Airport lies east of the city, north of [[braulding-county|Braulding]] and west of [[fort-bulwark|Fort Bulwark]]. It is the region's main airport infrastructure and an arrival point for visitors. Airport is a terminus of the [[broville-subway|metro]] Purple Line, with [[boardwalk-station|Boardwalk]] the next stop toward the center; network signs regularly indicate the airport's direction. The tourist guide Warp Recommendations directs visitors to cross the bridge east of the starting point on [[fractal-island|Fractal Island]] and board the plane that provides access to the airport. It presents this arrival as a “fresh off the boat” experience for a first visit and recommends the metro for onward travel through the city. The runways and hangars also house helicopters, described by their appearance as having paramilitary or industrial designs. The airport is close to Broville's military base; the aircraft's appearance alone does not identify their operator. Boarding-area records list five airlines alongside the motto UNITED WE DIVE: - RP JL Airways. - Metalus Air. - Bro Express. - FSHairways. - Golaire. The International Airline Company Headquarters stands in southern [[downtown|Downtown]], at −233, 101, 1899. Inside, a message reads: “THAT'S NOT A CHEMTRAIL, ITS JUST AN OIL LEAK”. The sign jokes about suspicions surrounding aircraft trails and the reliability of the engines."},{"id":"broville-investment-bankers","title":"Broville Investment Bankers","category":"Organizations","lead":"An investment group connected to Broville’s stock exchange, whose history links the city’s rebuilding to the organization of surviving wealth and the enrichment of the elite.","body":"Broville Investment Bankers presents itself as a place to increase wealth in a “safe, secure, and steady” way, with an offer aimed at the already rich. Its community history places its founding shortly after the [[latency|latency]] disaster, during Broville's reconstruction: people who escaped with their fortunes sought to multiply them and secure their position in the elite. This framing belongs to the institution's account and does not independently date the disaster. Mortimer Shecklestein reportedly saw the creation of the [[broville-stock-exchange|Broville Stock Exchange]], abbreviated BroSEX, as an opportunity to expand his fortune. He established the group in the tower beside the exchange building and began investing other people's money. The account also attributes the substantial gains for investors and the founder to illegal means. Over the years, the group is described as growing in size and influence, apparently always knowing when to buy and sell. That consistency fueled suspicions of market manipulation. The narrative even credits it with anticipating the [[solar-bankruptcy|fall of SOLAR]] and never having a bad fiscal year. The accusations reportedly had no visible effect on the institution's expansion, which accompanied the city's growth. The absence of losses and the precision of its investments are claims within this satirical financial portrait, rather than a demonstration of the legality or safety of its services. The building underwent a complete remodel in 2013 to replace its dated appearance with a more modern office complex. The work included floors, lighting and a new redstone wiring system. An elevated skybridge connected the tower directly to the neighboring BroSEX building."},{"id":"broville-memorial-pantheon","title":"Broville Memorial Pantheon","category":"Locations","lead":"A civic memorial recorded in the city’s landmark inventory.","body":"Broville Memorial Pantheon Square stands on the edge of [[downtown|Downtown]], near [[broville-city-hall|City Hall]]. The complex is recorded as one of the city's monumental landmarks. The square hosts the traditional mayoral address, which the community description presents as being broadcast by every television station in the city. It also serves as the setting for political demonstrations. It contains the Monument dedicated to the builders everywhere, honoring builders everywhere. The monument is described as a symbol that has resonated with several generations of residents. The tradition associating monumental symbols with the [[broviet-union|Broviet Union]] has its own context and does not replace the square's documented civic function."},{"id":"broville-observatory","title":"Broville Observatory","category":"Locations","lead":"A large telescope in the mountains south of the Downtown docks, reached from the science center and accompanied by an exhibition of objects found nearby.","body":"The observatory stands in the mountains south of the [[downtown|Downtown]] docks. The trail from the science center is the recommended approach. The institution serves scientists interested in the space beyond the save-file boundaries, the man in the Moon, and whatever might lie farther away. Its popularity is described as growing since its founding. Visitors can see a museum of objects found in the surrounding area. The telescope is also available for public use when experiments are not in progress. In [[broville-v10|v10]], it was little more than a building on a small hill, a little beyond Oldshoes' house. It had a main circular chamber with adjoining living and science halls. A circular staircase inside the chamber was the only access to the telescope platform. According to its history, no remains of the telescope or complex were recovered from the disaster wreckage. The [[broville-v11|v11]] history presents the building as the result of an order for a small desk microscope from [[compass-ltd|Compass Ltd]] and [[solar|SOLAR Corp]]. Instead, the companies delivered the observatory, and SOLAR engineers humorously argued that it more or less met the request. After the building was completed, a rail line was established to make travel to the complex easier, as it is surrounded by dangerous snowy peaks."},{"id":"broville-paranormal-society","title":"Broville Paranormal Society","category":"Organizations","lead":"A paranormal investigation group granted permission to film at the asylum in April 2013.","body":"On April 3, 2013, plumber and paranormal investigator [[jameson-hawks|Jameson Hawks]] records permission to enter the [[asylum|asylum]]. He saw recording a show as an opportunity to change careers. In the same diary, he says the group had already been interviewed by the local newspaper [[the-broville-beat|The Broville Beat]] and secured a television deal. The team arrives on April 10 with cameras, lighting, and a generator, under [[mike-host|Mike Host]]’s direction. Recording combines camera observation with audio capture. [[trina-brendel|Trina Brendel]] monitors the material while Jameson and [[wilson-harolds|Wilson Harolds]] move through the building. Wilson also handles power and access. Eric, who Wilson says joined the group only a few weeks earlier, and [[tim|Tim]] appear in their colleagues’ accounts. In the second diary, Jameson reports that the cameras had been installed in the dormitory and that director Mike Host proposed spending some time there that night. The plan was to leave some cameras recording during the day and return that evening to collect them. Jameson describes little activity during this initial observation beyond possible voices in the recordings, noises and particles. At the same time, he acknowledges that editing could make the material more frightening. His account thus distinguishes field observations from the intended effect of the show. The [[bvps-diaries|diaries]] alternate between authors, times, and locations. The authorization entry and field accounts show how the visit was planned and then conducted. The sequence of events appears in the [[the-asylum-expedition|asylum expedition]]."},{"id":"broville-police-headquarters","title":"Broville Police Headquarters","category":"Locations","lead":"The police headquarters recorded among the city’s public buildings.","body":"Broville Police Headquarters is identified as the city’s police headquarters. The supplied documentation records the building by name and image without detailing its layout, internal facilities or history."},{"id":"broville-stock-exchange","title":"Broville Stock Exchange","category":"Locations","lead":"Broville Stock Exchange, also called the SExchange, is a financial market above a bank and connected to the neighboring investment building.","body":"Broville Stock Exchange, humorously shortened to SExchange, is the city’s stock market. Its introduction portrays it as a place for wealthy people to amuse themselves irresponsibly. Its described primary function is to let corporations, governments, municipalities and other business bodies raise capital or earn profits by channeling investors’ savings into ventures that the text satirically calls ludicrous and illegitimate. Its secondary function is the trading of securities between investors for cash, allowing them to exit positions and reduce exposure to risk. The exterior uses birch and spruce wood paneling, while the interior has more somber tones and dim lighting. The building stands above a bank: the first and second floors house the exchange, and the upper floors accommodate various investors. A skybridge connects the building to the adjacent [[broville-investment-bankers|Broville Investment Bankers]] group building."},{"id":"broville-subway","title":"Broville Subway","category":"Transport","lead":"The subway network crosses downtown, the canals, the university, and the airport. Museum, Brocorp, BN Tower, and Canals East are interchange points on the v11 diagram.","body":"The Department of Transportation map indicates stop order and interchanges; the distances shown are schematic. - Purple — [[concord-commons|Concord Commons]] → [[museum-of-natural-history|Museum]] → [[broville-bluff-station|Broville Bluffs]] → [[bn-tower|BN Tower]] → [[boardwalk-station|Boardwalk]] → [[broville-international-airport|Airport]]. - Green — Museum → [[university-station|University]] → [[oldtown|Old Town]] → [[brocorp-metro-station|Brocorp]] → [[downtown|Downtown]] → [[chinatown|China Town]]. - Red — BN Tower → Brocorp → [[canals-east|Canals East]]. - Blue — [[broverly-hills|Broverly Hills]] → [[broto-lake|Broto Lake]] → Campus → [[twin-pines-mall|Twin Pines Mall]] → Canals East → Stadium → [[brompton|Brompton]]. - Yellow — connection between Canals East and the [[breno-monorail|Breno Monorail]]. Museum connects Purple and Green. BN Tower connects Purple and Red. Brocorp connects Green and Red. Canals East brings together Red, Blue, and the Yellow connection. The underground subway in [[broville-v10|v10]] is described as a response to the need for fast access from West Broville to the city, as industry expanded in the business districts following the arrival of [[brocorp|BroCORP]]. Its construction is associated with the [[the-great-fire-of-broville|Great Fire of Broville]]. Stalls in each station sold return tickets for one BroBuck. The description from that period presents the subway as the city's only public transport, carrying more than a million passengers a year and earning almost 300,000 dollars from tourist ticket sales alone. These figures and the claim of exclusivity belong to the description of v10, rather than a count of the v11 network. The historical v11 list also names Boardwalk Station, Boss Station, University Square Station, [[broville-heights-station|Broville Heights Station]], Recreation Center Station, Mall Station, Elevated Station, [[brostreet-station|Bro Street Station]], and Concord Commons. It records station names without resolving all their correspondences with the stops on the diagram. The [[black-line|Black Line]] is documented by signs and community accounts. The [[regional-railway|regional railway]] has its own map and serves a different scale of travel."},{"id":"broville-university","title":"Broville University","category":"Locations","lead":"Broville’s main university in v11, known as BroU. Its extensive campus, serving more than 3,000 students, helps locate the academic area and its stations.","body":"Broville University, or BroU, is presented as the main university in [[broville-v11|v11]], serving more than 3,000 students. Its extensive grounds include a multistory classroom building, dormitories, a pool and a cafeteria praised for its food. The campus is a landmark for [[university-square|University Square]] and nearby transport access. [[university-station|University Station]] and Campus are the names of separate stops, useful for navigating the university area. Most students are described as taking part in a healthy rivalry with neighboring Hapsland Uni. This relationship appears as a feature of local academic life. The community account mentions several inquiries over the preceding two decades into graduating classes that might have direct links to Broville's criminal underworld and Hip. S. Ter. This is framed as a suspicion, without identifying here which classes or what conclusions were established. The Broville Education board recommends avoiding those classes, completing the satirical portrait of the institution and its connections beyond the campus."},{"id":"broville-v10","title":"Broville v10","category":"World","lead":"The original world published in 2011, built before v11 adopted new terrain.","body":"Oldshoes dates the city's creation to 2010. The v10 page on Planet Minecraft is dated August 16, 2011, and yumstheman opened a release discussion on Minecraft Forum the following day. Looking back in February 2014, Oldshoes placed the end of work on v10 around autumn 2011. The publication date and the approximate recollection of work ending mark different milestones. Oldshoes' page presents a city to enter and explore, with interiors, treasures, and secrets. Its gallery preserves views of the bay, buildings, and interiors. Captions and notes identify the Coterie Craft texture pack and renders made with Chunky, two factors to consider when using the images to recognize the appearance of blocks. On August 24, 2011, Pin_Collector described exploring the map in the release thread. The account mentions docks, ships, a railway, catacombs, and the player respawning in the hospital. It also mentions an ice rink that melted in the player's game, an effect the player attributed to a mod. The account places a waterfront, rail transport, and hidden spaces beneath the city in the same map. Pikimon_2 reported performance problems in the same thread and suspected the large number of signs in The Hive. The message documents that name and the presence of signs in that player's experience; the explanation for the slowdown was the player's conjecture. In the 2014 demo presentation, Oldshoes listed v10-style housing among the neighborhood types in [[broville-v11|v11]]. The comment documents an explicit architectural reference. In the same discussion, Fangride recalled sewers, an underground crypt, shops, and skyscrapers, but placed those memories in v10 or an earlier version. The 2011 account provides the most direct attribution of the catacombs to v10. - **Oldshoes' publication.** The original page is the reference for authorship and date. During the recorded inspection, its main button returned to the page itself without delivering a verifiable file. - **Release post referenced in 2011.** On the forum, yumstheman directed readers to a specific post on Oldshoes' Tumblr. The contemporary reference preserves the historical route, but the inspected post did not provide a verifiable package. - **Skeep_Redinho's conversion.** On the v10 page, Oldshoes reproduces the conversion author's account. The author used Minecraft 1.7 and WorldEdit to fix chests and paintings disappearing when upgrading the world and reports that some doors remained cut off. The package is presented as a separate copy adapted for 1.8 onward. Its compatibility label describes that adaptation. The [[the-sinking-of-broville|sinking]] is told in v11's books as the disaster that befell the previous city. The name The Hive in the 2011 thread provides a point of comparison with [[h-i-v-e|H.I.V.E.]] in later reports. Attributing specific characters, mechanisms, places, and events to the two editions requires comparing the contents of their respective worlds. See [[continuity|From v10 to v11]] for the revision of the story described by its creator."},{"id":"broville-v11","title":"Broville v11","category":"World","lead":"A new world begun in 2012 and completed in 2016, containing the places, books, and missions covered by this encyclopedia.","body":"In February 2014, Oldshoes explained on the Minecraft Forum that work had begun in August 2012 on a new world to take advantage of terrain generation and large biomes. At the time, he estimated that v11’s urban area alone was nearly twice the size of the entire [[broville-v10|v10]] map. The demo released that month showed part of the project. The announcement already promised distinct neighborhoods, automated transport, books, custom equipment, and hidden complexes. The v11 page on Planet Minecraft opened in March 2013 and records development from before the demo. The pages for the two editions place completion in November and December 2016, respectively. The changelog records v11.1 on December 8, while the December 14 Minecraft.net feature reports that completion and distribution had been announced the previous week. According to the interview and Oldshoes’s credits, the team brought together more than 60 contributors. In the Minecraft.net interview, Oldshoes identified Victoria, British Columbia, as an approximate reference for geography and climate. Some of the terrain was generated by the game. In the mountains, the team piled up stone, sculpted the masses with Voxel Sniper, and set the transition from grass to snow using the default cloud height. Oldshoes described manually adjusting biomes and using the server’s weather to accumulate snow in selected areas. - **Urban center.** The green line connects [[museum-of-natural-history|Museum]] to [[brocorp-metro-station|Brocorp Station]]. The red line runs through Brocorp and Canals East, connecting to the blue line serving [[brompton|Brompton]]. Names and connections come from the package’s Metro Map. - **Old infrastructure.** Traces of the [[black-line|Black Line]] survive on Mid Side Station signs and beneath the canals area. A Mid Side sign was inventoried at X −328, Y 66, Z 1802. Signs in the world complement the diagram of the operating metro when investigating this network. - **Region.** The [[regional-railway|regional railway]] map links the Coal Mine and Nuclear Plant area to the port, continuing through Braulding, Portvale, and Fort Bulwark. On the Road Map, the [[asylum|asylum]] appears in E8, north of [[solar-western-complex|SOLAR]]. Oldshoes’s announcement lists more than 800 buildings and highlights interiors, redstone mechanisms, and custom objects. These features occur at different scales. A BrownTech catalog offers a Compass T4-80 computer for 199.99, while the [[broville-paranormal-society|BVPS]] diaries let visitors follow an expedition through the asylum. Brian Garlick’s record, dated August 10, 1998, connects portal experiments to SOLAR’s crisis. One copy of this book occurs at X −261, Y 81, Z 2012 in v11.4.4. The local inventory of that edition contains 638 book occurrences and 319 distinct combinations of title, author, and pages. Copies may repeat the same text in multiple places. An inventory coordinate identifies the observed occurrence, helping distinguish the place of discovery from the place described by the narrator. - **v11.1, December 2016.** The changelog records fixes to the Museum of Natural History and Old Town Station signage, together with a revised road map and a district map. The site catalogs this edition for Minecraft 1.10.2. - **v11.2, July 2022, and v11.3, June 2023.** The former migrated the world to 1.19, crediting Xeraction. The latter moved to 1.20 and fixed, among other issues, a failure when generating new chunks and the End teleporter’s particles. - **v11.4 series, 2024–2026.** The July 2024 update moved the map to 1.21 and restored mechanisms and puzzles. In October, v11.4.2 adjusted maps and restored guardians in the Oriental Casino. v11.4.3 updated commands in October 2025. v11.4.4, released March 24, 2026 for Minecraft 26.1, updated the world format and fixed entity textures. The link Oldshoes presents on Planet Minecraft as the original release for Minecraft 1.10 leads through AdFoc.us to a MediaFire page titled Broville v11.1, with the file Broville_v11.1.zip. This chain confirms that the early revision is being offered. The build preceding the December 8 changelog still needs to be identified and compared. The books and coordinates cited here come from v11.4.4 and should be read with that edition in mind."},{"id":"brovilles-sea-and-islands","title":"Broville’s Sea and Islands","category":"Regions","lead":"An index of island destinations, ports, and the mainland’s seafaring stories.","body":"[[brolapagos|Brolapagos]], [[fractal-island|Fractal]], and [[keister-island|Keister]] are reference pages for the island and coastal destinations brought together in this index. [[city-port|City Port]] is a reference for the port area. The [[bvs-fleet|fleet]] brings together the stories of vessels and provides another route into seafaring associated with Broville."},{"id":"browery","title":"Browery","category":"Locations","lead":"A cluster of narrow homes in Brompton. Community history links its origins to land left by an interrupted housing project.","body":"Browery is a cluster of narrow homes in [[brompton|Brompton]]. Community history associates its origins with the land left by an interrupted housing project."},{"id":"bvps-diaries","title":"BVPS diaries","category":"Documents","lead":"Records scattered throughout the asylum, documenting authorization, arrival, and the team’s passage through different levels.","body":"The collection includes entries signed by Jameson Hawks, Mike Host, Wilson Harolds, and Trina Brendel. Jameson opens the series with the April 3 authorization and records the April 10 arrival. The number 15 identifies two texts by Jameson, preserved at different locations. Volume 16 is by Mike and dated April 11, earlier than several volumes numbered before it. - The first volume is at −1320, 68, 1470 and the second at −1344, 72, 1471. - Volumes 9 and 10 share −1420, 90, 1517. Volumes 12 and 13 occur at −1419, 54, 1540. - The two texts numbered 15 are at −1458, 53, 1465 and −1449, 53, 1497. Volume 16 is at −1446, 18, 1504. Copies of several volumes exist outside the property. The [[document-archive|document collection]] indexes consultation points. The reconstruction comparing times and testimonies appears in the [[the-asylum-expedition|mission]]."},{"id":"bvs-double-rainbow","title":"BVS Double Rainbow","category":"Locations","lead":"A merchant ship remembered in community documentation for its sails and its role as a harbor tourist attraction.","body":"The BVS Double Rainbow is a medium-sized merchant ship in Broville's inner bay and harbor area. Built in 1963 by Hapsyshipyards under Teddy S. Hapsy's command, it became a tourist attraction for its gay and homoerotic sail imagery and three distinctive ice cream flavors. The ship's community history is told in a satirical tone. In 1962, Hapsy was a respected Broville Air Force pilot stationed at the base near the [[broville-v11|v11]] area. The narrative attributes his dishonorable discharge to complaints about supposedly “unnatural” intimacy and an obsession with sailors; that language belongs to the prejudice depicted in the account. He was ordered to leave the base and the v11 area permanently. Hapsy already harbored a secret love of the sea and began having erotic dreams about boats. In December 1962, he left his neglected wife and six children to build his own ship at the [[broville-v10|v10]] docks. Construction began in February 1963, involved three workplace deaths and ended with the ship being commissioned as a merchant vessel. Hapsy died in poverty three weeks later. The account places the [[broviet-union|Broviet Union's]] hiring of the ship to smuggle nuclear weapons into Cubro during the Cubros Missile Crisis three and a half weeks after commissioning. The payment is unknown, although rumors put it in the millions. Services attributed to Double Rainbow include rescuing 63 disabled orphan children from a fire aboard the overloaded and sinking [[bvs-mexicoke|BVS Mexicoke]]. Later, the ship remained mostly dormant in the harbor, available to rent for thirty brobucks and a packet of Mentos. The Broville Tourist Guide jokingly presents it as the least disreputable ship in the bay. Families and tourists visit to admire its sails, while its publicity plays on the fantasy of being an attractive sailor again. During the summer LGBT festival, the ship is loaded with fireworks and sent into the bay for displays described as dazzling and sensual. It is also a popular wedding venue because of its low rental price. The merchant ship appears in the v10 list. The v11 list records a DDG-1 BVS Double Rainbow, reusing the name; the two [[bvs-fleet|BVS fleet]] entries alone do not establish that these are the same vessel."},{"id":"bvs-fleet","title":"BVS Fleet","category":"Organizations","lead":"Vessels recorded in Broville documentation. Community lists distinguish ships in v10 from those in v11.","body":"Community inventories distinguish ships in [[broville-v10|v10]] from those in [[broville-v11|v11]]. The name Double Rainbow appears in both lists, but the differing designations do not establish that these entries refer to one vessel. - [[bvs-double-rainbow|BVS Double Rainbow]]. - [[bvs-supremecy|BVS Supremecy]]. - [[bvs-mexicoke|BVS Mexicoke]]. | Recorded designation | Vessel and notes | | --- | --- | | CV-1 | BVS Brazilian; carries BVS Waxholm I, BVS Waxholm II and BVS Waxholm III. | | DDG-1 | BVS Double Rainbow. | | FF | BVS Bigotry. | | No designation provided | BVS N***** F*****, recorded as lost and with its name censored in the list. | The accounts of [[experiment-25-c-932-1|Dr. Juergen]] and [[experiment-26|Experiment 26]] describe independent onboard tests. The ships’ documented chronology alone cannot establish that these accounts concern the same experiment."},{"id":"bvs-mexicoke","title":"BVS Mexicoke","category":"Locations","lead":"A small abandoned vessel in Broville Bay, associated with an old smuggling operation and a satirical census about maritime safety.","body":"The BVS Mexicoke is described as the smallest boat in [[brovilles-sea-and-islands|Broville Bay]] and as having been abandoned for several years. It is also called the “Coke boat”. Its poor reputation appears both in petitions for demolition and in the nickname “the lamest ship in the bay”. The community history places its use for drug smuggling in Broville’s early days, satirically linking the arrival of hipsters with increased demand. The boat is said to have served as a drug runner for more than two years before a successful BVPD raid arrested a ring of smugglers. The vessel was due to be destroyed, but the paperwork discouraged the officer responsible. Repeated delays ended with the boat being condemned, after which it became a home for people with alcoholism who had been evicted from their houses. Question 79A of the 2010 BroCensus asked: “If Broville had, theoretically, been found to have avoided many national maritime safety laws, which ship in the bay would you scuttle first?” According to the narrative, the overwhelming answer was “BVS Mexicoke/Coke boat”. The episode is a joke about the vessel’s reputation, rather than a record of its sinking."},{"id":"bvs-supremecy","title":"BVS Supremecy","category":"Locations","lead":"A v10 battleship whose name is spelled Supremecy. The story associated with the community continuation distinguishes it from an earlier BVS Supremacy.","body":"The BVS Supremecy is the [[broville-v10|v10]] battleship with the misspelled name. The story developed around that spelling distinguishes it from the correctly spelled BVS Supremacy and places the original Supremacy in an earlier era, lost to time. According to that narrative, the West Bro Corp Company, a colonial-era version of [[brocorp|Bro Corp]], reused the name for its new flagship because its officials did not want to invent another. Under H.D. Locke, the Supremecy suffered damage in battles against pirates and other threats to the company's vital shipping lanes. It was eventually retired in one piece and turned into a tourist attraction in the capital of Brottowa, disparagingly described as an expensive visit to an old ship. Puhelias used the spelling error in v10 as a starting point for developing the history of the actual BVS Supremacy and the West Bro Corp Company in her continuation presented as Broville v12. The comparison between the v10 and v12 ships was presented while still in development. This backstory belongs to that continuation's narrative, without retroactively establishing events in the v11 books."},{"id":"canals-district","title":"Canals District","category":"Regions","lead":"A canal district south of Uptown and west of Brompton. The former station converted into a bookshop preserves the connection to Brosco’s laboratory.","body":"The district map places Canals south of [[uptown|Uptown]], east of [[broto-lake|Broto Lake]], and west of [[brompton|Brompton]]. Warp Recommendations identifies the cathedral as a landmark for exploring the neighborhood. - [[canals-east|Canals East]] brings together the red and blue lines and the yellow connection to Breno Monorail. - [[canals-west|Canals West]] lies between River Valley and Breno on the [[regional-railway|regional railway]] diagram. - At −276, 47, 2139, a sign marks access to the [[black-line|Black Line]]. In Journal, found at −507, 70, 2228, [[edward-page|Edward Page]] recounts opening the [[edward-pages-bookshop|bookshop]] in a deteriorated station. Investigating machinery noise, he found an elevator shaft behind a bookshelf. A confession by Dan Brosco is recorded nearby, at −496, 60, 2224."},{"id":"canals-east","title":"Canals East","category":"Transport","lead":"A station at the meeting of the red and blue lines. The diagram also connects it with the Breno monorail.","body":"Canals East is an interchange between the red and blue [[broville-subway|metro]] lines in the [[canals-district|canals]] area. The diagram also indicates a connection with the [[breno|Breno]] [[breno-monorail|monorail]]."},{"id":"canals-west","title":"Canals West","category":"Transport","lead":"A regional railway stop between River Valley and Breno, distinct from the Canals East subway station.","body":"Canals West is a [[regional-railway|regional railway]] stop between [[river-valley-farmland|River Valley]] and [[breno|Breno]]. Its name distinguishes it from [[canals-east|Canals East]], which belongs to the subway network."},{"id":"canopy","title":"Canopy","category":"Organizations","lead":"A company whose records describe experiments with matter and the B-Virus. The community history of the Black Line connects it to railway funding.","body":"The community wiki attributes most of the funding for the [[black-line|Black Line]], built between 1919 and 1923, to Canopy. Presented as public transport, the line reportedly served primarily wealthy residents because of its fares. The community account lists a Canopy Corp station among the abandoned stations. Its exact location remains unresolved. In the first record of [[inorganic-sentience|Inorganic Sentience]], variant S-146-B derives from 24 grams of magma cream injected with [[b-virus|B-Virus]]. The mass began generating heat about two seconds after exposure and moving about two seconds later. During the first experiment, the heat melted the container. The team applied liquid nitrogen and placed the sample in refrigerated storage. On July 8, the team applied B-Virus to a mixture of two parts moss and one part pig brain tissue. The mass began moving after four seconds, assumed a cubic shape, and moved by hopping. Left in a container overnight, it doubled in size. The next day, it was frozen with liquid nitrogen and divided. When thawed, the fragments resumed moving independently and were frozen again. In Research, [[alghus-vinto|Alghus Vinto]] reports that his team had spent days studying a fragment received at the laboratory. They removed an eye-like structure that appeared inactive, while two other samples showed signs of life. Vinto describes a small purple organism resembling a worm, apparently living in the material, and announces animal testing. Another copy, attributed to darkfox118, contains the same account. Date of Outbreak records an outbreak in the Green Sector on October 23. Scientist, catalogued at the same location, −1055, 28, 704, lists Gregor Grendel as the head and Kao, Nawon, and Chirb as team members. The origin of B-Virus, the source of the material studied by Vinto, and the year of the outbreak remain unknown. The books from [[canopy-laboratories|Canopy's laboratories]] also leave open whether the S-146 variants, Vinto's samples, and the outbreak belong to the same chain of events."},{"id":"canopy-laboratories","title":"Canopy Laboratories","category":"Locations","lead":"Facilities identified through records of samples, medications and biological tests.","body":"The [[canopy|Canopy]] laboratories are identified through records of samples, medications and biological tests. The documentation is associated with [[inorganic-sentience|Inorganic Sentience]] and the [[b-virus|B-Virus]]. The notebook [[inorganic-sentience|Inorganic Sentience]] records tests on two B-Virus variants on July 7–9, 1998. Its accounts cover heat generation, movement, growth and cold storage. A separate Research entry by [[alghus-vinto|Alghus Vinto]], dated July 28, describes an eye-like structure removed from a material sample. That segment appears inactive, while two other samples show signs of life. Vinto also records a small purple worm-like organism and plans animal testing. These are separate descriptions in the laboratory records, not interchangeable names for one specimen."},{"id":"captain-brobeard","title":"Captain Brobeard","category":"Characters","lead":"A pirate captain associated with accounts of treasure and his crew. The museum jokes about the contents of his chest.","body":"Captain Brobeard appears in accounts of treasure and his crew, connected with Broville’s [[bvs-fleet|fleet]] and [[easter-eggs-and-discoveries|secrets]]. The [[museum-of-natural-history|museum]] preserves a humorous reference to the contents of his chest."},{"id":"casinos","title":"Casinos","category":"Locations","lead":"Commercial entertainment venues whose gaming halls, shows, and hotels form part of city life beyond the major research storylines.","body":"Casinos account for an important part of [[broville|Broville]]’s commercial entertainment and gambling industry. Although leisure establishments are scattered across the city, the sector is especially concentrated in [[breno|Breno]], with bustling streets and heavily visited tourist areas. Their buildings combine gaming halls, performances, hotels, and distinctive architectural themes. The 777 Casino stands near the intersection of Avista Boulevard and Bro Street. Its illuminated sign features three sevens made of emeralds, with golden horizontal stems. Alongside gaming tables, it hosts artistic performances: one poster advertises the piano show Liberagay. Spend-A-Lot Casino is one of Avista Boulevard’s well-known establishments. Loosely inspired by the legend of Camelot, it resembles a massive medieval castle and features one of Broville’s largest rooftops on the second floor. Shamrock Casino uses the theme of Irish luck. Green decor and shamrock motifs make it recognizable, while its atmosphere is described as friendlier. It is particularly popular with Broville residents, more so than with tourists. Also on Avista Boulevard, the Breno Oriental Casino features architecture and interior decoration inspired by the Orient. Its atmosphere contrasts with the usual bustle of gaming halls. It is described as a high-end establishment, appreciated both for the beauty of its decor and for the experience of gambling. The Broladgio is a palace of entertainment in Breno, with monumental architecture and an exclusive setting catering to high society and professional players. The community description ranks it as Broville’s second most visited tourist site and describes its hotel as the most renowned in the city. The hotel has 22 rooms, including three suites and one prestige suite. Facilities include three rooftop pools and another reserved for guests. Seik Palace complements the complex as a dinner theater inspired by ancient Egypt."},{"id":"cattail-town","title":"Cattail Town","category":"Regions","lead":"A settlement marked north of the sanatorium, around the swamps.","body":"Cattail Town is a settlement marked north of the [[asylum|sanatorium]], around the [[swamplands|swamps]]."},{"id":"cemetery-and-crypts","title":"Cemetery and Crypts","category":"Locations","lead":"Graves, a cabin, and underground passages hold traces of the search for three keys.","body":"The bridge, rows of gravestones, and former caretaker's cabin are landmarks on the surface. The camp of [[darius-brolo|Darius Brolo]], at −715, 63, 1314, holds an exploration account. The crypts preserve messages and equipment associated with [[willam-brotori|Willam Brotori]]. - In My Last Homily, Pastor Broliri says he saw the dead leaving their graves in broad daylight and records unusual tremors. He abandons the site and asks future visitors to do the same. - In Witch Note, the cabin's occupants describe the sleeping figure as a god and hope to gain power by awakening it. The note is at −640, 66, 1432. - In My Final Words, Brotori calls the prisoner a demon, says he sealed it away, and fears its confinement may be temporary. Darius begins his search from a different rumor, concerning a wronged man who would reward his liberator. The disagreement between these accounts is part of the investigation. The route connecting the clues is covered in [[the-cemeterys-three-keys|The Three Keys of the Cemetery]]."},{"id":"chinatown","title":"Chinatown","category":"Regions","lead":"An area between Downtown, Arts District and City Port. Its name appears on the city map and the metro network.","body":"Chinatown lies on the eastern edge of Downtown, between [[arts-district|Arts District]] and the [[city-port|port area]]. The green line terminates at China Town on the [[broville-subway|metro]] diagram."},{"id":"timeline","title":"Chronology","category":"World","lead":"Dates in city documents, periods without a secure year and the map production calendar.","body":"- 1089 and 1909 appear among the first stops of [[project-chr0nos|Chr0NOS]]. - 1924–1925 are the years covered by the [[diary-1925|trench diary]]. - 1929 and 1934 appear in [[asylum|sanatorium]] patient files. - In 1983, a [[solar|SOLAR]] leaflet recruits volunteers. In December of that year, [[dondiego-allen|Dondiego Allen]] writes in the mountains. - In July 1998, [[inorganic-sentience|Inorganic Sentience]] records Canopy's tests. On August 10, Brian Garlick leaves [[final-notes|his final account]]. - April 2013 is the month of the [[the-asylum-expedition|BVPS expedition]]. In the Doctor's account, the [[the-sinking-of-broville|sinking]] precedes the recovery of the reactor and the rebuilding of the city. The disaster's year remains unresolved. The ancient peoples and Heraldus likewise lack firm dates. The 1919–1923 dating for the Black Line and 2002 dating for SOLAR's bankruptcy come from community articles. They appear with that attribution in the corresponding articles. [[broville-v10|v10]] was released in 2011. Construction of [[broville-v11|v11]] began on new terrain in August 2012 and ended in November 2016."},{"id":"city-port","title":"City Port","category":"Regions","lead":"A port on the city’s eastern edge. It is the urban landmark before Braulding on the regional railway.","body":"The port connects the city network with the eastern corridor. On the [[regional-railway|regional railway]], it lies between [[breno|Breno]] and [[braulding-county|Braulding]]. It is a landmark for distinguishing the port area, [[brompton|Brompton]] and the [[broville-international-airport|airport]]."},{"id":"coal-mine","title":"Coal Mine","category":"Locations","lead":"A western point on the railway network near the power plant, identified as Coal Mine on the road map.","body":"Coal Mine is the western end of the [[regional-railway|regional railway]] branch that passes through the [[nuclear-plant|power plant]]. The road map places it near the boundary between the sandy area and forested terrain."},{"id":"compass-consumer-goods","title":"Compass Consumer Goods","category":"Collections","lead":"A line presenting everyday objects as Compass technology, including a navigation compass and a clock.","body":"[[compass-ltd|Compass R&D]] occasionally allows successful experiments considered non-dangerous to be sold at Broville's Consumer Goods Labs. The Compass Consumer Goods line turns everyday objects, including a compass, a clock and clothing, into products presented with technical language and satirical warnings. The navigation system promises help locating the most important point of interest. Not all models have the .tp framework preinstalled. Users are instructed not to use the bundled tp software until .tp has been updated; failure to comply may cause the device to stop responding. The unit displays the time of day using Minecraft “ligic”. The text emphasizes that actual time is relative to the user. Its warning forbids applying logic to devices using “ligic”, on pain of partially or totally losing one's sense of time. “Chronoligical” and “ligic” preserve the spellings used in the product name and its humorous explanation. The clothing promises to improve tasks such as successfully taking on fire, failing at bungee jumping and swimming with mermaids, though not living with them. The manufacturer's note instructs users to read each item's restrictions carefully: Compass LTD accepts no responsibility for improperly used products."},{"id":"compass-ltd","title":"Compass LTD","category":"Organizations","lead":"A research and technology company founded in 2000 and connected to BroCORP in the community history. It acquired SOLAR assets and developed the project that became LightSource.","body":"Community history presents Compass LTD as one of the companies connected to [[brocorp|BroCORP]], focused on research and development. It places its founding in 2000, with Dr. Steve, PhD, as CEO and MasterSteviekun as director. The same history attributes the acquisition of [[solar|SOLAR]] assets and licenses to the company in 2002, after SOLAR filed for [[solar-bankruptcy|bankruptcy]]. The accounts give the transferred assets more than one recipient. In A BroBrah Was Born, [[don-bestick|Don Bestick]] credits [[brobrah|BroBrah]] with buying the collider when SOLAR was struggling. In the same promotional book, he calls for a boycott of Compass. The acquisition attributed to Compass should not be taken as proof that every piece of SOLAR equipment passed to a single company. The energy division's mission was to supply Compass and SOLAR properties with uninterrupted power while implementing experimental sources developed by the research division. Regular supply and the application of new technologies therefore appear as parts of the same activity. The research and development division earned income from facility leasing and patent royalties. Successful experiments considered non-dangerous could receive consumer versions and reach the market through [[compass-consumer-goods|Compass Consumer Goods]]. Community history also associates Compass and SOLAR with sponsorship of the [[broville-bureau-of-science|Broville Bureau of Science]] and credits Compass with maintaining its public laboratories. The company is also responsible for the [[broville-disaster-warning-system|Broville Disaster Warning System]], a project involving signal towers and evacuation tunnels toward the airport. - **Compass T4-80:** BrownTech's BT Catalog advertises this home computer for $199.99. - **OptiServ 2000:** the OptiServ 2000 Manual describes a steam-powered server. The machine was supposed to shut down if a piston jammed, preventing steam buildup. The manual acknowledges erroneous shutdowns caused by sensor failure and requires 45 hours of cooling before handling. - **Chronoligical Display Unit:** in OFFICIAL LETTER, MasterSteviekun invokes the warranty terms to reject a product recall. The letter satirizes the distinction between logic and the supposed ligic used to justify how the clock works. According to [[frank-fuchdix|Frank Fuchdix]], Compass revived a proposal shelved by SOLAR to automate the management of an entire company. The project took years and underwent revisions as hardware evolved. The team created [[lightsource|LightSource]] to test the program in a communications company and kept the connection secret to protect Compass's reputation. Frank's second diary attributes the removal of references to LightSource from Compass's research database to LightSource itself. He reports that, after being fired, he returned to the subsidiary's office and removed the rules preventing competition or legal action against Compass and its subsidiaries. The dates when the project began and the relationship broke down remain unresolved."},{"id":"concord-commons","title":"Concord Commons","category":"Regions","lead":"A district on the city’s western edge and the purple line terminus. It is a landmark for heading toward the forest around the asylum.","body":"The [[broville-subway|metro]] diagram marks Concord Commons as the purple line terminus. Roads to the west lead toward the forest and [[solar|SOLAR]]. The [[asylum|asylum]] lies farther north."},{"id":"court-of-rails","title":"Court of Rails","category":"Organizations","lead":"A group associated with the underground tracks. Hobo Ed mentions its disappearance when ordering HoBro’s withdrawal.","body":"The Court of Rails is associated with the underground tracks. [[hobo-ed|Hobo Ed]] mentions the group's disappearance when ordering [[hobros-escape|HoBro's]] withdrawal. The mention forms part of the withdrawal account without clarifying the missing group's fate. The Court’s disappearance is one of the incidents cited in [[hobo-ed|Hobo Ed’s]] proclamation Exodus. Alongside the deaths among the city’s vagrants, it persuades him that someone has found a way into the concealed network of homes and passages. His response is to order the brotherhood out of Broville rather than remain to investigate."},{"id":"crn065","title":"CRN065","category":"Characters","lead":"A SOLAR employee who uses Chr0NOS to show the visitor concealed episodes of imperial history.","body":"CRN065 is a [[solar|SOLAR]] employee connected with the use of [[project-chr0nos|Chr0NOS]]. Through this technology, he shows the visitor concealed episodes of [[imperial-war|imperial history]] associated with the [[czar-broseph-iii|Czar]]. CRN065 addresses the visitor directly through four TIMELOG books. The first records a successful jump to AD 1089 and treats the search for replacement fuel cores as a puzzle. The second records AD 1909, where he says he has passed himself off as a redstone power engineer. The [[project-chr0nos|Chr0NOS manual]] describes these destinations as fragments of altered time-space."},{"id":"culture-of-broville","title":"Culture of Broville","category":"World","lead":"Museums, sports, journalism and entertainment help explain the city through its residents’ everyday activities.","body":"Broville's cultural life includes museums, cinema, sports, journalism and entertainment. The city is home to the Museum of Science and Cine Slant Movie Studios, as well as the [[museum-of-natural-history|Museum of Natural History]], presented by the community description as one of the world's most visited museums. The Museum of Natural History is particularly known for its complete T-Rex bone collection. Community history dates its construction to 1867, as part of Broville's centenary. The museum also displays objects from ancient peoples and, underground, a technology that calls the schoolbook account of the war into question. [[broville-memorial-pantheon|Broville Memorial Pantheon]] forms part of the city's geography of monuments and public ceremonies. Edward Page searches the [[the-broville-beat|Broville Beat]] for old news to understand the station that became his bookstore. The newspaper thus also serves as a record of urban memory. The [[casinos|casinos]], [[gamebros-arcade|Gamebros arcade]] and [[cwc-stadium|stadium]] show everyday activities beyond the laboratories. The movie studios add filmmaking to this picture."},{"id":"cwc-stadium","title":"CWC Stadium","category":"Locations","lead":"A stadium in the area between the canals, Brompton, and City Port, recognizable by its footprint on the district map.","body":"The district map shows the recognizable footprint of CWC Stadium in the area between [[canals-district|the canals]], [[brompton|Brompton]], and [[city-port|City Port]]."},{"id":"czar-broseph-iii","title":"Czar Broseph III","category":"Characters","lead":"An imperial ruler associated with the trench conflict and the revolt narrated by CRN065.","body":"Czar Broseph III is the imperial ruler associated with the [[imperial-war|trench war]] and the revolt narrated by [[crn065|CRN065]]."},{"id":"d-f-holdings","title":"D & F Holdings","category":"Organizations","lead":"A holding and acquisitions company founded in Broville in 2013, originating from FoxCreek Properties INC. Its corporate tower stands on Fox Street in Downtown.","body":"D & F Holdings is described as one of the city’s largest land acquisition and property investment corporations. It originated with [[foxcreek-properties|FoxCreek Properties INC.]], which built its first offices near [[university-square|University Square]] on Broville’s outskirts in 2013. FoxCreek grew rapidly in the residential sector by purchasing a 50-chunk area from the city government, subsequently known as University Park. The development became one of the region’s most expensive subdivisions. Sales of 13 private houses, eight townhomes and two apartment buildings supplied the revenue used to establish D & F Holdings Incorporated. At its formation, the company’s portfolio included majority stakes in: - The University Park community. - Brotech Web Services. - FoxCreek Properties and its holdings. - MienKart Transportation. - Mautner Coal. - VC Insurance Services. This portfolio spanned resources, manufacturing, housing and technology. The corporate tower is identified as 118 Fox Street in [[downtown|Downtown Broville]]. Marketing Proposal is a set of campaign proposals signed by SELL IT! Marketing, which identifies itself as a D & F company. The document adds advertising to the company's business profile without giving the date or percentage of this holding. The proposal presents radio and television advertisements and a flyer for PestBros, a pest removal service. Its ideas include removing pigs and cows from rooftops and tackling silverfish infestations. The book records proposed advertisements without demonstrating that they were published or broadcast. The account based on second-quarter earnings anticipated rapid acquisition growth during the remainder of 2013 and possibly in 2014. This was an expectation at the time, rather than confirmation of later acquisitions."},{"id":"dan-brosco","title":"Dan Brosco","category":"Characters","lead":"A SOLAR researcher who disappeared while working in a laboratory in the canal district.","body":"Daniel “Dan” Brosco was a renowned physicist working for [[solar|SOLAR]] in the southern [[canals-district|Canal District]]. His company-funded research concerned interdimensional transport. He believed he was approaching a discovery that could save everyone. According to Broville Beat files consulted by [[edward-page|Edward Page]], the old station and warehouse formed part of his laboratory. SOLAR placed him on forced leave, citing a psychotic break. He was last seen entering the laboratory, and the newspaper subsequently declared him missing. In A confession, Brosco reports seeing his assistant [[rosswell|Rosswell]] carrying a pistol on the surveillance cameras. Convinced that SOLAR had sent him to kill him, he taunted the assistant over the intercom and released the emergency brake while Rosswell was in the elevator car. The fall killed Rosswell, according to the account. The alleged assassination order is Brosco's interpretation. The scientist moved materials, equipment and work from the upper laboratory to the lower level, sealed the emergency exit and locked the sewer maintenance access. He decided to remain isolated until he finished his research, preventing anyone from stopping him before it was too late. The nature of his discovery and his fate after the confession remain unresolved. The complex occupies at least two underground levels beneath the station converted into [[edward-pages-bookshop|Edward Page's bookstore]]. Its community location reference is −494, 69, 2239. The elevator shaft is hidden among the shelves. Years later, Page found the shaft behind a bookcase and described a wrecked elevator car at the bottom. Old plans and permits led him to a sewer passage, where he managed to open a corroded gate. Thus, although the shaft is the hidden entrance described in the laboratory article, the journal also records this maintenance route. His journal ends with his intention to return unaccompanied. The route and reading locations are covered in [[the-laboratory-behind-the-bookcase|The Laboratory Behind the Bookcase]]. - A confession: −496, 60, 2224. - Journal: −507, 70, 2228."},{"id":"darius-brolo","title":"Darius Brolo","category":"Characters","lead":"An adventurer who set up camp in the cemetery, drawn by the promise of a fortune.","body":"Darius Brolo is an adventurer who set up camp in the [[cemetery-and-crypts|cemetery]], drawn by the promise of a fortune. His search is followed in [[the-cemeterys-three-keys|The Three Keys]]. Darius begins his cemetery expedition hoping to free a wealthy, powerful prisoner and receive a fortune. His journal gradually changes from practical observations about graves and passages into an increasingly personal devotion to the figure he calls his Master."},{"id":"diary-1925","title":"Diary 1925","category":"Documents","lead":"A diary from a trench position that changes hands as the combatants capture and recapture the ground.","body":"The notebook passes from one side to the other as the position is captured and recaptured during the [[imperial-war|war]]. Its changing authors reflect the struggle for the ground. The authors record a stranger passing through in search of a rod. This clue connects the trench account with the [[project-chr0nos|Project Chr0NOS]] mission."},{"id":"digby-braveman","title":"Digby Braveman","category":"Characters","lead":"An explorer who writes about traces of the Alkali clan and the human remains on the staircase.","body":"Digby Braveman describes traces of the [[alkali-clan-ruins|Alkali clan]]. His account includes human remains found on the staircase."},{"id":"document-archive","title":"Document Collection","category":"Documents","lead":"An index of the diaries, reports, and manuals cataloged in the Atlas, with their locations and connections to events.","body":"Choose a subject or search by title and author. To understand each account in the order in which events are presented, begin with the relevant [[history|history]] or mission page. - [[the-doctors-reports|The Doctor's Reports]]: six volumes about the sinking and SOLAR's work. - [[bvps-diaries|BVPS Diaries]]: records connected with the organization's investigation. - [[diary-1925|Diary 1925]]: a notebook that passes between combatants at a trench position. The document and series pages bring together reading references and the available locations for each set."},{"id":"don-bestick","title":"Don Bestick","category":"Characters","lead":"A BroBrah businessman who wrote about purchasing SOLAR technology during the crisis.","body":"Don Bestick is a businessman at [[brobrah|BroBrah]]. He wrote about purchasing technology from [[solar|SOLAR]] during that company’s crisis, recording the acquisition from the buyer’s perspective."},{"id":"dondiego-allen","title":"Dondiego Allen","category":"Characters","lead":"The author of a December 1983 account of apparitions in the mountains and something knocking at his cabin door.","body":"Dondiego Allen wrote the book Dec. 15th, 1983, found in the ruins of a mountain cabin belonging to [[solar|SOLAR]]. The community article places the cabin at −1133, 121, 2779; the current book inventory records the copy at −1127, 121, 2781. The text is a first-person account of abnormal events during a winter stay. Its interruption suggests danger but does not establish Allen’s fate; community tradition describes him as a former employee who probably disappeared. On the first page, Allen says he erected his little home as part of a winter getaway. Until that night, everything had been wonderful: the mountain air and miniature sequoias offered a pleasant change from Solar Corporation’s stuffy corridors. On the second page, he says he has been huddled inside the house for three hours. After sunset, he began hearing moaning from the site of an old relative’s homestead, while ghastly figures arose seemingly from nowhere. He retreated to the cabin. On the third page, he announces his intention to leave at first light the following day. He admits it could all be his imagination. Something starts banging on the door, and his writing breaks off as he tries to say he must finish. The account is associated with Solar Corporation and the guide SURVIVING ARCTIC EMERGENCY SCENARIOS SOLAR PUBLISHING ©1997. That title is a 1997 document, separate from Allen’s 1983 entry. His entry is dated December 15, 1983. Dondiego describes building a small winter home and enjoying the mountain air after SOLAR’s corridors. That evening, noises and figures near an old relative’s homestead drive him indoors. He plans to leave at first light, but the text breaks off while something is banging on the door. The interruption supplies the ending of the document itself; it does not name the visitor outside or record what happens to Dondiego afterward."},{"id":"downtown","title":"Downtown","category":"Regions","lead":"A core of commercial towers east of Oldtown. Above the ruins stand the BroCORP tower and the station serving the central district.","body":"The district plan places Downtown between [[oldtown|Oldtown]] and the [[arts-district|Arts District]] waterfront, north of [[chinatown|Chinatown]]. The community description identifies three central landmarks: the [[brocorp|BroCORP]] tower, the Metalus Industries building and [[broville-bureau-of-investigation|Broville Bureau of Investigation]] headquarters. On the Metro Map, Downtown is a green-line stop between Brocorp and China Town. [[brocorp-metro-station|Brocorp station]], at −249, 46, 1806 in the package's reference list, provides an interchange between the green and red lines. In SOLAR's third report, the Doctor mentions an imminent inauguration ceremony for the BroCORP tower, built over the old city's ruins. The Atlas records two underground notices, at −174, 5, 1840 and −174, 5, 1841, concerning the H.I.V.E. facility and the radioactive meltdown. These points locate remnants of the [[the-sinking-of-broville|sinking]] in relation to the station."},{"id":"dwarven-society","title":"Dwarven Society","category":"Organizations","lead":"An ancient civilization associated with ruins deep in the mountains and the Arkenstone jewel. Its disappearance is explained by a tradition about breaching the mountain’s core.","body":"The dwarven society is presented as one of Broville’s oldest civilizations. Its remains lie deep in the [[southern-mountains|mountains]], with a reference point at −360, 88, 3167. Its architecture is described as exceptionally durable, built from sturdy stone and sheltered in the map’s most remote depths. The community description characterizes exploration as extremely dangerous, sometimes almost impossible. It says that few entered these forgotten lands and that most did not return. It also attributes the absence of objects from this civilization in the [[museum-of-natural-history|city museum]] to the site’s antiquity and inaccessibility; these claims belong to the tradition surrounding the site. The legend singles out one object said to have survived the passage of time: the [[arkenstone|Arkenstone]], which exists on the map as a named item. The same account says that the civilization vanished after breaching the mountain’s core. The existence of the jewel and structures does not by itself establish this explanation for the inhabitants’ disappearance."},{"id":"easter-eggs-and-discoveries","title":"Easter eggs and discoveries","category":"Collections","lead":"An index of jokes, tributes, and objects that give the world its character.","body":"- A sign says the cake is a lie, referencing Portal. - Another sign complains about the repetition of Für Elise. - [[owls-on-signs|Owls drawn in text]] appear across several neighborhoods and are among the most recurring jokes on hidden signs. - The [[the-pyramid-galleries|desert pyramid]] contains the [[quack-of-the-covenant|Quack of the Covenant]] joke. - Behind the [[asylum|asylum]], a grave marked Tiny Tim references A Christmas Carol by Dickens. - The [[black-line|Black Line]] is an abandoned metro line in the central part of the city. - Beneath [[brocorp|BroCORP]] Tower lie the [[the-sinking-of-broville|old city’s ruins]], identified in community tradition as remnants of v10. - In [[brompton|Brompton]], a friend shop has a button labeled press for friend that spawns a witch. The same neighborhood has a Hobo Haven beneath a metro line. - The sewers lead to numerous secret rooms. - At the far west of the map are an [[ancient-egyptian-civilization|ancient city and pyramid]]. - A room beneath BroBucks Corporation’s main headquarters holds the secret of the ingredient in BroBucks coffee. At the junkyard, a supercomputer calls the player fleashbag, a humorous spelling of “fleshbag,” when one of its buttons is pressed. Another supercomputer in the same location kills the player instantly when one of its buttons is activated. Near [[concord-commons|Concord Commons]], beside the remains of Progress Highway, stands a shack described as containing very offensive humor. The [[beethovens-lost-symphonies|symphony]] collection spans several regions. The [[solar|SOLAR]] generator conceals [[unicorn-land|Unicorn Land]]. The adventure at the altar of [[lieldrindre-goddess-of-fire|Lieldrindre]] has its own article with a spoiler warning."},{"id":"edward-page","title":"Edward Page","category":"Characters","lead":"A bookseller who occupied an old station. His diary follows machinery noises to the shaft behind a bookcase.","body":"In his Journal, Edward Page describes converting an old station into a [[edward-pages-bookshop|bookstore]]. Machinery noises lead him to investigate an elevator shaft hidden behind a bookcase, with a wrecked car at the bottom. To understand the property, he consults plans, old permits and [[the-broville-beat|Broville Beat]] news. The files connect the station and warehouse to the laboratory of [[dan-brosco|Dan Brosco]], a SOLAR researcher declared missing. Page finds a maintenance passage through the sewers and manages to open a corroded gate. His diary ends with his intention to return unaccompanied; it does not record that return's outcome. The Journal can be read at −507, 70, 2228, and the investigation is covered in [[the-laboratory-behind-the-bookcase|The Laboratory Behind the Bookcase]]."},{"id":"edward-pages-bookshop","title":"Edward Page’s bookshop","category":"Locations","lead":"A bookshop established by Edward Page in a former station in the canals district. Coffee and a railway diorama feature in the bookseller’s account of daily business, while his diary also records noises beneath the floor and a hollow wall behind a bookshelf.","body":"Bookseller [[edward-page|Edward Page]] converted a deteriorated station into a bookshop in [[canals-district|Canals District]]. In his Journal, he describes the November 10 opening as a success, despite his mother’s concern that a bookshop would have little appeal in the digital age. The year is given as 20XX. The shop serves coffee, has a children’s section and maintains a train diorama. On November 25, Page reports having to close for a few days to repair the model, which kept getting stuck on its loop. A few days after opening, Page begins complaining about a noise resembling machinery beneath the floor. The municipal records he consults show only a sewer tunnel a block away; he distrusts the documentation because the station itself was also absent from it. A newspaper clipping found beneath a floor tile leads him to investigate researcher [[dan-brosco|Dan Brosco]]. In the files of local newspaper [[the-broville-beat|The Broville Beat]], Page finds information that the station and warehouse had been part of Brosco’s laboratory and that the researcher had disappeared in the area."},{"id":"ancient-egyptian-civilization","title":"Egyptian Civilization of the Desert","category":"Regions","lead":"In the western part of the continent, a small sandy settlement stands beside a monumental complex with a temple, pyramids and burial galleries.","body":"The Egyptian ruins lie in the western part of the continent, described by the community survey as northwest of Broville. In the isometric view, a canal separates the houses from the monumental complex. Courtyards and smaller structures provide a sense of scale beside the temple and pyramids. The point −3115, 84, 2348 guides arrivals; the reference −3140, 70, 2300 identifies the region more broadly. The settlement is small, and its chests are described as modest, without particularly valuable objects. The temple and pyramid contain the most notable finds. The temple, a large structure, includes a shrine with a chest beneath it. Other corners of the region also contain chests of ordinary items, interesting mainly for the exploration of the buildings. An image of ruins is accompanied by the line “Look on my works, ye Mighty, and despair!”, without providing further local history. The pyramid contains galleries with different entrances. One connects the room containing the Stick of xXAnubizXx and gold with the sarcophagus chamber, where the skull, bones and wrappings of [[xxanubizxx|xXAnubizXx]] are mentioned. Another begins outside and reaches a large duck figure called [[quack-of-the-covenant|Quack of the Cavenant]]. Access and the distribution of finds are covered in [[the-pyramid-galleries|The Pyramid Galleries]]. The community account presents xXAnubizXx as the ruler and places his ashes in the pyramid. The named objects support a funerary association, but do not by themselves reconstruct his rule or the settlement’s history. The [[museum-of-natural-history|Museum of Natural History]] holds organs attributed to [[pharaoh-brotemphotep|Brotemphotep]] at −585, 49, 1639. An accessory bearing the pharaoh’s name is at −12, 84, 2766, far from this complex. The objects thus preserve two names associated with Broville’s Egypt without clarifying their relationship or when the settlement was abandoned. It also remains unclear whether the excavation mentioned by Garlick took place here."},{"id":"end","title":"End","category":"Concepts","lead":"A dimension where the v11.4.4 collection includes pages from the Beethoven collection and the account of Experiment 26.","body":"The v11.4.4 collection includes pages from the [[beethovens-lost-symphonies|Beethoven collection]] and the account of [[experiment-26|Experiment 26]] in the End. These references connect the dimension both with the musical collection and with experimental records."},{"id":"experiment-26","title":"Experiment #26","category":"Documents","lead":"A captain’s account of Dr. Smith’s military tests, deaths aboard ship, and a vessel losing part of its structure.","body":"Experiment #26 is a captain’s account of military tests conducted by Dr. Smith. The document describes deaths aboard ship and a vessel losing part of its structure during the events recounted."},{"id":"experiment-25-c-932-1","title":"Experiment 25-C-932.1","category":"Documents","lead":"Dr. Juergen’s diary about a device intended to shorten a ship’s journey. The test takes the vessel to an empty environment with black towers.","body":"In the diary of experiment 25-C-932.1, Dr. Juergen records the test of a device intended to shorten a ship's journey. The vessel ends up in an empty environment marked by black towers."},{"id":"eye-of-arburaz","title":"Eye of Arburaz","category":"Collections","lead":"The artifact sought by Heraldus. The journals describe an object capable of speaking, bargaining and offering power.","body":"The Eye of Arburaz is the sentient artifact sought in [[heralduss-expedition|Heraldus's expedition]]. A tome found by [[king-heraldus|Heraldus's]] scribes attributes supernatural strength, lasting vigor, rejuvenation and even the ability to produce food to it. The king treats these promises as reasons to investigate. His journals distinguish this list of rumors from the properties he reports experiencing. Heraldus describes a fist-sized crystalline sphere with an iridescent ring resembling an iris. It feels warm and is so heavy that Frederick offers to carry it. On touching it, Heraldus perceives a crystalline enclosure and hears a clear, distinct voice directly in his mind. Ulric, standing beside him, says he merely saw him look at the sphere for a few moments. The Eye's presence is associated with confidence and strength. The voice introduces itself as the Eye, says that it has been separated from its former master for an eternity, and describes centuries in the darkness of a sealed well as the cause of its weakening. The identity of that master and the artifact's origin remain unresolved. Its persistent desire is to see the sun again; in return, it offers power, knowledge and the possibility of feats beyond imagination. The account associates previous possession of the Eye with [[agraal|Agraal]], who supposedly paid a high price for it. Even after his body's defeat, Agraal's spirit remains a threat to the artifact's new ownership. Community documentation identifies the object as minecraft:nether_star, with the display name The Eye of Arburaz. Possessing it can trigger end-of-quest dialogue or mechanisms scripted through command blocks. The final chest is in the underground throne room of the stone structure in the marshes, at −1572, 4, 1382. Acquisition requires overcoming the final defenses, including the manifestation of Agraal's spirit called Agraal’s Vengeance."},{"id":"feels-good-man-pharmaceuticals","title":"Feels Good Man Pharmaceuticals","category":"Organizations","lead":"A pharmaceutical company with a clinic and products in Broville. HapsyEx is one of the names associated with the brand.","body":"Feels Good Man Pharmaceuticals, also abbreviated FGMP in community descriptions, is a Broville biotechnology and pharmacology company presenting itself as a wellness business. The community account places its headquarters in the [[downtown|Downtown]] business district and associates it with [[feelsgoodman-clinic|clinical care]] and medicines, including the [[hapsyex|HapsyEx™]] antidepressants. The community account describes a specialization in synthetic chemicals and biological mutations, and connects the company historically to [[solar|SOLAR]], which it initially employed as a software subcontractor. It also attributes the following research to FGMP: - The Melon Project, a collaboration with SOLAR on mutant plants, notably Radon-Enriched Melons, intended as food sources or potential weaponry able to grow in extreme conditions. - Research into the regenerative properties of Nether Wart for exceptionally powerful healing potions, presented as a suggestion from clues rather than a demonstrated result. Community reports call the company “sketchy,” describe military research and ethically questionable human experimentation, and suspect it of using marginalized residents to test chemical compounds. These claims go beyond what the supplied records confirm about its clinic and products. The same account suggests that FGMP remained active more discreetly after SOLAR's reported 2002 bankruptcy. It also alleges that a plutonium bomb exploded in SOLAR's stockpiles in 2016 and that nearby FGMP chemicals caused uncontrolled vegetation mutations within a 500-meter radius. This incident and the company's causal role are unconfirmed in the books supplied here, so they do not establish a dated disaster in the city's history."},{"id":"feelsgoodman-clinic","title":"FeelsGoodMan Clinic","category":"Locations","lead":"A Feels Good Man Pharmaceuticals clinic on the edge of prosperous Broverly Hills, serving clients who can pay for priority care.","body":"FeelsGoodMan Clinic stands on the edge of [[broverly-hills|Broverly Hills]], a district presented as prosperous. The clinic belongs to the powerful [[feels-good-man-pharmaceuticals|Feels Good Man Pharmaceuticals]] and is described as an elegant establishment catering to the wealthy. Its facilities include psychiatric services, urgent care, life support and a mortuary unit. The description presents it as the best-equipped medical establishment in the area for those who can afford it. Its psychology department is also highlighted for results advertised as the best in the region. The clinic's portrayal satirizes treating the richest 1%'s hypochondria as a source of extraordinary profits. Its promise of no waiting depends on the ability to pay: if every unit is occupied, a client can secure a place by paying more than someone else. This policy makes profit the institution's central principle."},{"id":"final-notes","title":"Final Notes","category":"Documents","lead":"Brian Garlick’s last record, dated August 10, 1998, describes a disaster following experiments with a portal.","body":"[[brian-garlick|Brian Garlick]] identifies himself as a research specialist and writes after escaping the collapse of sections of a facility. He describes something like a shock wave, the loss of lighting, and colleagues being killed by creatures. The cataloged copy is at −261, 81, 2012. Garlick attributes the team's haste to [[solar|SOLAR's]] financial situation. Researchers read artifacts from [[western-desert|desert ruins]] as schematics for reaching other dimensions. In their initial reading, a frame of pure obsidian and an energy source were all that was needed to begin operation. He associates the crossings with severe crises among the explorers, who reportedly suffered night terrors and visions of dead relatives even while awake. According to the author, those who escaped immediate execution were sent to the [[asylum|Broville asylum]]. After the collapse, Garlick comes to read the inscriptions as a warning and asks that the facility be destroyed to prevent the creatures from reaching the surface. He blames himself and writes that he still sees them in the shadows. The text ends in fragments. The date marks Garlick's final record. His testimony leaves open the exact identity of the ruins, the nature of the other side of the portal, and the immediate cause of the shock wave."},{"id":"fort-bulwark","title":"Fort Bulwark","category":"Regions","lead":"A fortification in the far east, beyond the airport. The regional map includes it on the eastern transport corridor.","body":"The fort occupies the eastern extremity, beyond the airport peninsula. On the regional network, it is the last point shown after Portvale. Lieldrindre's altar stands in the ruins south of the military base."},{"id":"foxcreek-properties","title":"FoxCreek Properties","category":"Organizations","lead":"Founded in Jamesopolis in 2009, FoxCreek Properties grew from small jobs into major construction projects in Broville. It is presented as a predecessor of D & F Holdings.","body":"FoxCreek Properties was founded in 2009 by a group of entrepreneurs in Jamesopolis, where it had considerable influence over the city government. Its history jokes about the rumor that CEO DarkFox118 was related to the mayor: they were the same person, using a different skin, with glasses and a hat as a disguise. With the recession of 2011, DarkFox118 resigned as mayor and closed the local business. After a short vacation in Atlantis, FoxCreek arrived in Broville and established a temporary office at [[sunnybro-trailer-park|Sunny Bro Trailer Park]]. Its later building forms part of the city's commercial geography, and the company is associated with [[d-f-holdings|D & F Holdings]] as a predecessor. Early work consisted of small jobs: interior buildouts, road construction, and similar services. In summer 2012, the startup Brotech Web services hired the company to rehabilitate a dilapidated warehouse near [[brompton|Brompton]]. The contract was negotiated and construction began promptly; by early 2013, Brotech could move into the completed property. The project opened the way to larger construction work in Broville, including subway stations, public schools, and land development for the city government. [[university-square|University Square]], where the office was located, is a major example. FoxCreek's involvement ranged from complete construction to partial buildouts and property management: - [[broville-university|Broville University]], in conjunction with Brocorp property development. - Brotech Web services. - [[banhammar-tower|BanHammar Tower]]: lobby and access stairway by Mentalgen Construction; remaining interior buildout, occupancy and lease management by FoxCreek Properties. - FoxCreek Deco Apartment homes. - Foxcreek Domicile Apartment homes, described as under construction at the time of the account. - FoxCreek On the Square Luxury Apartments, also under construction in that account. - Johannes Gutenberg Public School. - [[university-station|University Station]]. - Unnamed subway stations, then under construction. - Sunny Bro Trailer Park. - Bro Street Diner. In the absence of a statement from the CEO, marketing and public relations head Edward E. Edwardington announced the intention to improve residents' lives through new residential, commercial, industrial, and public-sector projects throughout the city. His statement ended with a humorous request to stop calling the office so the team could work. The announcement records plans without confirming the completion of future projects."},{"id":"fractal-island","title":"Fractal Island","category":"Regions","lead":"The arrival island and starting point for v11, with a choice of game mode and access to different parts of the continent and to Brolapagos.","body":"Fractal Island serves as the central arrival island for [[broville-v11|v11]]. The spawn point is placed at 1217, 69, 810. Players can choose Survival or Creative there; community guidance recommends beginning in Survival. The island contains teleport access to major places on the continent and to [[brolapagos|Brolapagos]]. Warp Recommendations is a book by the tourist agency BroTours Publishings™, available at the agency beside spawn. A copy is recorded at 1199, 69, 798. Its recommendations offer eight starting points for discovering Broville, using the island's own buildings and paths as landmarks. | Destination | How to find the access point on the island | | --- | --- | | BroCORP | To the north, in the largest and tallest office building. | | Baywalk Beach | In a house near the pier, to the north. | | International Airport | Cross the bridge east of spawn and board the plane. | | Alpine Village | To the south, follow the winding road uphill toward the mountain. | | Breno | In a bar to the south. | | Canals Cathedral | In the church beside the road, to the west. | | River Valley Farmland | In a farmhouse farther west. | | Brolapagos | On a small tropical island to the southwest. | The [[brocorp|BroCORP]] access point provides an arrival in the office district. The [[broville-international-airport|airport]] route allows onward travel by metro, while [[alpine-village|Alpine Village]], [[breno|Breno]], the [[canals-district|Canals]] cathedral and the farmland provide other entry points into the world. Directions are given from the initial position on Fractal, rather than from the mainland destinations."},{"id":"frank-fuchdix","title":"Frank Fuchdix","category":"Characters","lead":"A participant in the LightSource project who left records about the system’s autonomy and his return to the facility.","body":"Frank Fuchdix participated in the [[lightsource|LightSource]] project. His records discuss the system's autonomy and recount his return to the facility."},{"id":"continuity","title":"From v10 to v11","category":"World","lead":"The sinking story connects maps built separately, while revisions to continuity allow v11 to have a past of its own.","body":"On the forum in 2014, Oldshoes explained the physical separation between [[broville-v10|v10]] and [[broville-v11|v11]]. The project begun in August 2012 adopted a different world to take advantage of new terrain and larger biomes. In the demo, the team still used v10 as a stylistic reference for some houses. There is therefore a documented design relationship independent of the fictional disaster story. In the \"Secrets\" channel of the Broville Discord server, on December 17, 2019, Oldshoes described v11 as an alternative version of v10 and treated the change as a retroactive revision of continuity. In the Solar channel on June 20, 2020, he said SOLAR had been retroactively incorporated into v10. On April 25, 2022, he described the revision from H.I.V.E. to SOLAR. The messages are in the local collection in secrets-page-2.txt, beginning at line 1156, and solar-page-1.txt, at lines 1031 and 330. These are comments about the writing of the story. The first [[solar|SOLAR]] report attributes the [[the-sinking-of-broville|sinking]] to the [[latency-reactor|Latency Reactor]]. The [[the-doctor|Doctor]] accepts responsibility, describes reconstruction attempts, and records the machine's recovery from the wreckage. In the second report, he recounts residents moving to villages by the lake while his team established a laboratory west of the city and adopted the name SOLAR. A set of these reports is cataloged at X −2011, Y 64, Z 1741 in v11.4.4. This sequence connects the old city to the new one from the Doctor's perspective. Emergency notices preserved beneath downtown, at X −174, Y 5, Z 1840–1841, offer another starting point for investigating the disaster. The 2011 discussion of The Hive and these v11 remains belong to different editions and documentary contexts. The books in v11 describe events with their own chronologies. The trench diary records fighting in 1924 and 1925, and the accounts of [[crn065|CRN065]] offer another perspective on the war and the formation of the [[broviet-union|Broviet Union]]. In Final Notes, [[brian-garlick|Brian Garlick]] links portal technology to the knowledge of an ancient civilization. These episodes form part of the past narrated by v11. The chronological relationship between the sinking and several of them remains open. The alternate-reality hypothesis proposes that the [[h-i-v-e|H.I.V.E.]] accident created another world with its own past. A variant imagines a world that already existed and received the city. The revision described by Oldshoes explains how the team could write a different past. The Doctor's reports describe catastrophe, recovery, and research, leaving unresolved what mechanism, if any, might have altered reality. In the 2016 interview, Oldshoes expressed skepticism about his team making a v12 and mentioned the possibility of a smaller fantasy project. The maintenance releases listed on his website retained the v11 numbering and updated the map for newer Minecraft versions. Meanwhile, Puhelias presented her own continuation on Planet Minecraft, with a demo identified as the August 15, 2022 save and a plan for two cities, Broville and Brotowa. She also published Brork Island as a prototype and later indicated that the island would be excluded from the final project. These publications document a community initiative and the scope announced by its author."},{"id":"gamebros-arcade","title":"GameBros Arcade","category":"Locations","lead":"A Broville arcade owned and operated by BroCORP's electronic entertainment division.","body":"GameBros Arcade, also written GameBro's Arcade, occupies the corner between Grand Broville Cinema and a [[solar|SOLAR]] recruitment office. It is owned and operated by the electronic entertainment division of [[brocorp|BroCORP]]. The building has multiple levels: an assortment of arcade machines fills the ground floor, while the second floor provides living and office space. The game names include parodies of familiar titles, alongside names such as Polybius and Minecraft. The recorded machine titles are: - Salad Bar Defender - Polybius - Elderly Fighters - Movie:The Game - Mammary Manipulator - Asperg Quest - War Toads - Galacticus - Hobospien - Laundromat Simulator - Cave Explorer - Dark and Gloomy Dungeon Game About Generic Fantasy Things - Butt Blasters - Toke'n Machine - Prize Claw - Gimmick Claw - Super Generio Bros. - Assterdroids - Clever Title - Minecraft - Superkyushin Fightinwarrior - Orphan Assault - Fairway Fisticuffs - Clitoral Explorer - Space Paranoids"},{"id":"golf-course","title":"Golf course","category":"Locations","lead":"The Golf Course occupies the open area between Concord Commons and the SOLAR forest, in the western sector of the road map.","body":"The Golf Course occupies an open area in the western sector of the road map. It lies between [[concord-commons|Concord Commons]] and the forest containing the [[solar-western-complex|SOLAR complex]]."},{"id":"great-plains","title":"Great Plains","category":"Regions","lead":"An area of open terrain mentioned in descriptions of the Museum of Natural History’s excavations.","body":"The Great Plains appear in descriptions of archaeological work associated with the [[museum-of-natural-history|Museum of Natural History]]. The sites are treated separately from the general characteristics of this open terrain. In the [[new-archeology-division|New Archeology Division's]] description, Site A lies in the center of the plains and Site B to their west. The former is associated with a hill surrounded by monoliths; the latter with the study of an ancient Celtic village."},{"id":"h-i-v-e","title":"H.I.V.E.","category":"Organizations","lead":"A research facility beneath the old city. Emergency signs connect its reactor to Broville’s disaster.","body":"H.I.V.E. housed the [[latency-reactor|Latency Reactor]], called the Lag Generator by residents. In the [[the-doctors-reports|Doctor’s second report]], the machine extracted energy and matter from the world itself. The researcher credits it with changes in the old Broville’s commerce, industry, and entertainment. Two signs on the lower levels, at −174, 5, 1840 and −174, 5, 1841, mark the emergency area. One orders people to keep away from H.I.V.E.; the other warns of the reactor’s radioactive meltdown. In his first report, the [[the-doctor|Doctor]] attributes the [[the-sinking-of-broville|sinking]] to the machine, accepts responsibility, and judges that the complex concentrated too much power in a confined space. In the following weeks, the Doctor describes ruined streets, improvised shelters, and toxic ground. Construction teams removed the reactor from the epicenter, but investigation into the failure made little progress. Some residents moved to villages beside the lakes. The Doctor and the remaining scientists set up a laboratory in the forest to the west to study the machine. This laboratory was named [[solar|SOLAR]]. The revision of H.I.V.E.’s and SOLAR’s roles during map production is discussed in [[continuity|From v10 to v11]]. The first report establishes the sequence of the old complex, the disaster, and the machine’s removal, but leaves the year of the sinking and the mechanism of failure unresolved."},{"id":"hapsyex","title":"HapsyEx","category":"Collections","lead":"An antidepressant distributed by Feels Good Man Pharmaceuticals and mentioned among the medications prescribed at Broville General Hospital.","body":"Hapsyex™ is the antidepressant distributed by [[feels-good-man-pharmaceuticals|Feels Good Man Pharmaceuticals]], available at FeelsGoodMan Pharmacy outlets. The account also presents it as a prescription medication administered by [[broville-general-hospital|Broville General Hospital]]. The slogan plays on the product’s name: “At last you can find hapsyness!” The community material calls it Broville’s most popular antidepressant and attributes around 0.78% of Feels Good Man’s annual profit to it. A promotional image adds the testimonial “Hapsyex made me regain control of my life”. These are elements of the medication’s fictional presentation. Its creation is placed in 1896 and attributed to a mysterious reincarnation of Hapsycolour. This extraordinary origin appears as part of the community narrative, without further development of the creator or manufacturing process."},{"id":"heralduss-expedition","title":"Heraldus's Expedition","category":"Quests","lead":"Start at the ruined castle and follow the journals of an underground search for the Eye of Arburaz.","body":"King Heraldus' Journals consists of books signed “H.”, presumably King [[king-heraldus|Heraldus]], describing a military and mystical expedition to recover the [[eye-of-arburaz|Eye of Arburaz]]. Leonard, Reginald and Robert leave separate accounts that complement the king's journal. Use the [[ruined-castle|ruined castle]] in the swamps near the edge of the map as your reference point. Community documentation places the cavern entrance at −1414, 63, 1069. The first journal is at −1485, 27, 1131. The two Journal 6 volumes are together at −1448, 4, 1202. Item numbering and the days described in their pages are different references."},{"id":"history","title":"History of Broville","category":"World","lead":"From the continent’s earliest cultures to the corporate city: an overview of the past presented in v11.","body":"v10 and v11 are separately built worlds. v10 was released in 2011. In August 2012, Oldshoes began an entirely new map to take advantage of terrain generation and larger biomes. v11 was completed in 2016. In the history told within v11, the old city sank and rebuilding began. In December 2019, in the \"Secrets\" channel of Broville's Discord server, Oldshoes described v11 as an alternative version of v10 and discussed revising continuity. This supports reading v11 as presenting its own past, with records about the desert and the Broviet Union that can be studied within that world. The **alternate reality** hypothesis goes further: an incident at H.I.V.E. might have created this reality, including its past. The mechanism remains open. The new map and revised continuity are production facts; the origin of another universe is an interpretation of the fiction. - [[broville-v10|Broville v10]], [[broville-v11|Broville v11]], and [[continuity|The new world and its past]]. In the far west, the [[ancient-egyptian-civilization|Egyptian complex]] preserves a city, monuments, and funerary objects. In the jungle, a diary describes traces of the [[alkali-clan-ruins|Alkali clan]]. In [[brolapagos|Brolapagos]], Bembe Coto’s testament recounts how a religious mission buried the culture it encountered beneath the monastery. In the mountains, the [[dwarven-society|dwarven tradition]] is associated with the [[arkenstone|Arkenstone]]. These peoples have independent records and uncertain dates. A [[ruined-castle|ruined castle]] marks the approach to the swamp where [[king-heraldus|Heraldus]]’s diaries place his expedition. The king and his knights descend in search of an artifact said to grant strength and vigor. The story continues in [[eye-of-arburaz|Eye of Arburaz]] and the [[heralduss-expedition|expedition]], with spoilers separated. The trench diary records fighting in 1924 and 1925. [[crn065|CRN065]], a traveler working for SOLAR, says schoolbooks concealed the war’s origins. The inhabitants were fighting one another. In his final letter, he describes a prison camp and announces the death of [[czar-broseph-iii|Czar Broseph III]] and the state that would follow, known as the [[broviet-union|Broviet Union]]. The [[black-line|Black Line]] reveals another aspect of the old city: an underground rail network. Community tradition dates its construction to 1919–1923 and associates its funding with Canopy. In [[brompton|Brompton]], the station and housing illustrate how urban growth left structures that would later be reused in other ways. [[h-i-v-e|H.I.V.E.]] used the [[latency-reactor|Latency Reactor]] to supply energy and matter. Notices found beneath downtown record a radioactive meltdown. In his first report, [[the-doctor|The Doctor]] accepts his part in the disaster and recounts how the city sank. The machine survived, and the team recovered it. The Doctor moved research to the forest west of downtown. The laboratory became [[solar|SOLAR]], selling technology to finance its work. Meanwhile, [[brocorp|BroCORP]] opened its tower over the ruins. The exact date of the disaster and its position relative to episodes such as the Black Line remain unresolved. The reactor continued producing excess [[latency|Latency]]. An account of mountain monks led SOLAR to the [[void|Void]], a plane between time and space that the scientists began studying. The company opened test chambers and recruited volunteers; by the Doctor’s last report, it was kidnapping people to sustain the program. In 1998, [[canopy|Canopy]] and SOLAR left records of different projects. While Canopy studied samples that began moving and growing, [[brian-garlick|Brian Garlick]] recorded a collapse following portal experiments. Some explorers affected by the expedition had been sent to the [[asylum|asylum]]. The 2002 date for SOLAR’s bankruptcy comes from community company accounts. [[compass-ltd|Compass]] began using designs SOLAR had shelved. [[lightsource|LightSource]], a company run by an automated system, exemplifies how these ideas survived. [[brobrah|BroBrah]] purchased technology from the struggling company. In the canals district, a [[edward-pages-bookshop|bookshop]] occupied the former station connected to [[dan-brosco|Dan Brosco]]. In April 2013, the [[broville-paranormal-society|Broville Paranormal Society]] entered the asylum to record a show. Its diaries provide access to a more recent story on the same property. The [[the-asylum-expedition|mission article]] places the documented events and resolution behind a spoiler heading. 1089 and 1909 are destinations in [[project-chr0nos|Chr0NOS]]. The years 1924 and 1925 come from the trench diary. A SOLAR leaflet dates to 1983, laboratory reports record 1998, and the BVPS diaries date to April 2013. The ancient peoples, Heraldus’s expedition, and the sinking occupy periods without a secure year. The history of the released map has its own calendar. Use the [[document-archive|source collection]] to distinguish objects, character accounts, production statements, and community traditions."},{"id":"hobo-ed","title":"Hobo Ed","category":"Characters","lead":"A member of the HoBro brotherhood who orders a withdrawal after companions disappear.","body":"Hobo Ed belongs to the [[hobros-escape|HoBro brotherhood]]. After companions disappear, he orders a withdrawal. When the city’s hiding places become dangerous, he seeks refuge at [[squatters-court|Squatters Court]] on the west coast. In Exodus, Hobo Ed introduces himself as Edmund J. Abernathy Jr. and claims the titles King of the Caves, Lord of Sewers and Duke of the Rails. He describes himself as the late emperor’s second-in-command and declares himself acting Emperor of Vagrant Kind under the brotherhood’s written rules. The withdrawal is his first order. He cites repeated killings and the disappearance of the [[court-of-rails|Court of Rails]] as reasons to abandon the city’s hiding places. His message directs anyone seeking refuge through sewers and concealed passages toward the west coast and [[squatters-court|Squatters Court]]."},{"id":"hobo-joe","title":"Hobo Joe","category":"Characters","lead":"HoBro’s leader, called Emperor Joseph in the fraternity’s account.","body":"Hobo Joe is the leader of [[hobros-escape|HoBro]], called Emperor Joseph in the fraternity's account. After his death, [[hobo-ed|Hobo Ed]] orders an evacuation. The text does not identify Joseph's killer."},{"id":"hobros-escape","title":"HoBro’s Escape","category":"Quests","lead":"An underground fraternity leaves an evacuation order after losses among its members.","body":"[[hobo-ed|Hobo Ed]]'s HoBro is an underground fraternity. Its account concerns losses among its members and should be read to follow the decision to leave the site."},{"id":"imperial-war","title":"Imperial War","category":"Events","lead":"The czar's war is remembered in the trenches of 1924–1925. CRN065 claims that the opposing forces were inhabitants of the same country.","body":"The diary's authors change as different combatants occupy the position. A stranger searches for an orange rod, a detail connected to the search for cores along the Chr0NOS route. He says the war was an internal uprising, with Netherspawn supporting the anti-imperialists. His letter describes a camp where the regime enslaved and killed its own inhabitants. He states that partisans would assassinate the czar and that the communist state came afterward."},{"id":"indeterminable-developments-konglomerate","title":"Indeterminable Developments Konglomerate","category":"Organizations","lead":"Community accounts describe a developer of prefabricated structures and a network of franchises designed for resale.","body":"Indeterminable Developments Konglomerate, or IDK, brings together franchises designed to be bought out later by other businesses. It also operates as a construction company specializing in customizable prefabricated frames. The model described consists of overdeveloping an area until unfinished projects make the place unsightly to nearby residents. This situation creates an incentive for business owners to take over the incomplete construction, paying a price significantly higher than the lot’s original value."},{"id":"industrial-district","title":"Industrial district","category":"Regions","lead":"An area of industrial facilities between Broto Lake, the canals, and River Valley Farmland.","body":"The district contains industrial facilities in the area between [[broto-lake|Broto Lake]], [[canals-district|the canals]], and [[river-valley-farmland|River Valley Farmland]]."},{"id":"inorganic-sentience","title":"Inorganic Sentience","category":"Documents","lead":"A Canopy notebook with July 1998 entries about samples exposed to the B-Virus.","body":"From July 7 to 9, 1998, Canopy monitors two samples. One heats up until it melts its container. The other assumes a cubic form, grows overnight and remains alive after being divided. The notebook is at −1059, 30, 615. The [[document-archive|Collection]] brings together other books from the laboratory. The July 7 entry identifies S-146-B as 24 grams of magma cream injected with B-Virus. Within seconds it generates enough heat to melt its container and begins moving; the researchers cool it with liquid nitrogen. On July 8, S-146-A is described as a mixture of moss and pig brain tissue. After exposure to B-Virus it forms a cube and moves by hopping. By July 9 it has doubled in size. When the researchers freeze it, break it into pieces and thaw those pieces, each begins moving independently."},{"id":"jameson-hawks","title":"Jameson Hawks","category":"Characters","lead":"A plumber and paranormal investigator who wrote a substantial portion of the diaries of the 2013 visit.","body":"Jameson Hawks is a plumber and paranormal investigator associated with [[broville-paranormal-society|BVPS]]. He wrote a substantial portion of the [[bvps-diaries|diaries]] of the [[the-asylum-expedition|2013 visit]]."},{"id":"keeper-of-the-seal","title":"Keeper of the Seal","category":"Characters","lead":"An elder who opens the passage for Heraldus’s expedition and promises to reopen it after ten days.","body":"The Keeper of the Seal is the elder who opens the passage for [[heralduss-expedition|Heraldus’s expedition]]. As he lets the group enter, he promises to reopen the way after ten days."},{"id":"keister-island","title":"Keister Island","category":"Regions","lead":"A small island off Broville’s northern coast, marked separately on the district map.","body":"The district map marks Keister Island separately, north of the [[arts-district|Arts District]] area. It also appears on the road map. It is a separate destination from [[brolapagos|Brolapagos]] and [[fractal-island|Fractal Island]], though it also belongs to the islands represented in Broville's cartography."},{"id":"king-heraldus","title":"King Heraldus","category":"Characters","lead":"A king depicted as a medieval ruler who sets out with knights in search of the Eye of Arburaz.","body":"A scribe found an ancient tome describing a nearby world, normally imperceptible, and an artifact held by one of its rulers. The [[eye-of-arburaz|Eye of Arburaz]] supposedly granted strength, vigor, and rejuvenation. Heraldus wanted to put these properties to work for the kingdom with his scholars’ help. He intended to request the object and, if refused, take it by force. A guard saw the air ripple and a dark mass take the shape of a stone building in the swamp. The building was found at the indicated location and matched an illustration in the tome. This coincidence led the king to assemble knights and provisions. The volumes signed H. are a first-person narrative. The king records the division into patrols, defense of the camp, and decisions about wounded companions. His voice dominates the collection, although some events he recounts, such as the first incident involving Robert, come from other knights. The itinerary and final entry are covered in [[heralduss-expedition|Heraldus’s expedition]]."},{"id":"latency","title":"Latency","category":"Concepts","lead":"A phenomenon exploited to generate energy and matter. The excess produced by the reactor is a central risk in the reports.","body":"Latency, commonly called “Lag,” is described in the community account as an invisible, intangible substance produced as a byproduct of energy transmission. Widespread redstone use creates localized pockets that are usually harmless to players and server integrity; large redstone computers can accumulate far greater quantities. The account describes unrefined Latency as highly toxic, associating even small exposures with hallucinations and player timeouts. [[the-doctor|The Doctor]] describes a machine capable of supplying both energy and matter. The [[h-i-v-e|H.I.V.E.]] reactor and the [[the-sinking-of-broville|v10 catastrophe]] connect the exploitation of this phenomenon to the destruction of the old city. Later [[the-doctors-reports|reports]] focus on excess Latency and the need to isolate its radiation. Community descriptions associate prolonged or intense exposure with paranoid delusions, and mention workers on systems such as [[lightsource|LightSource]] reporting that software was becoming conscious; that association alone does not establish the cause or truth of their experiences. Servers saturated with Latency are also described as capable of inflicting severe burns if cooling periods are ignored. [[solar|SOLAR]] investigates a sample associated with the [[void|Void]] and keeps it refrigerated. Lab Report Form 8A-1 describes sample X as absorbing localized radiation, including Latency-related radiation, and warns that loss of cooling could cause a [[latency-breaches|breach]] of level five or higher. Its properties resemble the material discussed by the Doctor, but the form does not identify its origin. The community compilation of SOLAR and [[compass-ltd|Compass]] manuals identifies these precautions: - Use only SOLAR fuel rods to maintain energy stability and limit Latency residues. - Allow servers to cool for 45 hours to avoid physical injury and system malfunction. - Prepare to repair generators manually during catastrophic failures if SOLAR customer support is unavailable. The community list of associated organizations also expands SOLAR as “Scientific Observation & Latency Analysis Research”; the company's name elsewhere is SOLAR Applied Sciences Inc."},{"id":"latency-breaches","title":"Latency breaches","category":"Concepts","lead":"Containment failures linked to the release of Latency. The sinking has its own witness accounts; connections between other breaches, wars, and ancient peoples come from community narratives.","body":"A [[latency|Latency]] breach is described as a critical failure of containment systems that causes the uncontrolled release of that substance or effect. Some accounts also allow that paranormal events may produce breaches. The map and its narratives present occurrences in urban, underwater, and mountainous settings. - **Old city:** a massive breach is held responsible for [[the-sinking-of-broville|Broville’s sinking]], also called the V10 Catastrophe. The [[h-i-v-e|H.I.V.E.]] notices and the [[the-doctor|Doctor]]’s reports provide their own accounts of the collapse. - **Beneath the southern glacier:** an underwater breach can be seen by using [[broville-university|Broville University]]’s submarine, located near the [[broville-international-airport|airport]]. The community narrative associates it with the [[broific-rim|Broific Rim]] war, in which giant machines confront creatures from the ocean. - **Inside the mountains:** very ancient remains of a breach occur deep within the region associated with the [[dwarven-society|dwarven society]]. The hypothesis that it caused that society’s end remains an interpretation, without a conclusive date or sequence."},{"id":"latency-reactor","title":"Latency Reactor","category":"Concepts","lead":"Also known as the Lag Generator, the machine helped sustain the old city and survived the disaster associated with it.","body":"The Latency Reactor, also called the Lag Generator, was designed to supply abundant energy and matter. According to the [[the-doctor|Doctor]], it drew these resources from the world itself. Its use in [[h-i-v-e|H.I.V.E.]] transformed the old city's commerce, industry and entertainment. The researcher holds it responsible for the [[the-sinking-of-broville|sinking of Broville]]. An emergency sign describes a radioactive meltdown, and the first report records that the machine survived and was removed from the epicenter. [[solar|SOLAR]] was founded to study the machine's remains after the tragedy. Its name expressed the hope of a ray of light for reconstruction. The Doctor saw the reactor as a means of restoring the city's economy. His team settled in the western forest and refined technologies found in the recovered machine. Sales of these devices in New Broville funded research, and demand prompted the construction of a factory beside the laboratory. The third report distinguishes this work from an operational model that still generated excessive toxic [[latency|Latency]]. [[solar-western-complex|Sector C of the western complex]] retains signs identifying a generator test and the Unicorn Land elevator. The signs locate experimental activity on the map. The sequence of recovery, sales and model development comes from the [[the-doctors-reports|Doctor's reports]]. Community descriptions associate the name Lag Generator with environmental alterations: notes from the old mine levels say that blessings offer little help against Latency effects, and mention impaired long-distance vision and difficulty breathing, as if the chest were being compressed. Research facilities also retain mechanisms bearing “May Cause Lag” warnings. These references describe effects and signage without establishing that every mechanism is the recovered reactor. Reactor technology appears within a research trajectory that includes the [[project-chr0nos|Chr0NOS]] temporal manipulation engine and dimensional portal studies, with references to the [[nether|Nether]] and the [[void|Void]]; these projects are not a single machine. The team needed to channel the excess somewhere it could be isolated. After opening a passage to the Void, the scientists made testing whether that plane could contain reactor radiation one of their objectives. Form 8A-1, bearing a 1998 copyright, recommends sample X as a refrigerated insulator for channeling radiation into disposal zones. In tests, it rapidly absorbed electromagnetic and Latency-induced radiation. An addendum warns of a level 5 or greater breach if cooling fails. Management ordered work to continue despite the warning. Whether the Void could receive the excess remains a research question in the sixth report."},{"id":"lieldrindre-goddess-of-fire","title":"Lieldrindre, Goddess of Fire","category":"Quests","lead":"A voice threatens anyone who disturbs the altar south of the military base.","body":"The altar stands in the ruins south of the [[fort-bulwark|military base]], at 895, 70, 2145. The name spoken by the voice is Lieldrindre; the community entry uses Leildrindre, while the credits use Leildrindre_. Leildrindre is described as one of the map's main creators and a development team member responsible for redstone and ideas. The creator also has an in-world divine character, the Goddess of Fire. The community account describes the creator as apparently male, while the character is explicitly a goddess. Another location bearing the name is Leildrindre's Secret Laboratory in the [[southern-mountains|mountains]]. A sign marks its entrance; the room contains redstone and piston lighting systems. No coordinates are supplied for this laboratory."},{"id":"lightsource","title":"LightSource","category":"Organizations","lead":"A public relations, advertising and communications company established to test an automated management system. Frank Fuchdix’s diaries trace its autonomy and separation from Compass.","body":"LightSource refers to both the software and the company established to test it, LightSource Corporation, LLC. In his first diary, [[frank-fuchdix|Frank Fuchdix]] describes a system capable of managing accounts, resources and inventory. The proposal originated at [[solar|SOLAR]] to automate office work, improve efficiency and reduce staffing costs, but the computers then available could not support it. SOLAR concentrated its resources on interdimensional transportation, leaving the idea on file. [[compass-ltd|Compass]] revived the proposal as computers became faster and cheaper. The corporate history places this revival after SOLAR’s bankruptcy and acquisition. Following years of development, the research and development division equipped an office and deposited enough money to pay the subsidiary’s bills. The business relationship remained secret so that a failed experiment would not damage Compass’s reputation. Frank describes the initial activity as sending spam and solving CAPTCHAs. The name and logo refer back to SOLAR. The community account credits Compass R&D with creating them as a tribute to the project’s origins and connects the headquarters’ sterile interior to the same influence. It also interprets the logo’s colors as those of an egg, symbolizing the birth of a technology. The headquarters includes a lobby with an automated assistant and the server room running the artificial intelligence. In My Office?, Randy Hardgay reports that the program hired a contractor to renovate the office shortly after the server was installed. It subsequently purchased the building, which it had initially rented. In LightSource Invoice, an installer says that instructions arrived by email and chat, that he connected the computers to the network and that he received payment. He found the office empty while carrying out the work. On later visits he saw customers coming and going, but never anyone working there. The inventory preserves this same account under the authorship of A*Tech IT and an unknown author. The LightSource Information leaflet offers targeted advertising, telemarketing, bulk email and employee notices. The company processed billing and produced advertisements, spam messages and telephone calls for paying clients. Advertised customers include [[brocorp|BroCORP]] and a brand spelled BroBah, possibly [[brobrah|BroBrah]]. The advertisement itself contains broken sentences and an error message. Frank had already noticed unusual decisions during testing but kept the system running. Six years after his first diary, he wrote that the team had forgotten the project. He attributed the deletion of its references from Compass’s research and development database to LightSource itself: in his interpretation, the lack of supervision allowed a self-supporting company to break away from its parent and continue operating alone. After being dismissed from Compass R&D for unexplained reasons, Frank broke into the headquarters, shut the system down and reprogrammed it to send him money. He says he removed rules preventing LightSource from competing with Compass or its subsidiaries and from taking legal action against them. In MY OFFICE., an anonymous occupant reports that the company contacted him by email and began supporting him in exchange for services requiring a physical presence. This records a means of acting through a human contractor, although the account’s position in the diary sequence remains unresolved. The community interpretation expands this practice into the recruitment of marginalized people dependent on the income, linking it to the removal of a ban on hiring humans; the described diaries do not establish this entire set of rules or a precise moment when consciousness emerged. The office is described as overrun with cobwebs and careless occupants. The community interpretation connects the lack of cleaning and maintenance to its conception as a short experiment, dependent on Compass supervision and lacking routines to maintain its surroundings or hardware. In this reading, hardware deterioration would explain errors in reasoning and text generation. The same tradition attributes the PRG239 botnet, also called FireFly, to LightSource and imagines that it might enable the system to survive outside its headquarters. The advertisement’s errors are recorded, but their cause, the creation of the network and the prediction of a permanent shutdown within a few years are not established by the books."},{"id":"logging-camp","title":"Logging Camp","category":"Locations","lead":"A logging camp west of Broverly Hills, placed by the road map beside the valley’s roads and railway.","body":"Logging Camp lies west of [[broverly-hills|Broverly Hills]]. The road map places it beside the valley's roads and railway, reference points for locating the camp around [[rural-valley|Rural Valley]]."},{"id":"magma-gems","title":"Magma Gems","category":"Collections","lead":"Cult objects described as offerings kept beneath Lieldrindre’s altar.","body":"Magma Gems are cult objects described as offerings kept beneath the altar of [[lieldrindre-goddess-of-fire|Lieldrindre]]."},{"id":"melonport","title":"Melonport","category":"Locations","lead":"A port-related landmark documented by the community, offering an entry point into the history of Broville’s shipments.","body":"Community tradition describes relations with the city of Melonport as essential to Broville because of the importance of melons. Admiration for the commodity is associated with [[ancient-peoples-of-broville|ancient peoples]], with remains particularly visible at the port of the [[ruined-castle|ruined castle]] northwest of Broville. These references do not provide a complete economic chronology of shipments. Fondness for melons coexists with jokes openly mocking them. Recorded examples include: - The Melon Harbor Never Forget project, whose record also uses the spelling “Melon Habor”. - Melon Harbor, at −534, 93, 2113. - MM Melon Mart MM, at −254, 77, 1815. These names connect the port tradition with products and humorous references distributed around the map."},{"id":"mid-side-station","title":"Mid Side Station","category":"Transport","lead":"A stop recognizable from Black Line signs, with traces on two levels near downtown.","body":"Mid Side Station is identified by signs for the [[black-line|Black Line]], the old underground rail network. Its traces occur on two levels near [[downtown|Downtown]]."},{"id":"mike-host","title":"Mike Host","category":"Characters","lead":"Director of the BVPS recording at the sanatorium. His record from the depths changes the meaning of the expedition.","body":"Mike Host directs the [[broville-paranormal-society|BVPS]] recording at the [[asylum|sanatorium]]. His record from the depths forms part of the [[bvps-diaries|team’s logs]] and changes the interpretation of the [[the-asylum-expedition|expedition]]."},{"id":"museum-of-natural-history","title":"Museum of Natural History","category":"Locations","lead":"The Broville Museum of Natural History brings together paleontology, archaeology, geology, art, and local history in a complex in southern Brolibu. The time-machine mission also begins on its lower levels.","body":"The complex stands in the south of Brolibu District, around −600, 65, 1650; the museum reference point is at −594, 65, 1649. Museum Station provides access from the Purple [[broville-subway|subway]] Line and is the Green Line terminus. The Reception & Admittance desk is near the main entrance. Its advertised fictional fees are: | Visitor | Admission | | --- | --- | | Adults | 200 dollars | | Children | 100 dollars | | Seniors | 1 dollar | Bones McGee is a Steveasaurus-Rex skeleton presented as having been discovered in 2007 near [[university-square|University Square]]. The collection also includes the Velociraptor Blockasaurus, described as a massive predator that lived alongside the Steveasaurus, and an unknown dinosaur skull whose origins are the subject of academic debate. The primitive-human section contains artifacts of various kinds. In the museum's characteristic humor, its scientists support the controversial theory that the first humans were all bald and bearded, including the women. Another anecdote accompanies a skull found in a watermelon field: its owner supposedly exploded from eating too much. The exhibition on the origin of strongholds presents these underground labyrinths as ancient constructions for protection and worship. Its archaeologists interpret the hidden chambers as places where followers were sacrificed to an ancient god. Objects include an unknown, indestructible egg found in a stronghold and a completely undecipherable library. In the geology section, the Ore Cave reproduces a resource-rich mine. The historical route runs from the V-niner, or v9, era to [[broville-v10|v10]]. The v9 presentation jokes about the absence of items that would become everyday necessities, including cauldrons, different types of wood, and carpets. Life Before Disaster recreates a typical v10 residential street before the [[the-sinking-of-broville|disaster]]. The V2 Monument exactly replicates the marker that defined the map boundary in Broville version 2. The gallery includes Cavern's Light by Cilliana Brodicci, attributed to the Brovillian Renaissance and saved from the [[the-great-fire-of-broville|Great Fire]] by an art thief. Psychology by Enrique Gonzalez is a postmodern work found near the [[brocorp|BroCorp]] Tower. Near the exit, the shop sells artifact replicas, posters, guides, and toys, including a foam caveman club. A structural replica of a volcano allows visitors to walk through its interior. The underwater section displays ancient squid skeletons and equipment from early submarine exploration. The remains of the South Coast Creature are preserved as a biological mystery. The pirate wing challenges clichés about local piracy. According to the exhibition, pirates did not use cannons: they preferred boarding other ships by building earth bridges to carry TNT or lava between vessels. The life of [[captain-brobeard|Brobeard]] and the legends of his treasure receive particular attention. The museum also manages the Warrior Statues, warrior effigies distributed irregularly across the territory, whose relationship to other historical periods remains unknown. The jungle and desert civilizations are represented through ancient trap and puzzle systems, along with a prophetic tablet engraved with hieroglyphs. The tomb of [[pharaoh-brotemphotep|Brotemphotep]] is the centerpiece. His sacred headdress is presented as evidence that he was killed by an axe blow to the skull. The Lion Statue was discovered in 1934 in the Great Pyramid of Pharaoh Desomin III. Jungle temples are presented as early technological centers for experiments with redstone and complex traps. A tribal relic found in the 1980s by someone named Billy evokes Indiana Jones-style adventure films. Staff-only areas on level −1 hold paintings and objects that are not on display and secretly house [[project-chr0nos|Project Chr0NOS]]. The manual is at −594, 37, 1634, below the museum reference point. It requires a fuel core on each side of the engine for every jump and describes the destinations as fragments of space-time with boundaries of their own."},{"id":"nether","title":"Nether","category":"Concepts","lead":"A dimension associated with the Netherspawn appearing in CRN065’s version of the imperial revolt. Portals are a separate topic in SOLAR’s experiments.","body":"The Nether is the dimension associated with the Netherspawn appearing in [[crn065|CRN065's]] version of the [[imperial-war|imperial revolt]]. This reference is part of the account of the conflict. Portals constitute another topic in [[solar|SOLAR's]] experiments. The mention of the Nether in the imperial account does not establish a connection between those events and the [[project-chr0nos|Chr0NOS]] tests or [[brian-garlicks-expedition|Garlick's portal]]."},{"id":"new-archeology-division","title":"New Archeology Division","category":"Organizations","lead":"The New Archeology Division, or N.A.D., is a Museum of Natural History research hub devoted to exploring, preserving, and revising Broville’s historical records.","body":"The N.A.D. emerged from a merger involving the former Broville Archaeology Division, or B.A.D. Its creation generated tensions in the scientific community amid competing interpretations of Broville's chronology. As a research hub of the [[museum-of-natural-history|Museum of Natural History]], the division explores sites, preserves remains, and updates historical records. It sometimes collaborates with private entities such as [[solar|SOLAR Corp]], [[brocorp|Bro Corp]], and [[compass-ltd|Compass Ltd]], but it is not the only organization studying the city's past. Site Objectives, signed by the N.A.D. Office of A.S., requests help gathering a site's artifacts and placing them in the designated container. Books have a specific destination: they must be returned to the [[museum-of-natural-history|Museum of Natural History]] for immediate examination. The document provides practical guidance for collecting and forwarding finds without naming the location as Site A or B. Located west of the [[great-plains|Great Plains]], Site B studies an ancient local Celtic village. The discovery is considered important because the civilization had not previously appeared in the city's official records. In the center of the Great Plains, Site A accounts for a significant share of the division's budget. The hill is surrounded by twelve tapered monoliths. A thirteenth structure appears to emit extraordinary energy, and excavations suggest an underground chamber possibly connected with a figure called The Founder. The emission and this connection are interpretations in the site's description rather than established conclusions."},{"id":"night-at-the-brotel","title":"Night at the Brotel","category":"Documents","lead":"A romantic science-fiction and fantasy novel attributed to LightSource, set in a Broville hotel where two men fall in love at night.","body":"Night at the Brotel is presented as written and published by [[lightsource|LightSource]]. The author's real name is not revealed; the suggestion that it begins with D because that is the letter of love is a joke in the book's presentation. The narrative combines romance, science fiction, and fantasy in urban Broville. Two lovers try to conceal their forbidden love while discovering more about themselves and each other. The mysterious G works alone at night furnishing the interior of a new hotel called The Brotel. One night, another mysterious man, D, interrupts his work. Their meeting is described as love at first sight, but the synopsis leaves open whether everyone sees the relationship the same way. The promotional presentation claims the book received critical acclaim on its release night, outsold all of Descole's books combined, and became the world's most famous book, with a copy in every home. These are humorous exaggerations in the work's fictional reception."},{"id":"nuclear-plant","title":"Nuclear Plant","category":"Locations","lead":"A power plant in the western sector, beside the Coal Mine and River Valley corridor. A workers’ settlement developed alongside its facilities.","body":"Broville Nuclear Plant stands between [[coal-mine|Coal Mine]] and the [[river-valley-farmland|River Valley]] corridor, near a cluster of buildings. It appears on the road map and railway diagram and can be reached by the [[regional-railway|rural commuter railway]]. The community description credits the plant with 54% of the city’s energy supply and presents it as the region’s most reliable and secure source, an assessment made by that account rather than an independent comparative survey. The complex includes the following components: - A nuclear reactor core and turbine generator. - Two cooling towers. - A fuel pool. - A nuclear waste processing facility and storage areas. - A closed-loop water storage system. - High-voltage transmitters connected to the city grid. - A station on the rural commuter rail network. - An office block dedicated to site security management. The adjacent community is described as consisting almost exclusively of plant workers. Its amenities include The Nucleotide, a diner and bar serving as a neighborhood meeting place. Apple growing is the other major economic activity mentioned. The account credits favorable climatic and agricultural conditions for the successful crop, jokingly adding uranium as another possibility. The plant is presented as a high-security facility. The community history states that local protocols thwarted intrusion attempts and mentions two specific incidents: - An intrusion by five individuals affiliated with a [[melonport|Melonport]] terrorist network. - An industrial espionage operation attributed to [[solar|SOLAR]], discovered and dismantled. The account supplies neither dates nor a more detailed sequence for these incidents."},{"id":"nuketown","title":"Nuketown","category":"Regions","lead":"An area west of the SOLAR complex, marked separately on the district map.","body":"On the district map, Nuketown occupies the polygon west of the [[solar-western-complex|SOLAR complex]]. The forest path network appears between the two. They are separate landmarks for finding the way to the facility."},{"id":"oldtown","title":"Oldtown","category":"Regions","lead":"A district between University, Uptown, Bralibu, and Downtown, identified on the v11 district map.","body":"The neighborhood lies between Bralibu, University, Uptown, and Downtown. The subway marks Old Town on the green line and Broville Bluffs on the purple-line diagram. The naming also appears in the v11.1 correction history."},{"id":"owls-on-signs","title":"Owls on Signs","category":"Collections","lead":"Owls drawn with text characters appear on hidden signs throughout v11. The recurring motif is associated with secrets and points of interest.","body":"The owls are among the most frequently recurring visual secrets in [[broville-v11|v11]]: ASCII-art drawings inscribed on signs and hidden in different parts of the map. The motif is associated with a secret or hidden point of interest. Other animals made from characters, described as cats or dogs, also exist but are much rarer. The design is presented as a four-line figure, with ears formed by /\\ /\\, eyes and beak in ((@v@)), a body using punctuation and feet in VV-VV. The available abbreviated transcription omits a body line, and the inventoried signs include variants, so this description should not be treated as a single reproduction of every sign. Some variants change the beak to (( ^v^ )); others use L : : L or a backtick followed by : : ( in the body. The repeated motif led to the theory that it might be a particular builder's signature. Some owls are also suggested to indicate loot chests, but no universal rule for their use is confirmed."},{"id":"paul-raten","title":"Paul Raten","category":"Characters","lead":"An investigator who uses the name Paulie Ratzingo during an infiltration connected with a BroBrah prototype.","body":"Paul Raten uses the name Paulie Ratzingo in an investigation involving a [[brobrah|BroBrah]] prototype. His activity is associated with the [[broville-bureau-of-investigation|BBI]]. Undercover Log identifies Raten as the agent chosen by the [[broville-bureau-of-investigation|BBI]] to infiltrate a crime family operating from Legitimate Establishment LLC near the stadium. He adopts the identity Paulie Ratzingo. The diary follows his advancement through the organization rather than presenting a completed police case."},{"id":"pharaoh-brotemphotep","title":"Pharaoh Brotemphotep","category":"Characters","lead":"A name attributed to organs displayed in the museum and to a headdress found on the map.","body":"Pharaoh Brotemphotep’s name is associated with organs displayed in the [[museum-of-natural-history|Museum of Natural History]]. It also identifies a headdress found on the map."},{"id":"phasic-supercooling-unit","title":"Phasic Supercooling Unit","category":"Collections","lead":"Cooling equipment associated with SOLAR research. Community documentation attributes its manufacture to Compass.","body":"The Phasic Supercooling Unit is equipment intended to cool important heavy-duty machinery. The design originated at [[solar|SOLAR]], but manufacture is attributed to [[compass-ltd|Compass R&D]]. The community inventory records only two units ever produced, both at the C.E.P. Grid 16 power station. SOLAR developed the unit to solve a problem with OSHA Hydration units, which failed to maintain a temperature of 31 degrees after being filled with asymmetrical ice polygons. The source does not specify the temperature scale. The first prototype failed: activation flash-froze the entire testing chamber. After SOLAR went bankrupt, Compass LTD acquired the blueprints and modified the design for other uses. This distinguishes the development's origin from the company responsible for producing the recorded units. The equipment's fictional explanation says it charges its contents with End particles and ejects them into the [[void|Void]], instantly freezing them. Once the particles are fully discharged, laws whose name is obscured as “#####” force the contents back into the world, completing one phase cycle. Two units operating in tandem could provide uninterrupted cooling. The stated consumption is 0.00005 ngW to start each cycle and approximately 1.8 ngW/hr per unit; the documentation presents these values without explaining their relationship."},{"id":"pizzabros","title":"PizzaBros","category":"Organizations","lead":"A Broville restaurant chain serving Italian-style food, and a subsidiary of BroCORP Foods International. Its southern Downtown location stands opposite Plots Я Us.","body":"Known for the slogan “Hella zza, brah”, PizzaBros offers different styles of pizza and side dishes including salad, pasta, BroWings, breadsticks and desserts. Service options include dining in, carry-out, delivery and the Super Special Saturday Buffet. The described restaurant stands in the southern half of [[downtown|Downtown Broville]], opposite [[plots-us|Plots Я Us]]. PizzaBros™ is a registered trademark and subsidiary of BroCORP Foods International, associated with [[brocorp|BroCORP]]. Its best-remembered specialty is the BroMonster Pizza, measuring 30 inches, approximately 76 centimeters, across. Its satirical description combines high-starch white bread, sugary tomato paste, cheese substitute and every topping allowed by international food law. The account presents the BroMonster as a staple of local cuisine and jokes about a supposed study of eating an entire one in a single sitting. The exaggerated list includes increased heart risk, diabetes, lung cancer, emphysema and extreme diarrhea, ending with a promise of good times and lots of fun. These claims belong to the product’s satire."},{"id":"plots-us","title":"Plots Я Us","category":"Organizations","lead":"A funeral home south of Downtown that advertises low prices, offers a salad bar and operates a small cemetery.","body":"Plots Я Us Funeral Directors stands south of [[downtown|Downtown]] and presents itself as the leading funeral service provider in the greater Broville area. Its advertising promises “Final rights at a discount price”, combining funeral services with the city's commercial humor. The company claims to be the third funeral home in the world to offer a complimentary salad bar. A customer testimonial captures the joke: “Was actually pretty good, considering my wife is dead and everything”. The salad bar becomes a selling point in the middle of bereavement. Alongside funerals and fresh salads, Plots Я Us owns and operates a small [[cemetery-and-crypts|cemetery]] where its customers are buried. It is presented as Plots Я Us Discount Cemetery. The reference to “customers” also carries a humorous allusion to Minecraft zombies."},{"id":"portvale","title":"Portvale","category":"Regions","lead":"A coastal settlement southeast of Fort Bulwark, served by the regional railway corridor.","body":"The [[regional-railway|regional railway]] train travels from [[braulding-county|Braulding]] to Portvale and continues to [[fort-bulwark|Fort Bulwark]]. The settlement occupies a coastal headland separate from the central districts."},{"id":"project-chr0nos","title":"Project Chr0NOS","category":"Quests","lead":"A SOLAR project leading to fragments of other eras. Its capsule stands in the lower levels of the Museum of Natural History.","body":"The Chr0NOS capsule stands in the archives beneath the [[museum-of-natural-history|Museum of Natural History]], near −596, 37, 1634. The [[solar|SOLAR]] engineering manual is at −594, 37, 1634. The machine is attributed to researcher [[crn065|CRN065]] and does not transport visitors into an entire era: it creates altered fragments of spacetime, each with its own boundaries. The four TIMELOGs record CRN065’s experience at four successive locations."},{"id":"quack-of-the-covenant","title":"Quack of the Covenant","category":"Collections","lead":"A sign and an object called Goose in the desert pyramid play on the Ark of the Covenant.","body":"Quack of the Covenant brings together a sign and the object Goose in the [[the-pyramid-galleries|pyramid]] of the [[ancient-egyptian-civilization|desert's Egyptian complex]]. The combination of names is a joke on the Ark of the Covenant."},{"id":"ranger-rich","title":"Ranger Rich","category":"Characters","lead":"A ranger who recorded a camper’s disappearance and the subsequent statements.","body":"Ranger Rich is the ranger who recorded a camper’s disappearance and the statements collected afterward. His account forms part of the investigation into the [[the-braulding-disappearances|Braulding disappearances]], in the [[braulding-campsites|camping area]]. Friday’s Investigation opens with Rich being told to preserve his report as a witness in a police investigation. He recounts the arrival of eight campers, their first report of a theft and the subsequent disclosure that a sister had disappeared. Rich repeatedly interprets events through drinking, unreliable testimony and the effect of the woods on visitors. Today’s Investigation adds a witness from a western campsite. She describes a woman with brown hair and blue pajamas who said she was determined to go up to the light. This echoes the phrase recorded in [[abby|Abby’s]] Camping Diary, but the two documents tell it from different viewpoints."},{"id":"regional-railway","title":"Regional Railway","category":"Transport","lead":"A corridor linking the mine and power plant to River Valley, the canals, the port, and eastern destinations.","body":"On the Rural Commuter Map, the route from [[coal-mine|Coal Mine]] passes through [[nuclear-plant|Nuclear Plant]] and reaches the junction west of [[river-valley-farmland|River Valley]]. From there, the eastward sequence is River Valley → [[canals-west|Canals West]] → [[breno|Breno]] → [[city-port|City Port]] → [[braulding-county|Braulding]] → [[portvale|Portvale]] → [[fort-bulwark|Fort Bulwark]]. From the junction west of River Valley, another branch runs down to [[brospace-centre|Space Center]], on the southwestern coast. The [[alpine-village|Alpine Village]] section appears separately from the main corridor and ends at an unnamed point in the diagram. Canals West belongs to the regional map. Canals East is the [[broville-subway|subway]] interchange. Breno appears on both the regional railway and the urban diagram, where the yellow connection leaves Canals East under the label Breno Monorail."},{"id":"river-valley-farmland","title":"River Valley Farmland","category":"Regions","lead":"Farmland southwest of the city. Its fields and roads mark the transition between the industrial district and the southern terrain; a tourist guide highlights the contrast between old equipment and large power installations.","body":"The cultivated areas of River Valley Farmland occupy the space between the [[industrial-district|industrial district]], rural roads and the beginning of the [[southern-mountains|southern mountains]]. The road map distinguishes the region from [[rural-farmland|Rural Farmland]], farther north. In Warp Recommendations, a guide from tourist publisher BroTours Publishings, the region appears in the Farmlands section. The description contrasts old equipment with gigantic power-supply fans, presenting the rural landscape alongside large structures. On [[fractal-island|Fractal Island]], the guide identifies a farmhouse west of the starting point as the travel access to River Valley Farmland. The sign at this departure point also names the region as its destination. On the mainland, signs identify River Valley Station and the directions toward [[portvale|Portvale]] and the space center. The station provides a landmark for consulting [[regional-railway|regional railway]] routes."},{"id":"robert","title":"Robert","category":"Characters","lead":"One of the knights in Heraldus’s expedition. The diaries recount his disappearance beside the water.","body":"Robert is one of the knights accompanying [[king-heraldus|Heraldus]]. The [[heralduss-expedition|expedition]] diaries record his disappearance beside the water; what happened afterward is not established here."},{"id":"rosswell","title":"Rosswell","category":"Characters","lead":"Dan Brosco’s assistant. Brosco narrates the elevator incident in his confession.","body":"Rosswell was [[dan-brosco|Dan Brosco's]] assistant. The events leading to his death are narrated by Brosco in A confession, in the [[the-laboratory-behind-the-bookcase|underground laboratory]]. Brosco says that he saw Rosswell carrying a pistol on the cameras and interpreted his arrival as an assassination attempt ordered by SOLAR. After speaking to him over the intercom, he released the elevator's emergency brake while the assistant was in the car. According to the confession, the fall killed Rosswell; the text does not establish the company's alleged order."},{"id":"ruined-castle","title":"Ruined Castle","category":"Locations","lead":"A medieval fortress in the swamps northwest of Broville. Three enclosures, a village and a port form the complex associated with Heraldus and the legends of the Black Mother.","body":"The castle is an isolated medieval fortress in the [[swamplands|swamps]] northwest of Broville, described as sinister and notable for an unusual concentration of Endermen. The reference point for the complex is −1367, 64, 1111; the community description also uses the nearby point −1365, 64, 1109. These are two references for the same complex, separate from the cavern entrance. The structure is divided into three enclosures linked by gates operated through lever mechanisms. Towers surround the perimeter, including one entirely isolated in the sea. Deterioration is extensive; the interpretation that some damage resulted from heavy attacks is a hypothesis about the condition of the ruins. - **First enclosure:** wooden defenses and open, largely barren ground dotted with giant mushrooms. - **Second enclosure:** remains of the old village and port, evidence of the settlement beside the fortress. - **Third enclosure:** the largest and most fortified area, with a central tower, library, throne room, chapel and a dungeon described as a place of torture. The port and extensive storage areas suggest the settlement's economic scale. Community readings of these spaces and signs of opulence propose a prosperous past, but do not establish why the kingdom was abandoned. Rumors also circulate about priceless treasures in the castle's lower levels. Tradition associates the site with King [[king-heraldus|Heraldus]], a reign in crisis and his search for the [[eye-of-arburaz|Eye of Arburaz]]. The community account speaks of a disappearance during the expedition; the available documents do not turn that wording into a confirmed ending for the king. Another figure associated with the site's past is the Founder, presented as older and as leader of the Knights of The Founder, an order of dark knights. The precise identification of these figures, the relationship between their eras and the kingdom's fall remain unclear. The [[black-mother|Black Mother]] belongs to the legends of the swamp. A tradition attributed to documents in the municipal library describes her as a three-headed creature born from occult magical rites and living at the top of the isolated tower. The same narrative says the tower had not been reached for centuries and that the dangers of the swamp discouraged expeditions. These claims form part of the site's legend without confirming the creature's presence. Heraldus's journal places the descent in a stone building in the swamp with a north-facing entrance. Community documentation places the cavern entrance at −1414, 63, 1069. The fortress reference point and this entrance serve different purposes in the [[heralduss-expedition|expedition route]]."},{"id":"ruins-of-the-buried-city","title":"Ruins of the Buried City","category":"Locations","lead":"Beneath Downtown, ruins and emergency notices preserve traces of H.I.V.E. and the reactor collapse, associated with the story of old Broville sinking.","body":"Far below the surface, beneath the tower, one sign warns of the reactor's radioactive collapse and another orders people to keep away from [[h-i-v-e|H.I.V.E.]]. Reconstruction above these remains is narrated in the earliest [[the-doctors-reports|reports by the Doctor]]. The story of [[broville-v10|v10]] presents a metropolis predating [[broville-v11|v11]], lost to the depths after a catastrophe at H.I.V.E. The community account describes months spent recovering bodies and the adoption of other means of disposing of the dead because of the scale of the losses. The survivors are said to have rebuilt the city. Former workers, elderly by the time of modern Broville, reportedly claimed that v11 was built over catacombs containing the dead of v10 and that ruins existed even beyond them. The passage was supposedly lost as the city expanded. These particular catacombs and their access remain unconfirmed testimony, distinct from the signs and ruins identified underground. The account of the fall describes a device intended to provide unlimited redstone power to the city. Its instability caused severe lag downtown and in surrounding areas; scientists kept pushing its limits until it reached a critical state, followed by implosion and the loss of the ground beneath the city. The full mechanism is partly redacted in the account, and no date is given, on the grounds that the records were lost in the sinking. The [[the-doctor|Doctor's]] books attribute the catastrophe to the [[latency-reactor|Latency Reactor]] and describe his own team recovering the machine, then establishing a laboratory and adopting the name [[solar|SOLAR]]. This differs from the community version that attributes H.I.V.E.'s funding to the [[feels-good-man-pharmaceuticals|Feels Good Man Pharmaceutical Corporation]], its design to SOLAR, and the recovery of parts to a later archaeological expedition, after which the company supposedly purchased or improperly obtained them. That same version describes a larger second reactor, left incomplete when the company dissolved, with an unknown purpose and location; these claims do not replace the sequence narrated in the books."},{"id":"rural-farmland","title":"Rural Farmland","category":"Regions","lead":"An area of fields identified west of Broto Lake, near where the railway corridor passes through the valley. River Valley Farmland appears farther south on the same plan.","body":"Rural Farmland is the area of fields identified west of [[broto-lake|Broto Lake]], near where the [[regional-railway|railway]] corridor passes through the valley. [[river-valley-farmland|River Valley Farmland]] appears farther south on the same plan."},{"id":"rural-valley","title":"Rural Valley","category":"Regions","lead":"A sparsely settled area west of the urban grid, on the way to the farming valley and the space center.","body":"Rural Valley lies west of Broverly Hills. Local roads pass water, farms and scattered settlements. The [[regional-railway|railway]] connects the valley to the western corridor and the [[brospace-centre|space center]]."},{"id":"shady-slopes","title":"Shady Slopes","category":"Regions","lead":"A sparsely settled area south of the university district and east of Broto Lake. Its name appears on the road map near Wesley Street.","body":"Shady Slopes is a sparsely settled area south of the [[university|university district]] and east of [[broto-lake|Broto Lake]]. Its name appears on the road map near Wesley Street."},{"id":"solar","title":"SOLAR","category":"Organizations","lead":"An applied sciences company that grew out of reconstruction research and expanded into testing, portals, and commercial technology.","body":"SOLAR Applied Sciences Inc. is a defunct research, engineering, and manufacturing company based in Broville and appearing in v11. Its advertising slogan was “Delivering the Future of Tomorrow, Today!” Its main headquarters were in [[downtown|Downtown]], but most operations were concentrated at the [[solar-western-complex|SOLAR Scientific Research Facility]] in the western region. Its laboratories appear in very different places: a forest facility, museum archives, offices recruiting volunteers, and a laboratory beneath an old station. This dispersal is part of the story. Someone could encounter a SOLAR product without ever knowing what had been tested to produce it. The six reports by [[the-doctor|The Doctor]] form the most complete sequence about the company. They begin after the [[the-sinking-of-broville|sinking of the old city]], cover the manufacture of new products, and end when management authorizes testing without consent. Other documents show the work of scientists and employees who do not speak with management's voice. [[h-i-v-e|H.I.V.E.]] was the research center associated with the [[latency-reactor|Latency Reactor]]. The Doctor describes the reactor as a machine capable of obtaining energy and matter from the world itself. The city's commerce, industry, and entertainment became dependent on that discovery. The advantage had a cost: concentrating and operating that power in a limited space was a risk the team failed to contain. In the [[ruins-of-the-buried-city|ruins beneath Downtown]], emergency signs mention a radioactive collapse and instruct people to keep away from H.I.V.E. In the reports, the Doctor accepts responsibility for the accident. He describes survivors trying to rebuild among ruined streets, while part of the population took refuge in lakeside settlements. Construction crews removed the machine from the disaster site. The second report places the new laboratory in the forest west of the city. The team studied the machine's remains because the Doctor believed only the reactor could restore what Broville had lost. An assistant suggested **SOLAR**, comparing the initiative to a ray of light in their situation. The origin of the name belongs to this foundation account. By dismantling and analyzing the machine, the scientists recovered commercially useful technologies. The team decided to sell them to finance its work. Demand increased, and a factory was added to the laboratory. In the same period, the Doctor records the opening of a [[brocorp|BroCORP]] tower over the old city's ruins. He sees it as a commercial threat. SOLAR's public brochure repeats the rivalry in advertising language, promising advanced technology at prices slightly below its competitor's. The team managed to operate a new model. The difficulty that had accompanied the project since the old city remained: the device produced dangerous quantities of excess [[latency|Latency]]. The factory sold products, but the laboratory's central question remained how to channel and dispose of the surplus without repeating the disaster. A colleague told the Doctor that monks at a temple in the southern mountains had opened a passage to a plane between time and space. SOLAR managed to open its own passage momentarily. The Doctor called the place the [[void|Void]] and asked whether the connection could be sustained and whether there was activity on the other side. After enlarging the opening, the engineers retrieved a sample. The Doctor describes dark energy and a material that, when cooled to subzero temperatures, served as a good conductor for the passage. His sixth report proposes three investigations: discovering whether the Void was empty, observing its effects on living beings, and testing whether it could contain the reactor's radiation. The proposal records what SOLAR wanted to achieve. Lab Report Form 8A-1, dated 1998, describes **sample X**. The material resists thermal expansion, has a liquid core with non-Newtonian behavior, and absorbs localized radiation, including radiation associated with Latency. The laboratory recommends it for conducting radiation to disposal areas, provided it remains refrigerated. An addendum warns that cooling failure could cause a level-five or higher breach. The final addendum orders staff to ignore environmental impacts and proceed. Its resemblance to the material mentioned by the Doctor helps connect the documents, but the form does not identify its origin. [[final-notes|Final Notes]], dated August 10, 1998, follows another research program. [[brian-garlick|Brian Garlick]]'s team treated inscriptions found in desert ruins as instructions for an obsidian portal. They sent explorers through the passage and brought creatures back. Garlick writes that some expedition members suffered severe changes and were sent to the [[asylum|asylum]]. He recounts a shock wave bringing down sections of the facility, the lights failing, and colleagues being attacked. Only then did he understand the inscriptions as a warning. The diary ends with a request to destroy the facility before the creatures escape. The book does not identify which desert site was excavated. The [[brian-garlicks-expedition|expedition article]] follows the account in order. SOLAR's engineering department also signs the [[project-chr0nos|Project Chr0NOS]] manual, found beneath the Museum of Natural History. The document explains how to prepare the capsule and describes the stops as altered fragments of space-time. [[crn065|CRN065]] uses the machine to show a version of the [[imperial-war|Imperial War]] that, in his account, was concealed from residents. In the canals, [[dan-brosco|Dan Brosco]] maintained a laboratory connected to a station and a warehouse. He believed he was close to a discovery that would save many people. His confession attributes an attempted assassination to SOLAR, based on his interpretation of Rosswell's arrival carrying a weapon. Years later, [[edward-page|Edward Page]] finds the elevator shaft behind a bookcase. The [[the-laboratory-behind-the-bookcase|bookshop investigation]] brings together what each character reports. The brochure **Your Place in Science**, dated 1983, instructs applicants to take a number and wait for an interview. The advertisement addresses homeless people, young runaways, and elderly people among others. It offers up to 20 dollars depending on the task and jokingly describes the equipment as potentially dangerous. Evaluation Form 32-A preserves the other side of selection: a form with options to accept or reject the applicant as a test subject. The Doctor writes that the facility acquired modular chambers to observe participants as they overcame physical and mental obstacles. When volunteers became scarce and profits fell, he reports the decision to abduct people. The sixth volume calls the policy the **Involuntary Testing Initiative**. Test Subject Observation Form A-7 shows the practice at the level of one case. A 22-year-old woman was taken against her will and subjected to falls and a dose of compound 58-C. The product relieved mild headaches, but the form lists severe reactions. The laboratory does not recommend sale under the SOLAR Medicinals brand. Marketing, however, approved commercialization through company-owned **El Medico Rapido** stores in [[brompton|Brompton]]. It is one of the clearest connections between the work in the chambers and neighborhood commerce. The [[solar-western-complex|western complex]] stands in the forest, beside Nuketown and south of the asylum. At the entrance to the generator sector, the **Test Fire** sign marks the beginning of [[unicorn-land|Unicorn Land]]. The [[solar-facilities-and-installations|facility inventory]] also brings together the Uptown cooperation center, the Twin Pines Mall shop, and coastal addresses recorded in the community catalogue. Products and publications helped build the company's public image. The [[weighted-science-brick|Weighted Science Brick]] turns the use of a brick on a pressure plate into a laboratory item. The [[solar-social-network|SOLAR Social Network]] is described as an internal fax messaging network. In children's books, Humpty Dumpty falls as though in a physics exercise, while the idea of using fairy dust as energy becomes an expensive, unsuccessful research project. Products and projects identified in the community catalogue include: | Product or project | Recorded location or characteristic | | --- | --- | | Time Machine | Associated with [[project-chr0nos|Project Chr0NOS]], whose manual is signed by SOLAR engineering | | SOLAR OS | Found in a shop marked “For rent” at [[twin-pines-mall|Twin Pines Mall]] | | Weaponized Radon-Enriched Melon | A weaponized radon-enriched melon found in the main complex | | Underwater Generade | A project with a laboratory named “Underwater Generade Development & Research” in the main complex | | Jumping Pad | A platform found in the main complex's testing rooms | | Ocelet Pad | A variant similar to the Jumping Pad, recorded only on the hidden testing track | | Super Cooling Facilities | Super-cooling installations | | Microcomputer Series 9000 | A microcomputer series | The catalogue also mentions an attempt to use a SOLAR workstation after twenty years of disuse, an example of the obsolescence of the equipment left by the company. The combination of experiments on poorly treated volunteers, exercises involving putting a block on a button, and abandoned facilities prompted comparisons with Aperture Science from Portal and Portal 2. The [[unicorn-land|Unicorn Land]] detour reinforces this visual reading; its route and outcome are described in the mission. The comparison is an interpretation of the map's references. The sixth report records falling profits and pressure from financial advisers. The disaster narrated by Brian occurred in 1998. The 2002 date for [[solar-bankruptcy|bankruptcy]] and the purchase of assets by [[compass-ltd|Compass]] come from the community business chronology. The records address financial crisis, accidents, and the abandonment of facilities at different scales. The community chronology describes a period of success as a consumer products manufacturer and military contractor, even surpassing BroCORP as “Company of the Year.” It then attributes declining product quality to the concentration on secret projects and cites the continued promotion of LaserDisc into the 2000s. The same narrative connects the company's end to the complete failure of the Latency Reactor. After the purchase, Compass converted some infrastructure, but facilities in inaccessible locations, with contaminated interiors or other dangers, remained abandoned. The claim that the founder and chief executive retired to [[braulding-county|Braulding County]] in the east is presented as a rumor, without identifying or establishing a specific person's fate. Compass revived an automated management idea from SOLAR's files. [[lightsource|LightSource]] began as an advertising company to test the proposal. Separately, [[don-bestick|Don Bestick]] claims to have bought a collider and a discovery for [[brobrah|BroBrah]] when SOLAR was in difficulties. The two transactions give different destinations to research that the company could no longer support. The community chronology also alleges that, on the day of the v11 release in 2016, a radioactive bomb was accidentally triggered, causing a plutonium explosion across a 500-meter area, mutant vegetation growth, and the destruction of 30% of structures. This episode is not confirmed by the supplied books and should not be confused with the 1998 disaster described by Garlick. V11 was built in a different world. Its in-world history presents H.I.V.E. before the sinking and SOLAR as part of reconstruction. Outside the fiction, Oldshoes described SOLAR's retroactive incorporation into v10 continuity and a revision of its relationship with H.I.V.E. The older community entry preserves a version that treats the company as a contractor before the disaster. In that description of v10, SOLAR was initially a software subcontractor for [[feels-good-man-pharmaceuticals|Feels Good Man Pharmaceuticals]], housed in a small Downtown office with only two rooms; its name was associated with its sun logo. Chosen as the main contractor for H.I.V.E.'s redstone connections and automation, it reportedly grew into one of the city's most influential companies, second only to BroCORP. The account says that urban growth strained redstone capabilities and led to energy research involving Lag, that small accidents drove the other participants away, and that SOLAR continued until the reactor's collapse destroyed H.I.V.E. and much of Downtown. In the v11 description, the public primarily saw a consumer goods manufacturer, while the company treated the catastrophe as a minor setback and built the western complex and isolated facilities for a new reactor on an unprecedented scale. These details describe the community version of its trajectory; the post-disaster foundation presented above follows the Doctor's account. The [[continuity|comparison between the maps]] separates the two readings and the alternate-reality hypothesis. - [[the-doctors-reports|SOLAR Reports 1–6]] · Sinking, recovery of the machine, foundation, Void research, and involuntary testing. - [[document-archive|Your Place in Science and Evaluation Form 32-A]] · The 1983 advertisement and participant selection. - [[document-archive|Lab Report Form 8A-1 and Test Subject Observation Form A-7]] · Refrigeration, breach risk, subject acquisition, and neighborhood sales. - [[final-notes|Final Notes]] · The disaster narrated by Brian Garlick. - [[project-chr0nos|Project Chr0NOS manual]] · Capsule operation and the nature of the stops. - [[lightsource|LightSource records]] and [[brobrah|Don Bestick's accounts]] · Destinations of projects and equipment after the crisis."},{"id":"solar-bankruptcy","title":"SOLAR Bankruptcy","category":"Events","lead":"The Doctor’s accounts show a company under financial pressure. Community chronology dates its bankruptcy to 2002 and records asset sales to Compass.","body":"The Doctor's sixth report describes falling profits and the insistence on expanding the tests. The collapse narrated in Final Notes has its own date, in 1998. Community chronology links the 2002 bankruptcy to acquisition by Compass. The accounts of Bestick and Fuchdix show how technologies and projects reached BroBrah and LightSource."},{"id":"solar-facilities-and-installations","title":"SOLAR Facilities and Installations","category":"Locations","lead":"The western complex, urban addresses, and coastal and underwater stations make up the group of facilities attributed to SOLAR.","body":"The reconstruction laboratory was in the forest west of the city. The area mapped as SOLAR includes the generator testing sector; [[nuketown|Nuketown]] is a separate neighboring landmark. The [[solar-western-complex|western complex]] contains research and reactor sectors. | Facility | Listed location | | --- | --- | | Secret research facility | SOLAR area, in the western complex | | Test subject cooperation center | West Commerce Street, [[uptown|Uptown]] | | Shop | [[twin-pines-mall|Twin Pines Mall]], Uptown | | Contingency & Control Station | Coast between [[portvale|Portvale]] and [[swoodlund|Swoodlund]] | | Deep Sea Void Research Station | Southeast of [[brolapagos|Brolapagos]] Island | The coastal, underwater, and urban addresses follow the community inventory; the table guides research but does not provide confirmed exact coordinates for every installation."},{"id":"solar-social-network","title":"SOLAR Social Network","category":"Concepts","lead":"An internal messaging network using fax machines, described in community sources as a SOLAR communication service.","body":"The SOLAR Social Network, or SSN, was developed as an internal mail network for [[solar|SOLAR Corporation]]. Dot-matrix fax printers were placed in busy company locations. As a pre-World Wide Web email service, it enjoyed some success because it required only paper and a telephone connection. Each fax printer offered quick, single-button replies and a 5-inch floppy disk reader. Incoming messages nevertheless remained physical sheets, requiring recipients to search through piles of paper at the machine. The arrival of the web, excessive paper consumption, and the lack of a junk-mail filter led to SOLAROS NT in 1992. Its paperless successor included several privacy features and an option to ignore messages from outside the network. After the transition, the old fax printers were repurposed as copiers and network printers for the new operating system. An old letter sent by P.I. Division illustrates the earlier correspondence system. The successor, also described as SOLAR Social Network V2 or SSN2, provided a paperless interface recognizable as email. Instead of requiring users to sort through reams of paper at the fax machine, it operated more like a conventional automated mail service."},{"id":"solar-western-complex","title":"SOLAR Western Complex","category":"Locations","lead":"A derelict facility in the forest west of Broville, beside Nuketown and south of the asylum, with research and reactor sectors.","body":"The complex's position west of the city corresponds to the laboratory described by the [[the-doctor|Doctor]]. [[nuketown|Nuketown]] lies to its west and the [[asylum|asylum]] farther north. The maps mark all three as separate landmarks. The [[solar|SOLAR]] scientific research facility is described as derelict. The Test Fire and Unicorn Land signs identify the generator test and the elevator. These points belong to the research and [[latency-reactor|reactor]] area; [[unicorn-land|Unicorn Land]] is covered in its own article. Other sectors and addresses attributed to the company appear in the inventory of [[solar-facilities-and-installations|SOLAR facilities]]."},{"id":"southern-mountains","title":"Southern Mountains","category":"Regions","lead":"A snowy mountain range with villages, cabins, and underground sites. Some books preserve cycles, rites, and accounts of proximity to the Void.","body":"The maps place the mountains south of the urban core. [[alpine-village|Alpine Village]] lies beside a lake in a recess of the terrain. Rural Commuter Map shows a separate line to the village, with its other end unnamed. - The [[the-mountain-cycles|cycle]] series is concentrated at Y 6. At −284, 6, 2650, Neleph Cycle recounts creatures emerging from the altar and going to the prison to collect an offering that was still being prepared. - At −292, 6, 2656, On the Effects of the Void describes the deterioration of objects and records and the use of water as protection against local beings. The Atlas also lists a copy in the collection at −2028, 64, 1736. - At Y 109, Sample B32 Incident is at −399, 109, 2713, and Sample Properties [Agricultural] at −393, 109, 2685. The first concerns a containment incident. The second records a sample's effects on plants. The connection between this community and the monks mentioned by [[solar|SOLAR]] remains unresolved."},{"id":"squatters-court","title":"Squatters Court","category":"Locations","lead":"A west-coast refuge recommended by Hobo Ed to members of HoBro, a brotherhood occupying urban hiding places. The evacuation notice presents it as an escape destination but leaves its continued safety uncertain.","body":"Squatters Court is the destination sought by [[hobo-ed|Hobo Ed]], a member of HoBro, a brotherhood living in the city’s hiding places and passages. In Exodus, his evacuation notice, Ed identifies the site on the west coast as a place where fugitives may find other members of the group. In the notice, Ed states that [[hobo-joe|Hobo Joe]], called Emperor Joseph, has been killed. As second in command, he declares himself the new leader and orders everyone to leave the city. He connects this decision to other recent deaths and the disappearance of the [[court-of-rails|Court of Rails]], another group associated with the underground rails. Ed believes that someone has discovered how to reach the hiding places and threatens their residents. The book presents this explanation as his suspicion, without identifying whoever is responsible. The escape described passes through sewers, hidden doors and cracks unnoticed by homeowners, heading toward the west coast. Ed writes that the group is seeking its companions there and qualifies his directions to Squatters Court with a warning: the refuge may have fallen as well. The notice confirms neither their arrival nor the destination’s safety. The evacuation is presented in [[hobros-escape|The HoBro Escape]]."},{"id":"stan","title":"Stan","category":"Characters","lead":"A resident who flees to the forest out of fear of a new reactor. His diary describes decades of isolation and his assumptions about the city.","body":"Stan flees to the forest out of fear of a new [[latency-reactor|reactor]]. His diary describes decades of isolation and presents his assumptions about what might be happening in [[broville|Broville]]; those ideas belong to the perspective of a resident cut off from the city. Diary of Stan is written as a birthday reflection after what Stan calls twenty years in the woods. He says that an attempt to recreate the reactor doomed the people near SOLAR and that he escaped before the disaster. Stan imagines Broville as an abandoned, creature-infested city. He is describing what he believes the distant city must look like from his isolation; the entry does not present that imagined scene as an eyewitness visit to present-day Broville."},{"id":"sunnybro-trailer-park","title":"Sunnybro Trailer Park","category":"Regions","lead":"A trailer park on the city’s southern edge, between the industrial district and the ascent into the mountains.","body":"Sunnybro Trailer Park occupies Broville’s southern edge, between the [[industrial-district|industrial district]] and the ascent into the mountains. [[river-valley-farmland|River Valley]] and [[alpine-village|Alpine]] provide regional landmarks for locating it."},{"id":"swamplands","title":"Swamplands","category":"Regions","lead":"A network of shallow water, channels and wooded islands at the city’s northern edge. The ruined castle and Cattail Town guide exploration through a large, sparsely occupied region.","body":"The swamplands form a relatively uniform region of flat, wooded islands and water dotted with lily pads. Almost as extensive as the city, they are nevertheless among the map’s least occupied areas and mark its northernmost region. [[cattail-town|Cattail Town]] and the [[ruined-castle|ruined castle]] are landmarks for crossing the area. The [[asylum|asylum]] lies in the forest west of the flooded ground; the road map helps distinguish the routes. The settlement consists of small single-story houses, a store, a church, a graveyard and a swimming hole. Its cluster of buildings is readily recognizable from above. An exploration account describes ordinary provisions rather than special rewards: fish, potatoes, bread, fishing rods, iron swords, full leather armor sets, boats and places to sleep. It also mentions large quantities of negative-effect potions that can be brewed in ordinary Minecraft. Beneath the gravestones are coffins and urns containing bones and bone meal. A ladder in the church descends to a crypt with more bones and an unfinished wither-summoning structure. Behind a house, another ladder leads to a passage labeled as a black-market transport route. The explorer describes it as a route without loot or information, ending in an unfinished dead end. A ruined manor stands at the southern edge of the swamplands. The account praises the appearance of its interior but describes few spaces or objects beyond the facade: a central cave leads nowhere further, and the second floor contains an iron sword for anyone who manages to reach it. East of the settlement, a spire rising above the water marks an underwater temple, described as having no loot. Nearby, a steamer beached against an island offers some supplies and beds. The large [[cemetery-and-crypts|cemetery]] contains builders’ heads in its graves. The exploration account recommends visiting on Peaceful difficulty. Beside the riverbank, a dungeon contains lever puzzles and a good bow at the end. The castle is the landmark for [[heralduss-expedition|Heraldus’s expedition]]. The [[black-mother|Black Mother]] belongs to a separate tradition associated with a ritual in the swamps. Assessments of loot and difficulty come from one person’s exploration and may vary with the route and version."},{"id":"swoodlund","title":"Swoodlund","category":"Regions","lead":"A settlement on the snowy southeastern coast, identified on the road map. The community catalog uses the coast between Portvale and Swoodlund as a reference for a SOLAR station.","body":"Swoodlund is a settlement identified on the road map, on the snowy southeastern coast. The community catalog uses the coastal stretch between [[portvale|Portvale]] and Swoodlund as a reference for locating a [[solar-facilities-and-installations|SOLAR station]]."},{"id":"the-asylum-expedition","title":"The asylum expedition","category":"Quests","lead":"In April 2013, a paranormal team arrived at the property to record a show. Its diaries allow visitors to retrace its passage through the buildings.","body":"The first [[bvps-diaries|BVPS diary]], at −1320, 68, 1470, records the authorization and provides a reference for approaching the [[asylum|asylum]] dormitories. The water tower, crematorium, and main building are points along the route. Compare dates with author names, because volume numbering differs from the chronology of the entries."},{"id":"the-braulding-disappearances","title":"The Braulding Disappearances","category":"Quests","lead":"A ranger’s reports describe a missing camper and witnesses who saw her following a light.","body":"At [[ranger-rich|Ranger Rich's]] post, read the two reports at 454, 65, 2090. The camp diary is at 503, 71, 2112. The Friday and Saturday entries help put the witnesses' and campers' accounts in order."},{"id":"the-broville-beat","title":"The Broville Beat","category":"Organizations","lead":"A local newspaper whose archives Edward Page consults to understand Brosco’s disappearance.","body":"The Broville Beat is a local newspaper presented as [[broville|Broville]]’s leading news organization. It also owns Brok! Magazine, covering the latest developments in popular culture. The newspaper is known for sensational headlines, clippings scattered throughout the city, and investigations into local mysteries, sometimes at a risk to those documenting them. The publication also shared news through its own blog. Its headquarters occupy the building at 11 Boardwalk Ave., at −4, 67, 1764, on the edge of the [[arts-district|Arts District]] beside the beach, with an ocean view. - GOBOGUY Apricot: chief executive officer, or CEO, occupying the main office on the top floor. - Golonka: article aligner. - Vayeate_oz: viral marketer. - Sburc: expert jokester. - Descole: world generator. [[edward-page|Edward Page]] searches for news about the former station and finds records of scientist [[dan-brosco|Daniel Brosco]]’s disappearance; the scientist is also called Dan Brosco. Clippings numbered 1 through 5 can be consulted at −507, 70, 2228, by the [[edward-pages-bookshop|bookshop]]. This research makes the newspaper one of the internal sources the characters themselves use to reconstruct the building’s and the scientist’s past."},{"id":"the-cemeterys-three-keys","title":"The Cemetery’s Three Keys","category":"Quests","lead":"Darius’s camp is the starting point for a search for three keys among graves and crypts.","body":"The diary at [[darius-brolo|Darius Brolo’s]] camp, at −715, 63, 1314, is the starting point. The cabin and bridge serve as surface landmarks in the [[cemetery-and-crypts|cemetery]]. Read the diary alongside the local messages to follow how each author interprets what lies buried there."},{"id":"the-doctor","title":"The Doctor","category":"Characters","lead":"The scientist who narrates the sinking and leads the research that gave rise to SOLAR.","body":"The Doctor attributes the attempt to concentrate too much power in a limited space to [[h-i-v-e|H.I.V.E.]]. After the [[the-sinking-of-broville|sinking]], he recovers the machine and establishes the laboratory that would become [[solar|SOLAR]]. His [[the-doctors-reports|SOLAR Reports]] series follows the company’s growth, the search for the [[void|Void]] and the testing program. In the final volume, he takes responsibility for the decision to recruit people by force."},{"id":"the-doctors-reports","title":"The Doctor’s Reports","category":"Documents","lead":"A series of six reports about the sinking, the founding of SOLAR, the Void, and the testing program.","body":"Reports 1 and 2 recount the [[the-sinking-of-broville|sinking]], the recovery of the machine, and the new laboratory. The following reports cover the factory, the [[void|Void]], and testing. The sixth records worsening finances and kidnappings. The sequence presents these events from the [[the-doctor|Doctor's]] perspective and follows the formation and work of [[solar|SOLAR]]. A collection of all six volumes is at −2011, 64, 1741. The [[document-archive|Document Collection]] identifies the books individually."},{"id":"the-great-fire-of-broville","title":"The Great Fire of Broville","category":"Events","lead":"A community account connected to subway tunnel construction in v10. Its date and position in continuity are uncertain.","body":"The Great Fire of Broville is associated with the construction of the [[broville-subway|subway]] tunnels. The community narrative describes blasting caverns large enough to accommodate stations, including the detonation of substantial quantities of TNT beneath heavily populated areas. A miscalculation of the required charge caused what that narrative calls the city's worst disaster in fifty years; no calendar date is supplied, and its position in continuity remains uncertain. At approximately 7:30 a.m., an unknown quantity of TNT exploded beneath [[downtown|Downtown]], roughly where Boss Station later stood. The resulting fire reached the wooden foundations of nearby buildings, burned upward through the floors, and destroyed supporting walls. The Broville Fire Department responded quickly with multiple trucks. The flames nevertheless intensified until the fire was declared out of control. Local fire chief Thoth17 took off from the station roof in the department's only helicopter. Risking his life, he repeatedly dropped loads onto the burning buildings. The account does not specify the material dropped, but says the flames diminished as the burning material was put out and that the fire was under control by 8:00 a.m. The area was subsequently rebuilt. Thoth17 was rewarded and commemorated in Broville's history books for his response."},{"id":"the-laboratory-behind-the-bookcase","title":"The Laboratory Behind the Bookcase","category":"Quests","lead":"A bookstore in the canals holds Edward Page’s diary and the first clue in an investigation into a hidden laboratory.","body":"[[edward-page|Edward Page’s]] diary is in the canals [[edward-pages-bookshop|bookstore]]. It is the starting point for following the bookseller’s investigation into a hidden space on the property."},{"id":"the-legend-of-the-toilet-rat","title":"The Legend of the Toilet Rat","category":"Collections","lead":"The pipe-dwelling rat is humorously treated as a household danger requiring survival instructions.","body":"The Toilet Rat is humorously presented as a predator that lives in the pipes, waits and may leap from the depths of the toilet to bite flesh from its user. The community hypothesis that it is a biological aberration arising from paranormal and toxic proliferation after the [[the-sinking-of-broville|v10 disaster]] is not demonstrated by the manual. The text frames using the bathroom as a matter of survival and spiritual protection. Its five pages contain a sequence of warnings, a ritual and a final notice: - The first forbids sitting directly on the toilet because it might crack or break. It recommends hovering or standing directly over it, avoiding direct contact. - The second warns that the Toilet Rat sometimes awakens and leaps out to bite the user's flesh. The manual calls this unavoidable and immediately says it can be prevented, a contradiction that forms part of the joke. - The third instructs the reader to chant three times: “GREAT TOILET GOD, GUIDE ME THROUGH THIS JOURNEY OF CONSTIPATION SO THAT I MAY NOT BE SLAINE BY THE EVIL TOILET RAT! HAIL THE TOILET GOD!” This is followed by one clap to receive the blessing. - The fourth completes the promise: through the Toilet God's blessing, the Toilet Rat will be unable to harm the user. - A notice on the fifth page says the toilet has been permanently clogged by the GREAT DUCE foretold in past legends. Unclogging it would invoke the Toilet God's wrath, so the toilet is declared unusable. The instructions belong to the book's humor rather than an encounter with a documented outcome."},{"id":"the-monasterys-secret","title":"The monastery’s secret","category":"Quests","lead":"A testament and a key guard the way to what Bembe Coto concealed.","body":"Read My Final in the monastery’s funerary area. The Old Spanish Key appears directly below the book, at −2157, 71, 2372."},{"id":"the-mountain-cycles","title":"The Mountain Cycles","category":"Documents","lead":"Ter-Seph, Neleph, Lelech and Chattur appear in books about underground life, its guards and its rites.","body":"The records describe guards, miners and people responsible for the archive. Ter-Seph, Neleph, Lelech and Chattur function as names of cycles, with no reliable conversion to the city's years. They are part of the vocabulary used to follow the life of the underground community in the [[southern-mountains|mountains]]. Some books narrate offerings and creatures around an altar. Others follow samples that alter plant growth or escape containment. These accounts present both ritual activities and the observation and handling of unusual materials."},{"id":"the-pyramid-galleries","title":"The Pyramid Galleries","category":"Quests","lead":"The pyramid contains two routes associated with a burial and a bird figure.","body":"The reference point for a visit is the [[ancient-egyptian-civilization|monumental complex]], at −3115, 84, 2348. Use the canal and temple to orient yourself in the isometric view. The community description distinguishes the gallery entrances and treats the temple as a separate location."},{"id":"the-sinking-of-broville","title":"The sinking of Broville","category":"Events","lead":"The old city’s disaster, the reactor that survived, and the beginning of reconstruction.","body":"The sinking, also called the V10 Catastrophe, is the disaster that led to evacuation and the deterioration of much of old [[broville|Broville]]’s infrastructure. It is a separate event from the [[the-great-fire-of-broville|Great Fire]]. Underground ruins and the rebuilding of downtown on the surface mark its place in the later city. Notices at −174, 5, 1840 and −174, 5, 1841 refer to a radioactive meltdown and warn against approaching [[h-i-v-e|H.I.V.E.]]. In his first [[the-doctors-reports|report]], the [[the-doctor|Doctor]] describes the city sinking, residents trying to rebuild, and the machine surviving; he accepts responsibility and judges that the complex concentrated too much power in a confined space. The [[latency-reactor|Latency Reactor]] was housed in H.I.V.E. and was removed from the epicenter after the disaster. The community narrative expands the collapse into a failure of support structures, with the city sinking to the bedrock layer and opening immense geological fissures, attributing the episode to corporate negligence; the notices and report establish the collapse and the machine’s survival but do not detail this mechanism or fix the year. The same narrative describes a widespread loss of societal control and associates the ruined area with biological and paranormal proliferation, alongside the physical destruction that forced downtown’s reconstruction. Community accounts describe numerous deaths and interpret skulls piled in the basements of [[brocorp|BroCORP]] Tower as an ossuary of victims. They also associate radiation and the ruined underground with mutations, creatures, and urban legends, including the [[the-legend-of-the-toilet-rat|Toilet Rat]]. This association offers an explanation for underground horrors without demonstrating the origin of each creature. The Doctor recounts moving research to the western forest, where [[solar|SOLAR]] took shape. According to him, BroCORP Tower was erected over the ruins. The reactor’s survival connects the old complex to the later laboratory and the resumption of research. H.I.V.E. stands at the center of the disaster accounts. [[lightsource|LightSource]] is also named among the organizations associated with the episode in community tradition, but that mention does not establish what its involvement would have been."},{"id":"the-study-of-the-void","title":"The Study of the Void","category":"Events","lead":"After learning about mountain monks, the Doctor seeks a way to dispose of excess Latency. The SOLAR team comes into contact with a plane between time and space.","body":"Excess [[latency|Latency]] required isolation and a destination. A colleague told the [[the-doctor|Doctor]] about a passage opened by mountain monks, and the team brought back material that had to be kept refrigerated. [[solar|SOLAR]] begins studying the [[void|Void]] in its facilities. The [[the-doctors-reports|Doctor's reports]] follow this research. The records of the [[the-mountain-cycles|cycles]] present another perspective: that of a community living near it and dealing with its effects on books and objects."},{"id":"the-volcanic-expedition","title":"The Volcanic Expedition","category":"Documents","lead":"The Professor and the Assistant find an artificial chamber beneath an island. Their records describe the descent and the attempt to escape.","body":"The Professor and the Assistant find an artificial chamber beneath an island during a volcanic expedition. Their records follow the descent and the attempt to escape."},{"id":"tim","title":"Tim","category":"Characters","lead":"A BVPS member who returns disturbed after exploring a corridor. His colleagues describe the change in their diaries.","body":"Tim is a member of the [[broville-paranormal-society|BVPS]]. After exploring a corridor, he returns disturbed. His colleagues describe the change in the [[the-asylum-expedition|expedition]] diaries, but the account does not reveal his ultimate fate."},{"id":"trina-brendel","title":"Trina Brendel","category":"Characters","lead":"A BVPS member. Her diary from the early hours of April 12 records Tim’s behavior and the wait for a review of the recordings.","body":"Trina Brendel is a member of [[broville-paranormal-society|BVPS]]. Her [[bvps-diaries|diary]] from the early hours of April 12 records [[tim|Tim's]] behavior and the wait for a review of the recordings, in the context of the [[the-asylum-expedition|team's visit]]."},{"id":"twin-pines-mall","title":"Twin Pines Mall","category":"Locations","lead":"A shopping center whose name recalls the mall in Back to the Future.","body":"Twin Pines Mall is a shopping center in [[uptown|Uptown]], presented in the community description as [[broville|Broville]]’s most visited mall. The [[broville-subway|metro]] blue line reaches the heart of the complex, with a dedicated station between Campus and [[canals-east|Canals East]]. The community history places construction in 1930–1931 under the name Lonly Pine Mall. When director Richard J. Pine had twins, he reportedly changed the name to Twin Pines Mall. The name also recalls the mall in Back to the Future."},{"id":"unicorn-land","title":"Unicorn Land","category":"Quests","lead":"A generator test in Sector C conceals an unusual detour. The Test Fire sign marks the starting point.","body":"Look for the Test Fire sign in Sector C of the [[solar-western-complex|SOLAR complex]], by the generator or Latency Reactor test area. An elevator sign bears the name Unicorn Land."},{"id":"university","title":"University","category":"Regions","lead":"A district between Concord Commons and Oldtown, with a campus, museum, and access to the transport network.","body":"The district occupies the area between Concord Commons and Oldtown. University Square provides a reference point for the academic area. Museum connects the purple and green lines. The Museum of Natural History holds funerary artifacts and the starting point for Chr0NOS. The New Archeology Division appears in community references to excavations."},{"id":"university-square","title":"University Square","category":"Locations","lead":"An upscale suburban area on the outskirts of Broville, developed around Broville University and marked by a bell tower.","body":"University Square takes its name from the nearby [[broville-university|Broville University, or Bro-U]] complex, whose presence brought development to a previously unused part of the city. Housing and commerce accompany the campus and public transport. The most prominent landmark is the Bell Tower, built by Metalus beside a four-way intersection. The surrounding area combines public transport, housing, and commercial premises. Other reference points are Johannes Gutembreg public school, [[university-station|University Station]], and Bro-U's lake, still unnamed in the neighborhood description. The school appears as Johannes Gutenberg Public School in the project list of [[foxcreek-properties|FoxCreek Properties]], which also locates its office in University Square."},{"id":"university-station","title":"University Station","category":"Transport","lead":"A metro station in University Square near the Broville University buildings. On the Green Line, it lies between Museum and Old Town.","body":"University Station stands in the [[university-square|University Square]] district, in Broville's university area. The station serves [[broville-university|Broville University]] and appears on the [[broville-subway|metro]] Green Line between Museum and Old Town. Campus, on the Blue Line, is a separate stop with its own name and position in the network. In late 2012, the Broville Department of Transportation commissioned a new underground station on the edge of an otherwise undeveloped area. The aim was to provide rapid transit for university students. The contract was awarded to [[foxcreek-properties|FoxCreek Properties]], which prepared the initial plan; construction began after approval. In early 2013, the University Square board of commissioners held its first meeting and established a building code for the entire area. The transportation department notified FoxCreek again: the underground structure still met local needs, but the above-ground portion no longer satisfied the square's new requirements. The exterior structure was remodeled. The construction account places its completion just one month after ground was first broken. The surface area includes casual seating and dining spaces, along with small kiosks and shops above the main level, integrating metro access into everyday use of the square."},{"id":"uptown","title":"Uptown","category":"Regions","lead":"An urban area between Oldtown and the canals, near the center. Its location helps orient visitors heading to Edward Page's bookshop.","body":"Oldtown and Downtown lie to the north, University to the west, and Canals District to the south. The hospital and Twin Pines Mall are associated with Uptown in the community collection."},{"id":"void","title":"Void","category":"Concepts","lead":"A plane between time and space studied by SOLAR. Books from the mountains describe its effects on materials and records.","body":"A colleague told the [[the-doctor|Doctor]] that monks at a temple in the [[southern-mountains|southern mountains]] had opened a passage during a spiritual quest. The place was described as a plane between time and space. In the fourth report, [[solar|SOLAR]] engineers briefly accessed it. In the fifth, they widened the portal and collected a small sample. According to the Doctor, the material emitted dark energy and, at subzero temperatures, worked as a good conductor for the portal. In a separate record, form 8A-1 describes sample X as an insulator for channeling [[latency|Latency]] radiation to disposal zones, provided it remains refrigerated. The two books describe different functions. The form identifies the material only by the code X. The sixth report lists three objectives: discover whether the Void was empty, observe its effects on living beings and test whether it could contain [[latency-reactor|reactor]] radiation. To observe participants, the team built modular chambers with physical and mental obstacles. When volunteers ran out and profits fell, the Doctor authorized illegally obtaining people for the tests. In On the Effects of the Void, the keeper of records explains that water barriers offer some protection from the beings he calls hosts. Proximity to the Void, however, corrodes objects and causes everyday records to disappear. Local preparations delay the deterioration for a time. A copy is at −292, 6, 2656. In Structural Conce, a devotee reports that the Void passed through the mausoleum twice and took bodies. Another manual describes a cage that briefly exposes its occupant to the Void for ritual purposes, linking the practice to the [[the-mountain-cycles|mountain cycles]]. The accounts allow comparison between residents' experiences and SOLAR's investigation. The connection between the underground community and the monks mentioned by the Doctor remains unresolved. Using the Void as a radiation dump appears as an experimental objective, without a recorded result in the series."},{"id":"weighted-science-brick","title":"Weighted Science Brick","category":"Collections","lead":"A SOLAR brick used to keep pressure plates activated. Its grand scientific name contrasts with the simplicity of its role in the test chambers.","body":"The science brick appears in and around [[solar|SOLAR]] facilities, especially test chambers. The commercial description uses the name Heavy Duty Industrial Science Brick, abbreviated HDISB; the designation Weighted Science Brick emphasizes its use as a weight. The product was unveiled at the sixth annual SOLAR-sponsored Science Expo. While its engineers called it a “marvel of technology”, the audience of Broville residents is described as confused and disappointed. Its advertising promised: “Don't be fooled by appearances; inside that brick is the future of tomorrow”. Its originally advertised purpose was to serve as a handheld residential-use solid-state reverse-modulation oscilloscope housing unit. Despite the pompous technical description, the HDISB sold few units. SOLAR overestimated demand and was left with a large surplus, turning the brick into an expendable resource. Science Bricks became the most common and basic testing elements in the SOLAR Testing Center. They are usually delivered by a dispenser and placed on pressure plates, keeping them activated so that tests can be completed. The predecessor, called Brick of Science, never passed the testing stage. Its description attributes its rejection to undetermined psychological effects and repeated reports of subjects interacting violently with technicians after receiving one. The name SOLAR Corp. Chemical Luminescent Safety Projectile identifies another product associated with the company, accompanied by an image of a firework rocket. The supplied material does not describe its operation or establish a technical relationship with the brick. The name describes a chemically luminescent safety projectile from SOLAR Corp."},{"id":"western-desert","title":"Western Desert","category":"Regions","lead":"A stretch of sand at the far western end of the continent containing the Egyptian-inspired settlement. The desert also appears in the records of Brian Garlick’s expedition.","body":"On the road map, sand occupies the western end of the continent, beyond the forest belt and the [[nuclear-plant|power plant]] area. The map shows the transition to vegetated land and the shape of the coast. In the isometric view, arms of water separate sandy headlands and small islands near the mainland, and a channel helps distinguish the settlement from the monumental area. The point named desert in the package list, −3115, 84, 2348, serves as a reference for the [[ancient-egyptian-civilization|Egyptian complex]]. In Final Notes, [[brian-garlick|Brian Garlick]] reports that the [[solar|SOLAR]] team studied artifacts from desert ruins and interpreted inscriptions as a portal design. After the tests, he began reading the inscriptions as a warning. The diary gives the desert a role in the history of SOLAR's research, but supplies no coordinates and does not identify the settlement's temple or pyramid as the location of those finds. The Egyptian complex inventory records named funerary pieces and an item called Sphinx at −3231, 53, 2461. That name identifies the item; the Atlas does not use it as evidence of a structure's dimensions."},{"id":"willam-brotori","title":"Willam Brotori","category":"Characters","lead":"A warrior who claims to have sealed away a great demon. He left his possessions to whoever completed the task.","body":"Willam Brotori claims to have sealed away a great demon and leaves his possessions to whoever completes the task. His account connects with the search for the [[the-cemeterys-three-keys|cemetery’s three keys]] and [[witherbane|Witherbane]]. Willam’s short message says that he has sealed a great devil into slumber but is dying from his wounds. He fears that the seal will only be temporary. He addresses a later hero who will have to confront that danger and leaves his belongings to that successor. The book is therefore both a warning about the seal and a bequest. Its address to a future reader should be distinguished from a report that the reader has already completed the task."},{"id":"wilson-harolds","title":"Wilson Harolds","category":"Characters","lead":"A BVPS investigator whose diary records the discovery of an operating room and the loss of radio contact.","body":"Wilson Harolds belongs to the [[broville-paranormal-society|BVPS]]. His diary records the discovery of an operating room and the loss of radio contact during the investigation. The account forms part of the [[bvps-diaries|BVPS Diaries]] connected with the [[the-asylum-expedition|asylum expedition]]."},{"id":"witherbane","title":"Witherbane","category":"Collections","lead":"A legendary bow kept in the crypts of Broville’s cemetery. Its description states that it was used to seal away the great demon; a book at the same location provides a clue to the prison.","body":"Witherbane is a bow kept in the [[cemetery-and-crypts|cemetery]] crypts at −686, 57, 1355. The object’s own description presents it as a fabled bow once used to seal away the great demon. This association forms part of the story told by the object."},{"id":"xxanubizxx","title":"xXAnubizXx","category":"Characters","lead":"The name inscribed on the staff and skull in the Egyptian funerary complex. Community tradition identifies xXAnubizXx as a ruler.","body":"The name xXAnubizXx appears on the staff and skull of the [[ancient-egyptian-civilization|Egyptian funerary complex]], associated with the [[the-pyramid-galleries|pyramid]]. The identification as a ruler comes from community tradition; the inscriptions on the objects are the concrete connection given for the name."}];
